{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '100,165p' PLAN.md && ls -la\"","aggregated_output":"Extend the existing engine pause menu, settings, rebindings, fullscreen behavior, shell, and menu focus. `E.Shell` offers TV sizing; `E.Menu` handles controller navigation and pointer-hover focus. Use the documented APIs before replacing them. Start with PlayStation glyphs and expose the engine's Xbox A/B/X/Y option. Generate prompts through the input label system so changing device or rebinding updates the same screens.\n\n| Action | Standard-mapped controller default | Keyboard and mouse default | Touch behavior |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Move between targets | Left stick or D-pad, directional snap | WASD/arrows for focus; mouse hover selects the actual target | Direct tap; invisible drag region for fine tool motion when useful |\n| Apply selected tool / confirm | Cross; hold when required | Left click or Space/Enter | Contextual pop action near the selected target; short assisted holds |\n| Back / drop safely | Circle | Escape in overlays; right click for drop/back in workspace | Tap the contextual back/drop affordance |\n| Cycle tools | L1/R1; hold a shoulder for a readable tool wheel if useful | Mouse wheel; 1–4 for the four physical tools | Contextual wheel or automatic legal-tool selection |\n| Precision / inspect | L2 for precision; Square for test/inspect | Shift for precision; E or middle click for inspect | Target snapping, enlarged hit regions, automatic legal-joint assistance |\n| Repeat work order | Triangle | R | Tap the current instruction |\n| Evidence / board view | R2, with Touchpad as an optional shortcut | Tab; clickable evidence display | Tap the compact evidence display |\n| Pause / menu | Options, respecting Hub's Create+Options chord | Escape | One persistent menu button |\n\nCheck bindings against the engine and actual gameplay, resolve any conflicts, and teach them in context. Left-clicking a target must operate on that target, not the previously focused one. Keep focus changes separate from destructive actions. Tool switching never discards a held part silently. Avoid requiring rapid cursor precision while the camera is moving.\n\nBuild a coherent screen flow: title, training/case select, short brief, play, pause, settings, developer tools, results, ending, About/Credits, and standalone host/join. Use the same menu components and navigation rules throughout. Settings include audio, glyph family, remapping, motion/flashes, readable text scale, and puzzle/tool assistance. Restore focus to a valid element when overlays close. Handle controller disconnect with an intelligible prompt and the correct pause policy.\n\nUse large text, generous targets, visible focus, safe screen margins, and concise instructions. Judge the 10-foot composition at 1080p. Retain the same game and screens for keyboard/mouse. On phones and tablets, use responsive scaling and a larger active workspace, a menu button, direct target selection, multitouch, invisible motion controls where needed, and contextual actions. Avoid a permanent wall of virtual controller buttons. Rotate/reflow the workspace sensibly; no physical-touch or real-iOS claim can come from browser emulation alone.\n\n8. Implement cooperative multiplayer as a complete shared game.\n\nSupport **one to four players**, with one shared bomb, code, evidence ledger, city state, and deadline. Each player claims a repair board and works its tool sequence on their own view. Everyone sees automatic board-completion and evidence updates. This supports useful concurrent work while retaining a collective disposal decision. Case definitions provide sufficient meaningful boards for all active seats. Scale work orders and the fuse openly for party size at start; joining or leaving does not secretly accelerate a running clock.\n\nUse one authoritative simulation in solo, Hub, and standalone co-op. Transport adapters carry the same semantic commands and state updates. A board claim is atomic and exclusive. Consequential commands include case ID, board ID, target ID, action ID, expected revision, and tool operation. Take sender identity from the transport, validate ownership and legal state, and make duplicate delivery idempotent. Remote cameras, focus, and cursor motion remain local presentation. Replicate accepted tool operations and resulting board state; physics synchronization is unnecessary.\n\nRepresent holds as validated begin/end actions with a host-bounded duration; a dropped connection cannot leave a soldering tool running forever. Release all holds when input focus or control is lost. Preserve an append-only set of truthful evidence cells. Emit cues from accepted event IDs so resync cannot replay every sound or celebration. Use labeled seeded random streams for case generation and cosmetic variation, with cosmetics unable to change puzzle state.\n\nThe host owns deadlines, heat, repairs, score, and terminal outcomes. Clients display time derived from the authority and request a snapshot before accepting actions after reconnect. Rendering and frame rate never determine fuse duration. Solo pause freezes the simulation. Online menus are local, visibly say the fuse continues, and do not pause authority. Configure the documented online `live:true` / `autoPause:false` behavior and nonpausing menu path as appropriate; native blur, hidden-tab, and controller-loss auto-pause must not stall the host independently of the menu. In online play, preserve elapsed time under background-tab throttling; follow Hub's explicit hidden-host termination rules. Define boundary ordering so accepted actions and expiry cannot produce both win and loss.\n\nFor shared code entry, a player proposes the code and intentionally confirms submission; this counts as their yes vote. Connected seated players can accept or veto during a five-second window. Any veto cancels the proposal. Unanimous acceptance submits immediately; otherwise the deadline submits only with a strict majority of eligible seats accepting and no veto. Without that majority, cancel and resume work. Remove departed seats from eligibility, exclude new seats until the next proposal, and cancel if the proposer leaves. A solo player uses deliberate confirmation without a vote. The host resolves the proposal once, and the fuse continues throughout. These are game actions, not a chat system.\n\nOn guest disconnect, cancel held actions and release their board according to the adapter's explicit connection state. A standalone reconnect within a brief grace period can reclaim its seat using a room-scoped token, after a complete state sync. Reject stale commands from the old connection. A Hub `leave` is final for that round and is not a reconnect opportunity. Guest departure must not strand a required component or lock code entry. Host departure ends the online round clearly; standalone players can return to menu and start a fresh case. Follow Hub's void/end rules inside Hub. Host migration is unnecessary for this slice.\n\nInside AI Hub, paste its **unmodified client as the first script in the head**, immediately after the charset declaration. Add the required session metadata for protocol 1, shared-world, one-to-four seats, goal, controls, and appropriate round duration. Connect through `AIHub.connect({mode:'shared-world', seats:[1,4]})`, attach lifecycle handlers, and call `ready()` once automatically when loaded. Use the Hub's standard card. Active sessions enter the round without title, training, attract mode, room entry, or other gates. Begin simulation only after `start` and when `AIHub.time()` reaches zero.\n\nOnly the host simulates, including when `AIHub.server` is true and the host has **no seated player**. Treat host authority and local player as separate concepts. Use `AIHub.rng(label)` for shared-world generation. Carry intents and state through `AIHub.send` and `message`; the Hub permits JSON packets up to 16 KB and 20 outgoing messages per second, with a burst allowance. Keep discrete operations reliable, coalesce updates, and send compact snapshots or board chunks with explicit revisions. Respect these protocol limits without turning the project into an optimization exercise.\n\nFinish the team round once through host `finish({standings, scores, won})`, tying the remaining cooperative seats. Handle `time-up` within its grace period. On `end`, stop gameplay input, sound, and outbound traffic. Respect Hub-supplied names as text, its storage isolation, fullscreen delegation, and Create+Options ownership. Use only Hub networking in an active Hub session. Keep custom chat, voice communication, emotes, custom names, browser dialogs, and navigation out of that session flow.\n\nOutside Hub, `connect()` returning null selects the ordinary game. For standalone online play, implement a **PeerJS 1.5.5 adapter** using secure cloud signaling and WebRTC data connections. Host creates a namespaced, randomly generated room ID; guests enter its short displayed code. Use controller-operable character selection alongside keyboard entry/paste. Validate protocol/content versions before admitting a seat, show connection and ready states, and support full/rejected/unavailable rooms, timeouts, retry, back, disconnect, and cleanup. Host and join must produce an actual peer connection and a playable co-op case.\n\nInstantiate PeerJS only in standalone online flow. Its official guide documents `new Peer`, `peer.connect`, the incoming `connection` event, and the connection `open`/`data`/`close` lifecycle; confirm options against the pinned release. Destroy owned peers on exit. PeerJS Cloud supplies signaling, but cross-network connectivity can require TURN. Ship actionable retry/back behavior and record the tested network boundary. Do not invent credentials, assume access to Hub's authenticated TURN service, or describe two tabs alone as proof of universal internet connectivity. A local self-hosted signaling fixture may aid deterministic tests; verify the shipped signaling path as well.\n\n9. Build content and developer tools that make the next cases ordinary authoring work.\n\nUse a modular source tree with clear ownership. Suggested layout:\n\n| Development path | Responsibility |\n| --- | --- |\n| `src/main.js`, `src/game/` | Startup, scene flow, save migration, campaign and session lifecycle |\n| `src/sim/` | Pure repair rules, clocks, heat, code/evidence logic, scoring, command validation, deterministic state |\n| `src/content/` | Case definitions, board templates, glyphs, authored clue masks, dialogue, default tuning |\n| `src/view/`, `src/art/` | Native engine scenes, original assets, tool/crew animations, camera and feedback |\n| `src/ui/`, `src/input/` | Shell screens, focus, prompts, input actions, touch assistance |\n| `src/audio/` | Procedural instruments, cue scheduler, voice synthesis, bus settings |\n| `src/net/` | Session abstraction, Hub adapter, standalone peer adapter, state codec |\n| `src/dev/` | Live tuning, board/case editor, validation, inspection, test controls |\n| `tests/`, `tools/` | Simulation tests, real-input playthrough driver, audio checks, Hub fixtures, bundling and verification |\n| `docs/`, `evidence/` | Research, controls, authoring notes, requirement coverage, observed issues and test evidence |\n\nKeep the simulation independent of the renderer, browser input, audio, and transport. Presentation consumes state and events; it never mutates the puzzle during drawing. Use the engine's fixed-step facilities and a clearly defined clock policy rather than creating competing update loops. Verify disposal and scene-transition behavior, including the engine's timer-clearing rules.\n\nA case definition should contain its ID, brief, theme, digit count, glyph/code source, evidence sectors, repair-board references, time/workload per party size, dialogue cues, introduced mechanics, and ending beats. A board template should contain component positions and terminal anchors, required job order, replacement rule, spare tray, hazard settings, and evidence reward. IDs remain stable across rendering, saves, networking, tests, and editor exports. Content variation is seeded and versioned.\n\nExpose developer mode through pause. Provide case/seed selection, restart/checkpoint, single-step and time scale for solo testing, fuse adjustment, component/state inspection, fault/fire injection, reveal/solve actions, input display, and network status. Clearly mark modified runs and isolate them from ordinary records. Hub sessions retain local inspection and presentation settings; gameplay-changing cheats, tuning, and editor controls are disabled in live session play. Standalone cooperative test tuning requires host authority and broadcasts its revision to all peers.\n\ntotal 72\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 micha micha  4096 Nov  6 04:43 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 4 micha micha  4096 Nov  6 04:43 ..\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha 49007 Nov  6 04:43 PLAN.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha 12492 Nov  6 04:43 TASK.md\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
