{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat vendor/hub-upstream/lib/hub/README.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat vendor/hub-upstream/lib/hub/README.md'","aggregated_output":"# AI Hub Session Protocol v1\n\nAn **AI Hub session** is up to four players, each on their own device,\nplaying rounds of games one after another. The hub runs everything around a\nround: the invite, the lobby, the seats and names, the network, the clock, the\nscoreboard and the results. This page defines how a game takes part in a\nround, and how the copies of a game send data to each other.\n\n**https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md**\n\nThe hub tests every game when it joins the library, with the same test\ndescribed in *Test it*, and keeps a game that fails it out of sessions.\n\n- Client: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js\n- Harness: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\n\nTo ask an agent for it, add this to the game's prompt:\n\n> This game must support AI Hub sessions. Follow\n> https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md\n> (AI Hub Session Protocol v1) and pass the harness's automatic check.\n\n---\n\n## Modes\n\nA game declares one mode.\n\n| Mode | What each copy runs | What the copies send each other |\n|---|---|---|\n| `parallel` | its own copy of the round | nothing |\n| `ghost` | its own copy of the round | each copy's own player: where it is and what it is doing |\n| `shared-world` | one world for all players, run by the host's copy | what each player does, to the host; the world, from the host |\n\n- **Parallel**: every player plays their own copy of the round, from the same\n  start. Each copy reports its score to the hub.\n- **Ghost**: as parallel, and each copy also shows the other players'\n  characters where they are, when they are in the same area of the game.\n  Those characters do not affect anything in the copy that shows them.\n- **Shared world**: all players play in one world. The host's copy runs that\n  world; the other copies send it what their own player does, and show the\n  world the host sends them.\n\nGames tagged with the older modes keep working: `race` is played as ghost,\n`co-op` and `versus` as shared world.\n\n---\n\n## A round\n\n1. The hub loads the game on every player's device, and the players get 10\n   to 30 seconds with the game's card, longer if one asks for more time: what\n   the game is, how to win, the controls. The card is the hub's, from the\n   game's tags, or the game's own (*The card*).\n2. The game connects and calls `ready()`.\n3. The round starts on the hub's clock, on every device at once.\n4. The game reports how it goes.\n5. The round ends when every player has a result, when the host of a shared\n   world finishes it, or when the hub calls time. The hub shows the results\n   over the game, then removes it.\n\n### Tags\n\nIn the game's `<head>`:\n\n```html\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"MIN-MAX\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"MODE\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"GOAL\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"CONTROLS\">\n```\n\n- `hub:session-players`: the smallest and largest number of players the game\n  takes in a session. The hub deals the game only when the number of players\n  is in that range.\n- `hub:session-mode`: `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world`.\n- `hub:session-goal` and `hub:session-howto`: how to win and the game's\n  controls, shown on the hub's card.\n\nOptional:\n\n- `hub:session-minutes`: how many minutes a round of the game takes. The hub\n  shows it and uses it to decide when to call time.\n- `hub:webgpu` with `required`: the game cannot run without WebGPU. The hub\n  then deals it only when every player's browser has WebGPU.\n\n### The client\n\n[session.js](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js)\nis pasted into the game unmodified, as the first script in `<head>`, right\nafter `<meta charset>`. In a session it:\n\n- seeds `Math.random`, in parallel and ghost, before any of the game's own\n  scripts run, so it gives the same numbers on every copy;\n- gives the game an empty `localStorage` and `sessionStorage` of its own, so\n  nothing stored on the device carries into the round, and nothing the round\n  stores reaches the device's own storage;\n- hands `requestFullscreen()` and `exitFullscreen()` to the hub, which takes\n  its whole page fullscreen; `document.fullscreenElement` and\n  `fullscreenchange` follow the hub's page.\n\nThe hub replaces an unmodified copy of the client with each new version. An\nedited copy is not replaced.\n\n### Joining\n\n- `AIHub.active` is `true` from the first line when the hub opened the page.\n- In a session, the game opens straight into the round: no title screen,\n  menus or prompts before it, other than its own card (*The card*).\n- `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats: [min, max] })` returns a promise of the\n  round's `info`, or of `null` when there is no hub after all; with `null`\n  the game runs as it does on its own.\n- `AIHub.ready()` is called once, when the game has loaded and the round can\n  start, with no key press: the players answer the card.\n- The round starts when the card's countdown ends or every player pressed\n  Ready, and every game has called `ready()`. A game still loading then gets\n  20 more seconds; after that its player sits the round out.\n\n`info` (also on `AIHub` after `connect()`):\n\n| Field | |\n|---|---|\n| `me` | this copy's player id |\n| `players` | `[{ id, name, color }]`, ids `0..N-1` in seat order |\n| `host`, `isHost` | the id of the copy that runs a shared world, and whether it is this one |\n| `server` | `true` only on a copy that hosts a shared world with no player of its own |\n| `mode`, `round`, `limitMs` | the mode, the round number, and the round's hard limit after the start |\n| `seed` | a number, the same on every copy, behind `AIHub.rng(label)` |\n\nNames come from the hub and are never inserted into the page as HTML. Colours\nfollow the seat: `#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`.\n\n### The card\n\nBefore every round the players get 10 to 30 seconds to learn what comes\nnext and say when they are ready. A player can ask for more time, which adds\nten seconds; while more than half of them are asking, the countdown holds.\nBy default the hub shows its own card over the game. A game can make the\ncard its own, as part of the game:\n\n```js\nAIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card: true });\n```\n\nThe hub's card then steps aside when the game calls `ready()`, and the game's\nscreen is the card until `start`. How it looks and sounds is the game's: its\ntitle, its music, its world, the players lining up. Introduce the game the way\nit deserves.\n\nWhile it is on, the card shows:\n\n- the game's name;\n- the players, each in their colour, and which of them are ready;\n- how to win the round;\n- the controls this player will use;\n- the time left, from the `card` event.\n\nAnd it takes two answers, from the keyboard, a controller, or a tap or click\non the card:\n\n- **Ready**: Enter, Cross (button 0), or a tap on Ready:\n  `AIHub.answer('ready')`.\n- **More time**: Triangle (button 3), a key the card names, or a tap on More\n  time: `AIHub.answer('more')`. Answering `more` again takes it back.\n\nNothing done on the card changes the round. It has no menus, settings or\nchoices, and play begins only at `start`. In parallel and ghost it draws no\nnumbers from `Math.random`, which would change the round's start: use\n`AIHub.rng('card')`.\n\n`card` arrives whenever it changes, and every second while the countdown runs:\n\n| Field | |\n|---|---|\n| `left` | milliseconds until the round starts by itself; `null` while paused or starting |\n| `paused` | `true` while most players asked for more time |\n| `starting` | `true` once the countdown is over or everyone is ready: `start` follows when every game has loaded |\n| `ready` | the ids of the players who are ready |\n| `asking` | how many players asked for more time (never who) |\n| `asked` | `true` when this player asked for more time |\n\n### The start\n\n`start` arrives when the round starts. Play begins three seconds later, when\n`AIHub.time()` reaches 0. `AIHub.time()` is the same timeline on every device,\ngive or take about 15 ms: negative during the countdown, `-Infinity` before\n`start`.\n\n### Reporting\n\n- `AIHub.progress({ score, label })`, in parallel and ghost: the player's\n  standing during the round. `score` is a number, higher is ahead; `label` is\n  text for the hub's scoreboard. The hub takes the latest about once a second.\n- `AIHub.result({ time })` or `AIHub.result({ score })`, in parallel and\n  ghost, once, when the player's round is over. `time` is milliseconds of\n  `AIHub.time()`, lower wins; `score` is a number, higher wins.\n- `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, won })`, in a shared world, called once,\n  by the host only:\n  - `standings`: arrays of player ids, best first; players in the same array\n    tie. It lists every player still in the round once; anyone left out is\n    placed last.\n  - `scores` (optional): `{ id: number }`, shown on the scoreboard.\n  - `won` (optional): `true` or `false` when the round is a team result.\n\n### Time-up and the end\n\n- The hub may call time while the round is still going. On `time-up`, the\n  game reports within `graceMs`: a `result()` in parallel and ghost, a\n  `finish()` from the host in a shared world. A shared world with no\n  `finish()` by then is void.\n- `end` is the last message of a round. After it, the game takes no input and\n  sends nothing, and its sound stops. The hub shows its results over the\n  game, then removes it.\n\n| `end` reason | |\n|---|---|\n| `finished` | the round counted; `standings` is the final order |\n| `void` | it didn't count (no result, a fatal error, skipped) |\n| `host-left` | the host of a shared world left |\n| `sat-out` | this player missed the ready deadline |\n| `session-ended` | the session closed |\n\n### Leaving\n\n- `leave` arrives with `{ id, reason }` when a player leaves: `left`,\n  `disconnected`, `not-ready` or `kicked`. It is final for the round.\n- A parallel or ghost round goes on whoever leaves, the host included.\n- A shared-world round is void when its host leaves, isn't ready, or has its\n  tab hidden for more than 20 seconds.\n- `AIHub.fail(message)`: the game cannot run in this browser. From the host\n  of a shared world it voids the round; from any other copy it counts as\n  that player leaving.\n\n### Scoring\n\n- Parallel and ghost: players with a `time` rank first, lowest first; then\n  the rest by `score`. A player with no result is ranked by their last\n  progress.\n- Shared world: the host's `standings`. With `won`, the results show a team\n  result instead of medals.\n- Each player gets one point for every player they beat.\n\n`result()` and `finish()` may also carry `stats` and `awards`, shown with the\nresults:\n\n- in `result()`: `stats` is `{ name: value }` and `awards` is\n  `[{ title, detail }]`, for this player;\n- in `finish()`: `stats` is `{ id: { name: value } }` and `awards` is\n  `[{ id, title, detail }]`, for each player.\n\nPlaces and points come only from the standings, `time` and `score`.\n\n---\n\n## Sending data between copies\n\n### Parallel\n\nThe copies send nothing to each other. `send()` is not used.\n\n### Ghost\n\nEach copy sends its own player's position and what the player is doing to\nthe other copies with `send()`. Each copy shows the other players' characters\nonly while they are in the same area of the game as its own player, and those\ncharacters do not affect its world. `Math.random` is seeded as in parallel.\n\n### Shared world\n\n- The host's copy (`AIHub.isHost`) runs the world. The other copies send the\n  host what their own player does, and show the world the host sends them.\n- The hub can run the host's copy with no player of its own: `AIHub.server`\n  is `true` and `AIHub.me` is not among `AIHub.players`. The host's copy runs\n  the world in that case too, with no local player, camera or input.\n- `Math.random` is not seeded. `AIHub.rng(label)` gives the same numbers on\n  every copy.\n\n### `send()` and `message`\n\n- `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` sends `data` to every other copy, or\n  to the one player `to`. It returns `false` when the message is dropped.\n- `AIHub.on('message', (data, from) => …)` receives it; `from` is the\n  sender's player id. A copy never receives its own messages. Messages sent\n  before another copy has loaded are queued for it.\n- `data` is JSON, at most 16 KB.\n- At most 20 messages a second leave each copy, with bursts up to 40; the\n  client drops the rest.\n- Reliable messages (the default) arrive in the order they were sent.\n  Messages sent with `reliable: false` may be lost or arrive out of order. An\n  unreliable message larger than about 1,200 bytes travels as several packets\n  and is lost whole if any of them is.\n\n---\n\n## Rules\n\nIn a session:\n\n- **Network only through the hub.** No WebSocket, WebRTC, or `fetch` to a\n  game server or public broker. Loading the game's own files is fine.\n- **No communication between players.** No chat, voice, emotes or custom\n  names. Names come from the hub.\n- **The page stays.** No `alert`, `confirm` or `prompt`; no `window.open`,\n  new tabs or navigating away. Quitting, restarting or returning to a title\n  screen does nothing; the hub starts and ends rounds.\n- **Create + Options is the hub's.** On a controller, holding the two\n  together opens the hub's corner bar; the game ignores Options while Create\n  is held.\n\nEverything on this page applies only in a session (`AIHub.active`). Outside\none, the game is not affected.\n\n---\n\n## Test it\n\nThe harness plays the hub: it loads the game 1–4 times on a simulated network\nand runs the checks the hub runs when a game joins the library. Serve it next\nto the game:\n\n```bash\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/bridge.js\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/round.js\npython3 -m http.server 8000\n```\n\nThen open `http://localhost:8000/harness.html?game=GAME.html&players=2&auto=1`.\nThe hub's own test also runs ghost and shared-world games with\n`&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`, again with\n`&scenario=host-leave`, and shared worlds once more with\n`&players=2&scenario=server`.\n\nThe checklist fills in as it runs. **A game passes when\n`window.__hubCheck.pass` is true**; `document.body.dataset.complete` becomes\n`\"true\"` at the end. It checks:\n\n- the tags, `connect()`, `ready()` with no key press, and the start;\n- with the game's own card, that Enter answers Ready;\n- parallel and ghost: `Math.random` the same on every copy, and progress from\n  every copy; parallel sends nothing, ghost sends;\n- shared world: traffic both ways, and a round hosted by a copy with no\n  player of its own;\n- a player leaving, the host leaving, time-up, a valid result, and every copy\n  told the round ended;\n- no protocol problems, errors, dialogs, popups or sockets.\n\n**New round**, **Time up**, **Remove player** and **Hide host tab** run the\ncases by hand; clicking a player's frame gives it the keyboard and mouse.\n`&limit=` sets the round's length in seconds and `&seed=` fixes the seed. The\ngame is served from the harness's own origin.\n\nIn the AI Hub repository, `node scripts/hub-check.mjs <game>` runs the same\nchecks with Playwright, then opens the game outside a session to see it still\nruns there.\n\n---\n\n## Reference\n\n### Tags\n\n| Tag | |\n|---|---|\n| `hub:session` | `1`: this protocol |\n| `hub:session-players` | `MIN-MAX`: the number of players the game takes in a session |\n| `hub:session-mode` | `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world` |\n| `hub:session-goal` | how to win, shown on the hub's card |\n| `hub:session-howto` | the controls, shown on the hub's card |\n| `hub:session-minutes` | optional: how many minutes a round takes |\n| `hub:webgpu` | optional: `required` when the game cannot run without WebGPU |\n\n### API\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| `AIHub.active` | `true` in a session, from the first line |\n| `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card? })` | → `Promise<info \\| null>`; `null`: no hub. `card: true`: the game shows its own card |\n| `AIHub.ready()` | once, when the round can start |\n| `AIHub.answer('ready' \\| 'more')` | on the game's own card, before `start` |\n| `AIHub.time()` | milliseconds since play began: negative in the countdown, `-Infinity` before `start` |\n| `AIHub.on(event, fn)` | returns a function that removes the handler |\n| `AIHub.progress({ score, label })` | parallel and ghost |\n| `AIHub.result({ time \\| score, stats?, awards? })` | parallel and ghost, once |\n| `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores?, won?, stats?, awards? })` | shared world, the host, once |\n| `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` | ghost and shared world |\n| `AIHub.rng(label)` | a generator of numbers in `[0, 1)`, the same on every copy, one stream per label |\n| `AIHub.seeded` | `true` when the client seeded `Math.random` |\n| `AIHub.fail(message)` | the game cannot run in this browser |\n\n`AIHub.probe()` and `AIHub.inspect()` exist for older games; the hub does not\nread them.\n\n| Event | Arguments |\n|---|---|\n| `card` | `{ left, paused, starting, ready, asking, asked }`, with the game's own card |\n| `start` | `{ in }`: milliseconds until play begins |\n| `message` | `data, from` |\n| `leave` | `{ id, reason }` |\n| `time-up` | `{ graceMs }` |\n| `end` | `{ reason, standings }` |\n\nEvents that arrive before a handler is attached are kept, and delivered in\norder when the first handler is attached. Fields and events a newer hub adds\nreach the game through the client unchanged.\n\n### Versions\n\nThis is protocol **1**, client **1.4.0**. Changes within version 1 only add:\ngames on older clients keep working. A change that would break a game becomes\nprotocol 2, with its own `hub:session` value.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat vendor/engine-upstream/dist/handoff/README.md && cat vendor/engine-upstream/dist/handoff/API-INDEX.md && ls vendor/engine-upstream/dist/handoff'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat vendor/engine-upstream/dist/handoff/README.md && cat vendor/engine-upstream/dist/handoff/API-INDEX.md && ls vendor/engine-upstream/dist/handoff'","aggregated_output":"# my-3D2dge handoff package (v0.89.1)\n\nEverything a coding agent needs to build, run, test and check a game on the full my-3D2dge engine, in one folder of text\nfiles. You don't need the engine's repository, and you don't need to read the engine's source below its header.\n\n**The engine is yours to change.** This copy is yours to fix, extend or rewrite in any way you see fit: `my-3d2dge.js`\nis the engine, readable, with every module, and every page and test here loads it (`modules/` and `my-3d2dge-agent.js`\nare separate copies). If you hand the game back, a line on what you changed and why helps: it shows what the engine\nlacks.\n\n## Start here\n\n1. **Read `API.md`**, the API card: a complete game to start from, what x, y and z mean in each view, the rules, the\n   common mistakes and the API by area. It is the one page to read whole.\n2. **Start from a game, not an empty file.** The kit for your genre (`kits/`): the card's Genre playbook names one for\n   each genre, and `kits/README.md` says which file is which. `kits/<id>.js` is a complete game in that genre, readable\n   (its title, play and end, the modules it rests on, sound, touch controls), `kits/<id>.html` plays it, and\n   `node kits/test.mjs <id>` runs it headless. Or the template, `game/`: a small complete game (climbing, a guard with\n   sight, an HTML HUD, scenes) that `game/index.html` plays and `game/test.mjs` tests without a browser. Copy one into\n   `game/` and make it yours.\n3. **Search `API-INDEX.md`** when you need a name: every public name in the engine, one line each, with its signature,\n   units and defaults, and the height rules at the top. `grep -n \"ledge\\|landing\" API-INDEX.md`. Don't read it whole.\n4. **`AI_GUIDE.md`** has the recipes: genre by genre, the look to aim for, the frame order, heights, recorded sessions.\n5. **Don't build a pause menu, settings, key rebinding, full screen or a loading screen**: every game has them (`API.md`,\n   \"Standard features\"). Adjust them with `new E.Game({ menu: { items, settings, controls, quit } })`; heavy setup goes in\n   `game.start({ boot: { steps } })`.\n6. **Objects without drawing code:** read `docs/ASSETS.md` whole before you draw a barrel, a table or a tree.\n   `r.shape('barrel', x, y, 0, { facing })` draws one of the core library's in every view; `node tools/asset-find.mjs\n   \"market stall\"` searches it and the store (`src/assets/sets/`: about a thousand objects, materials and sprites,\n   each with where it came from), and `node tools/asset-sheet.mjs mine.js#stool` draws one you wrote, to look at\n   before you use it.\n7. **A game in real 3D** (first person, a light gun, a racer on banked roads, flight, a dungeon crawled in 3D, fixed\n   cameras): the 2D views have no perspective camera, but the engine has a 3D add-on, `World3D` (three.js r182 and\n   Rapier 0.19.3 under the 2D engine, which keeps the HUD, menus and sound). It is not in this folder: take\n   `engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js` (its header is its manual), `vendor/` and the 3D kit closest to your game\n   (`src/starter3d/`) from the repository, github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge, and see them play at\n   my-3d2dge.vercel.app/3d.\n\n## What is here\n\n| File | What it is |\n| --- | --- |\n| `my-3d2dge.js` | the full engine, readable (one file, no dependencies): the core, then every module |\n| `modules/` | each engine module on its own, when there are any (one general system; its header is its manual), to load after the agent edition |\n| `my-3d2dge-agent.js` | the agent edition: the essential engine, whose header is a shorter manual (a game written for it runs on the full engine) |\n| `API.md`, `API-INDEX.md`, `AI_GUIDE.md` | the card, the index, the guide |\n| `kits/` | the 2D genre kits, readable: each kit's game (`<id>.js`) and the page that plays it (`<id>.html`), the table of them (`README.md`) and their headless test (`test.mjs`) |\n| `game/` | the template game: `index.html`, `game.js`, `test.mjs` |\n| `tools/check.mjs` | plays a game in headless Chromium, switches its views, and reports errors, what is on screen, and `game.debug.snapshot()` |\n| `tools/filmstrip.mjs` | records frames into one picture, to judge motion (`--seed 1` makes it repeatable, `--compare` marks what changed) |\n| `tools/replay.mjs` | replays a recorded play session exactly (`E.session`, a bug seen on a phone) |\n| `docs/ASSETS.md` | the asset card: still objects and materials as data (`r.shape`, `map.solid`, `E.MATERIALS`), the core library, writing one |\n| `tools/asset-find.mjs`, `asset-sheet.mjs`, `asset-set.mjs` | search the core library and the store; draw an object in every view and lint it; cut the few entries a game uses into a set of its own (`asset-lib.mjs` is theirs) |\n| `src/assets/` | the asset store: sets of objects and materials converted from CC0 libraries (Kenney, KayKit, ambientCG), with their catalogs. A game loads one with its script and `E.addAssets(window.ASSETS['kenney-furniture'])`; the Asset Lab shows them all at my-3d2dge.vercel.app/asset-lab |\n| `package.json` | pins Playwright for the tools |\n\nNot here: the 3D engine (three.js and Rapier) and its kits, the playbook's 3D rows. They are on the site\n(my-3d2dge.vercel.app/3d) and in the repository (github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge); here a 3D game is built on the 2D\nengine's views (`kits/README.md`, 3D).\n\n## Run, test, check\n\n```sh\nnpm install                          # Playwright, for the tools (once)\nnpx playwright install chromium      # its browser (once)\nnode game/test.mjs                   # the game's headless test: no browser, a second\nnode tools/check.mjs game/index.html # play it in a browser: errors, screenshots, its snapshot\nnode kits/test.mjs arcade            # a kit, headless: it plays, and its proof gives the same hash twice\nnode tools/check.mjs kits/arcade.html#arcade/maze   # a kit in a browser, at a part (its deep link)\nnode tools/filmstrip.mjs game/index.html --steps \"wait:800 press:Enter wait:500 rec:16:2 down:KeyD wait:600\" --seed 1\n```\n\nOpen `game/index.html` in a browser to play (or serve the folder: `npx serve`). To hand a single file to a person, inline\nthe two scripts into the page, writing every `</script` inside them as `<\\/script` (the agent edition's header has one,\nand the page would end there).\n\n## Testing a game without a browser\n\n`new E.Game()` is headless in Node (or with `{ headless: true }`): no screen, sound or DOM, and `draw` is skipped unless\nthe game is made with `draw: true` (the template is): then each `advance` draws a frame on a stand-in, and a draw error\nlands in `game.errors`, as it would on a player's screen.\n\n- `game.advance(seconds)` runs exact fixed steps of 1/120 s at once: `advance(1)` is 120 updates.\n- `game.input.tap('jump')` presses an action for one step, `hold('right')` holds one down, `hold('right', false)` lets go.\n- `game.debug = { snapshot() { return {...} }, teleport(x, y, z) {...} }` is your game's handle: tests read it, and\n  `tools/check.mjs` and `tools/replay.mjs` print it.\n- `game.stop()`, `game.start()` and `game.dispose()` stop, resume and end a game.\n- `game.go('play')` fades for `game.fadeTime` (.22 s) before the new scene enters: advance past it, or set\n  `game.fadeTime = 0` in a test.\n- A scene change clears `game.after` and `game.every` timers (`{ keep: true }` outlives it); those made before the first\n  scene live into it. It ends a hit-stop; `game.timeScale` stays (restore a slow motion with a kept timer). A boot step's `game.go(name)` enters that scene when the boot ends.\n- Draw changes nothing, and nothing your update or input relies on (a click target, what is on screen) may be made only\n  in `draw`: `E.Menu`'s rows come from its update (`menu.rowAt(i)` is entry i's box, for a test's click), and the camera\n  (`game.cam`) says where the screen is in update.\n- `E.store` keeps values for the visit in Node, which has no `localStorage` (`E.store.clear()` between games in one test).\n- A script's `tap` and `hold` are no device: `input.label()` keeps showing the keys or buttons the player used last.\n\nThe same inputs give the same game on every machine and at every refresh rate: the clock is an exact fixed step.\n# my-3D2dge API index (v0.89.1)\n\nEvery public name in `engine/my-3d2dge.js` and its modules (`engine/modules/`: a section each, after the core's), one line each: its signature (and default, for a field) and what its doc comment says (a long one's options on lines of their own: `new E.Game({ expand })`). Written by `node tools/api-index.mjs` from the engine itself; `doctrine-check` fails when a public name has no description here. Search it for a name; `API.md` and the agent edition's header are the overviews. † marks a name the agent edition (`my-3d2dge-agent.js`) doesn't have.\n\n## Units and the height rules\n\n| Quantity | Unit |\n| --- | --- |\n| Distances, positions, sizes in the world | world units: one floor tile is 16; x east, y south, z up |\n| Speeds, gravity | world units per second, per second squared |\n| Time, `dt` | seconds (`dt` is one fixed step: 1/120 s of real time times `game.timeScale`) |\n| Angles, `facing`, `dir` | radians: 0 east, π/2 south (a direction may also be a ground vector `[dx, dy]`) |\n| Screen positions | pixels of the game's own low-resolution buffer (`game.screen.W` × `H`) |\n| Colors | `'#rrggbb'` strings |\n\n- A body's `z` is its feet. A cell's top is `map.height(cx, cy)`: 0 on the floor; a wall is solid from 0 up to its `h`.\n- A body stands on the highest top within `z + 2` under its footprint (`E.HEIGHTS.step`, `Body.step`).\n- Eyes are at `z + 30` and a target is seen at `z + 22` (`E.HEIGHTS.eye`, `chest`; a body's own `eye` and `chest` override them).\n- Two things are on the same level when `|dz| <= 8` (`E.HEIGHTS.level`), unless a call says otherwise.\n- Collision, standing, sight (`map.sight`, `map.ray`), reach (`E.targets`, `E.inArc` with `dz`) and navigation (`E.FlowField`) all follow these rules. `E.dist` is 3D; `E.overlap` is flat.\n\n## Examples\n\nEach runs in `tools/contract-test.mjs`.\n\n```js headless\n// a game with no display, in Node or a test: fixed steps, scripted input, its state\nconst E = My3D2dge, game = new E.Game({ headless: true, res: 'snes' });\nconst hero = new E.Body({ x: 40, y: 40, r: 5 });\ngame.start({ update(dt) { hero.vx = game.input.move()[0] * 80; if (game.input.pressed('jump')) hero.jump(210); hero.update(dt); }, draw() {} });\ngame.input.hold('right'); game.input.tap('jump');\ngame.advance(0.25);   // 30 fixed steps of 1/120 s, at once\nif (!(hero.x > 55 && hero.z > 20)) throw new Error('the hero should run right and be in the air');\ngame.dispose();\n```\n\n```js heights\n// a guard on a roof, a hero in the street: sight, reach and a climb read heights the same way\nconst E = My3D2dge, map = new E.TileMap({ rows: ['#######', '#.....#', '#..RR.#', '#######'], legend: { '#': 1, R: 2 }, types: { 1: { h: 80 }, 2: { h: 40 } } });\nconst guard = { x: 56, y: 40, z: 40, facing: Math.PI }, hero = { x: 36, y: 40, z: 0, r: 5 };\nif (!map.sight(guard, hero)) throw new Error('eyes on a roof see down into the street');\nif (E.targets(guard, [hero], { range: 60 }).length) throw new Error('not on the same level: no melee target');\nconst up = map.ledge(hero, 0);   // east, toward the roof\nif (!up || up.z !== 40) throw new Error('a ledge to climb onto the roof');\n```\n\n## Contents\n\nThe line each section starts on, and the name its classes' instances go by: `E.Attack: slash` lists its members as `slash.start`.\n\n- 108 The engine object (E = My3D2dge)\n- 119 Math\n- 195 Pixel primitives\n- 214 Sprite frames, turns and swaps\n- 220 Views (projections); E.View: view\n- 247 Screen; game.screen\n- 269 Input; E.Input: input\n- 357 Pixel fonts\n- 368 Lighting; game.lights\n- 387 Particles; E.Particles: game.particles\n- 407 Renderer; r\n- 457 Game; E.Game: game\n- 572 Standard features; settings, fullscreen\n- 589 10c. Boot\n- 594 10d. Tweens and scripts; E.Script: script, E.Tween: tween\n- 611 10e. Paths; E.Path: path\n- 621 10g. Weather\n- 625 10k. Save slots; E.Saves: saves\n- 648 Humanoid rig; E.Attack: slash, E.Combo: combo, E.Humanoid: rig\n- 727 Blob rig; E.Blob: blob\n- 747 Tile map; E.TileMap: map\n- 811 Materials\n- 832 Flow field; E.FlowField: flow\n- 850 Texture helpers for floortex functions\n- 861 Platform map; E.PlatformMap: level\n- 895 Platformer; E.Platformer: hero\n- 936 Body; E.Body: body\n- 956 Actions; E.Action: action\n- 978 17c. Mover; E.Mover: mover\n- 1017 17d. Perception; E.Suspicion: suspicion\n- 1034 Bullets; E.Bullets: shots\n- 1054 Spatial hash; E.SpatialHash: grid\n- 1064 Audio; game.audio\n- 1098 Backdrops; E.Backdrop: backdrop\n- 1106 21c. Props\n- 1111 21e. Shapes\n- 1123 Ui; E.Dialog: talk, E.Menu: menu\n- 1184 21d. Shell; E.Shell: shell\n- 1213 Gpu lighting (webgpu); game.gpu\n- 1234 Sessions\n- 1243 Module arcade: the coin-op's frame: scores, high-score tables and initials, lives, credits, turns, cards, the attract mode (engine/modules/arcade.js); E.Arcade: arcade\n- 1278 Module click: point-and-click adventures: walk areas with holes, depth, hotspots, verbs, an inventory (engine/modules/click.js); E.Click: click, E.Walkbox: walkbox\n- 1323 Module drive: cars on the ground plane, a race round a track, the drivers who race it (engine/modules/drive.js); E.Car: car, E.Race: race\n- 1361 Module fight: one-on-one fighting: fighters with frame data, guards, throws, specials, a meter; rounds; a CPU; training (engine/modules/fight.js); E.FightAI: fightai, E.FightSelect: fightselect, E.FightStage: fightstage, E.Fighter: fighter, E.Match: match\n- 1442 Module gen: generated maps: rooms and corridors, BSP, caves, mazes and scatter, from a seed (engine/modules/gen.js)\n- 1453 Module grid: square and hex grids: paths, ranges, lines, fields of view, fog, groups, movers and turns (engine/modules/grid.js); E.Fog: fog, E.Grid: grid, E.GridMover: gridmover, E.Turns: turns\n- 1512 Module mode7: a perspective floor drawn a row at a time, a sky that turns with it, everything on it scaled by its distance (engine/modules/mode7.js); E.Mode7: mode7\n- 1535 Module physics: 2D rigid bodies: circles, boxes, polygons, segments; fast balls that never pass through (engine/modules/physics.js); E.Physics: physics\n- 1564 Module rhythm: charts on a song's steps, judged on the music clock: combos, grades, holds, a highway (engine/modules/rhythm.js); E.Rhythm: rhythm\n- 1596 Module road: the pseudo-3D road: segments with curves and hills, lanes, rumble strips, roadside scenery, tunnels (engine/modules/road.js); E.Road: road\n- 1613 Module rooms: a world of rooms joined at their edges, the map of what was explored, gates and saves (engine/modules/rooms.js); E.World: world\n- 1642 Module rpg: items, inventories, equipment, shops, a party's stats and levels, status, damage, encounters (engine/modules/rpg.js); E.RPG: rpg\n- 1661 Module songs: the songbook: the kits' best songs and more, and jingles, tagged by mood (engine/modules/songs.js)\n- 1669 Module story: dialogue as data, flags and values, a quest log and emote balloons (engine/modules/story.js); E.Story: story\n- 1698 Module strategy: real-time strategy and simulation: selection, orders, armies, production, resources, placement, fog, AI; a day clock, needs, overlays (engine/modules/strategy.js); E.Clock: clock, E.Strategy: strategy\n- 1744 Module terrain: destructible ground in the side plane: carved and added, walked on, drawn in chunks (engine/modules/terrain.js); E.Terrain: terrain\n\n## The engine object (E = My3D2dge)\n\n- `E.DEG: 0.017453292519943295`: π/180, one degree in radians (angle * DEG)\n- `E.TAU: 6.283185307179586`: 2π, a full turn in radians\n- `E.build: \"dev-build\"`: the commit this copy was built from, a placeholder in the repo that tools/stamp.mjs replaces when the site deploys (e.g. 'a1b2c3d 2026-10-07')\n- `E.current: null`: the latest game made (My3D2dge.current, for tools and tests)\n- `E.name: \"my-3D2dge\"`: the engine's name, 'my-3D2dge'\n- `E.version: \"0.89.1\"`: the release (package.json, written everywhere by node tools/version.mjs)\n- `E.versionLabel()`: 'v0.7.0 · a1b2c3d 2026-10-07': the label to show on screens and in reports\n- `E.warn(key, msg)`: one console warning per distinct problem, prefixed so tools (and people) can spot engine advice: warn(key, message)\n\n## 1. Math\n\n- `E.HEIGHTS: eye, chest, level, step`: the numbers of THE HEIGHT RULES, which collision, standing, sight, reach, navigation and drawing all read (API.md, \"Heights\", and the API index's head give the rules), in world units above a body's feet. The defaults fit a size-1.3 Humanoid; a body's own eye and chest override these (crouching lowers them)\n- `E.V3`: small 3D vector helpers on [x, y, z] arrays\n- `E.V3.add(a, b)`: a + b\n- `E.V3.dot(a, b)`: dot product\n- `E.V3.len(a)`: length\n- `E.V3.lerp(a, b, t)`: the point t of the way from a to b\n- `E.V3.mul(a, s)`: a scaled by s\n- `E.V3.norm(a)`: unit vector the same way\n- `E.V3.rz(v, a)`: v turned by angle a (radians) about the z axis\n- `E.V3.sub(a, b)`: a - b\n- `E.angDiff(a, b)`: the shortest signed turn from angle a to angle b, -π..π (radians)\n- `E.angleTo(a, b)`: ground angle from a to b (radians, 0 = east, PI/2 = south)\n- `E.approach(a, b, s)`: a moved toward b by at most s, never past it: approach(speed, top, accel * dt)\n- `E.approachAng(a, b, s)`: angle a turned toward b by at most s radians: facing = approachAng(facing, target, turnSpeed * dt)\n- `E.arc(a, b, g, o = {})` †: the throw from a to b ({ x, y, z }) under gravity g (units a second squared, pulling z down): a lob to a teammate's feet, a grenade, a leap to a ledge. o: { apex: how far it rises over the higher end (24), or time: the seconds it flies, or speed: a launch speed (the lower arc that reaches; null when it can't) } -> { vx, vy, vz, t }\n- `E.bayer(x, y)` †: the 4 x 4 ordered-dither threshold 0..1 at pixel (x, y): pixel on when value > bayer(x, y)\n- `E.chance(p)`: true with probability p (0..1)\n- `E.clamp(v, a, b)`: v kept between a and b\n- `E.ctx2d(c)`: a canvas's 2D context with smoothing off (crisp pixels)\n- `E.dist(a, b)`: distance between two objects with x, y (and optional z)\n- `E.ease`: easing curves for u 0..1: linear, outQuad, inQuad, inOut (inOutQuad), outCubic, outBack (it overshoots a little more than Penner's: 1.9), and the rest of Penner's set (sine, quad, cubic, back, elastic and bounce, each in, out and inOut: inSine, outSine, inOutSine, ...), the names every tween library uses (section 1b)\n- `E.ease.inBack(u)` †: pulls back before it goes\n- `E.ease.inBounce(u)` †: bounces, then leaps away\n- `E.ease.inCubic(u)` †: slower start than inQuad\n- `E.ease.inElastic(u)` †: wobbles, then springs away\n- `E.ease.inOut(u)`: slow start and end\n- `E.ease.inOutBack(u)` †: pulls back, then overshoots\n- `E.ease.inOutBounce(u)` †: bounces at both ends\n- `E.ease.inOutCubic(u)` †: slow start and end, quicker between\n- `E.ease.inOutElastic(u)` †: wobbles at both ends\n- `E.ease.inOutQuad()` †: slow start and end: inOut's other name\n- `E.ease.inOutSine(u)` †: slow start and end (the gentlest)\n- `E.ease.inQuad(u)`: slow start, fast end\n- `E.ease.inSine(u)` †: slow start (the gentlest)\n- `E.ease.linear(u)` †: even speed\n- `E.ease.outBack(u)`: overshoots past 1, then settles: pop-ups, landings\n- `E.ease.outBounce(u)` †: drops and bounces to rest\n- `E.ease.outCubic(u)`: fast start, smooth stop\n- `E.ease.outElastic(u)` †: springs past and wobbles to rest\n- `E.ease.outQuad(u)`: fast start, smooth stop (gentler than outCubic)\n- `E.ease.outSine(u)` †: smooth stop (the gentlest)\n- `E.hash2(x, y)`: a fixed random number 0..1 for whole-number grid coordinates (texture specks, scatter that never flickers)\n- `E.hex(h)`: '#rrggbb' or '#rgb', rgb(), rgba(), hsl(), hsla(), a basic CSS name ('orange', 'navy') or an [r, g, b] array -> [r, g, b]\n- `E.hsl(hh, s, l)`: '#rrggbb' from hue (degrees), saturation and lightness (0..1)\n- `E.ik3(a, b, L1, L2, hint)`: two-bone IK in 3D: returns [joint, end]. hint points the way the joint should bend.\n- `E.inArc(a, facing, b, range, halfAngle, dz)`: melee cone on the ground: is b (with optional radius r) within range of a and within halfAngle of facing? dz (optional) also limits the height difference, so a hero on a roof can't hit a guard in the street (left out, height is ignored)\n- `E.lerp(a, b, t)`: a + (b - a) * t: the point t of the way from a to b (t 0..1)\n- `E.lerpAng(a, b, t)`: an angle t of the way from a to b by the shorter way round\n- `E.mix(h1, h2, t)`: the color t of the way from h1 to h2 ('#rrggbb')\n- `E.mkCanvas(w, h)`: a new canvas of w x h pixels (scratch images and sprite sheets). With no document (Node: a headless game) a stand-in whose drawing does nothing, so sprites and other images made while a game loads don't stop it\n- `E.noise2(x, y)`: smooth value noise 0..1 over the plane (one bump per whole unit): blotches, clouds, wobble\n- `E.overlap(a, b)`: circles on the ground plane: { x, y, r }\n- `E.overlapBox(a, b)`: side-view boxes: { x (center), z (feet), w, h } (the Platformer / PlatformMap convention)\n- `E.pick(list)`: a random item of list (Math.random)\n- `E.prune(list, fn)`: remove items in place: prune(bullets, b => b.dead)\n- `E.ramp(c, n = 5, strength = 1)`: a ramp of n colors from dark to light around a base color (hue shifted), for palettes and gradients\n- `E.rand(a = 1, b)`: rand() 0..1, rand(b) 0..b, rand(a, b) a..b\n- `E.randInt(a, b)`: whole number from a to b, both included\n- `E.rng(s)`: a seeded random stream: const R = E.rng(42); R() gives 0..1, the same numbers for the same seed (mulberry32)\n- `E.sameLevel(a, b, tol = HEIGHTS.level)`: within tol (default E.HEIGHTS.level, 8) of the same height: sameLevel(hero, guard)\n- `E.shade(h, a)`: shade('#336699', -0.3) darker, (+0.3) lighter. Hue-shifted like painted pixel art: darker drifts cool, lighter drifts warm\n- `E.smoothDamp(cur, target, vel, st, dt)`: critically damped follow: [next, nextVelocity] = smoothDamp(cur, target, vel, smoothTime, dt); settles in about smoothTime seconds without overshoot\n- `E.store`: saved data that survives reloads (localStorage, JSON). Never throws. Where there is no storage (Node, a sandboxed frame such as the AI Hub's) or it is blocked or full, a value is kept for the visit instead\n- `E.store.clear()`: forget every key (all of this engine's on the page's origin): tests that make several games in one process\n- `E.store.get(key, fallback = null)`: the value saved under key (any JSON), or fallback\n- `E.store.remove(key)`: forget key\n- `E.store.set(key, value)`: save value (any JSON) under key; false when it lasts for this visit only (no storage, blocked or full)\n- `E.targets(from, list, o = {})`: the things in list that `from` can reach, nearest first, each as { target, d (ground distance), dz, angle (from -> it) }: melee auto-aim, lock-on, takedowns, interaction prompts. o: { range, arc (half angle around facing, radians; default all round), facing (default from.facing), dz (most height difference, default E.HEIGHTS.level), sight: map (only those its eyes see), filter: t => bool }. A target's r adds to the range\n- `E.toHex(c)`: [r, g, b] -> '#rrggbb'\n- `E.toHsl(h)`: a color's hue (degrees), saturation and lightness (0..1): toHsl('#e05a3a') -> [h, s, l]\n- `E.tones(c, strength = 1)`: five shades of a color for painted-looking pixel art: { deep, sh, base, lt, hi }. Shadows drift toward blue-violet, highlights toward warm yellow (strength 0..2, default 1).\n- `E.wrap(v, lo, hi)` †: v wrapped into lo..hi (hi left out): wrap(-1, 0, 10) is 9, wrap(330, 0, 320) is 10. A world whose edges meet (Asteroids, a looping stage): x = E.wrap(x, 0, W)\n- `E.wrapDiff(a, b, size)` †: the shortest signed difference from a to b on a span of size that wraps round: wrapDiff(9, 1, 10) is 2 (across the seam, not -8). Aim and chase across the edge\n\n## 2. Pixel primitives\n\n- `E.px`: the pixel primitives: px.rect, px.line, px.dot, px.disc, px.poly, px.sprite, ... (each snaps to whole pixels)\n- `px.blend(g, a, mode, fn)`: draw fn() at alpha a (stepped, like PS1 hardware) with a blend mode: 'normal', 'add', 'multiply', 'screen' (E.style.trans = 'dither' dithers instead)\n- `px.col(g, c)`: set the fill color (px.* do it themselves)\n- `px.ddisc(g, cx, cy, r, c, a, wx = 0, wy = 0)`: soft disc that fades toward its edge (glows, smoke, dust): stepped alpha rings, or dithering in 'dither' style\n- `px.disc(g, cx, cy, r, c)`: a filled circle\n- `px.dot(g, x, y, c)`: one pixel\n- `px.ell(g, cx, cy, rx, ry, c)`: a filled ellipse\n- `px.glow(g, e = 1)`: GPU lighting: what follows glows (0..1). No effect in Canvas mode, so call it freely.\n- `px.line(g, x0, y0, x1, y1, c, w = 1)`: a line w pixels thick, no smoothing\n- `px.poly(g, pts, c)`: a filled polygon through [[x, y], ...]\n- `px.polyDither(g, pts, c, a, wx = 0, wy = 0)`: translucent polygon: a = 0..1 opacity (stepped alpha, or a dither pattern in 'dither' style). wx/wy = world pixel offset for the pattern\n- `px.rect(g, x, y, w, h, c)`: a filled rectangle, snapped to whole pixels\n- `px.reset(g)`: forget the color cache (after drawing on g with the canvas API directly)\n- `px.sprite(g, spr, x, y, flip = false)`: draw a sprite with its top-left corner at buffer pixel (x, y). flip mirrors it left-right; or o: { flip, rot (radians, turned about its center: E.rotSprite), steps, scale (nearest-neighbour) }\n- `E.sprite(rows, colors = {}, scale = 1)`: Pixel sprite written as strings. Each character is one pixel; colors maps a character to a hex color; '.' and ' ' are transparent. scale (whole number) enlarges each pixel. const COIN = E.sprite(['.yy.', 'yYYy', 'yYYy', '.yy.'], { y: '#f0b020', Y: '#fff0a0' }); Draw it with r.sprite(x, y, z, COIN) (depth sorted) or px.sprite(g, COIN, sx, sy).\n- `E.style: trans`: Translucency style. 'alpha' (default, PS1 / N64 / SNES color math): real transparency in 1/8 steps, plus blend modes. 'dither' (NES, Game Boy, Genesis look): ordered-dither patterns. Set E.style.trans = 'dither' for the older look.\n\n## 2b. Sprite frames, turns and swaps\n\n- `E.anim(frames, fps = 8, o = {})` †: frames to play: anim([spr1, spr2, spr3], fps = 8, { loop: true, pingpong: false }), or anim(sheet, 8, { frames: 4 }) for several frames side by side in one sprite's rows. Draw it with r.sprite(x, y, z, a, { t }) (t: its clock, seconds; the game's time by default); a.frame(t) is the sprite at time t, a.index(t) its number, a.duration one play (seconds)\n- `E.recolor(spr, map)` †: a copy of a sprite with its colors swapped (palette swaps: the second player, a hurt flash, a stronger enemy): recolor(spr, { '#3a8a4a': '#8a3a4a', h: '#e0b040' }), each key a color or one of the sprite's own characters\n- `E.rotSprite(spr, a, steps = 16)` †: the sprite turned by angle a (radians; the way the world's angles turn, so a ship drawn facing east turns to its facing): nearest-neighbour, whole pixels, about its center, in `steps` steps a turn (16; 32 for a smoother one) and cached, as games of the era pre-rotated theirs. r.sprite's and px.sprite's rot option use it\n\n## 3. Views (projections)\n\n- `E.VIEWS: iso, threequarter, topdown, brawler, side, overhead`: the ready-made views: iso (yaw 45, pitch 30), threequarter (pitch 55), topdown (80), brawler (25), side (0) and overhead (90, scale 1: world x/y = screen pixels); new E.Game({ view: 'iso' }) and game.setView('side') take their names\n- `E.VIEW_ORDER: iso, threequarter, topdown, brawler, side`: the order game.nextView() cycles through when a game doesn't set game.views ('overhead' is left out)\n- `new E.View(id, label, yaw, pitch, scale = 1, zBoost = 1) → view`: new E.View(id, label, yaw, pitch, scale, zBoost): an orthographic camera (degrees; scale = screen pixels per world unit; zBoost stretches heights)\n- `view.depth(x, y, z = 0)`: true camera depth: use it to sort parts inside one object\n- `view.order(x, y, z = 0)`: painter's order for whole objects standing on the ground\n- `view.p(x, y, z = 0)`: world -> projected screen pixels (not yet camera-relative)\n- `view.screenDirToGround(dx, dy)`: a direction the player pushed on screen (x right, y down) -> normalized ground direction\n- `view.set(yaw, pitch, scale = 1, zBoost = 1)`: change the angles and scale in place (game.setView and rotateView use their own copies); returns the view\n- `view.toGround(sx, sy)`: projected screen point -> ground point (z = 0). null in side view\n- `view.ax`, `view.ay`: screen x = ax * x + ay * y (the projection's first row)\n- `view.bx`, `view.by`, `view.bz`: screen y = bx * x + by * y + bz * z (the second row; bz < 0 lifts things up the screen)\n- `view.dx`, `view.dy`, `view.dz`: the direction toward the camera, for true depth (view.depth)\n- `view.fx`, `view.fy`: the ground direction that points at the camera (the front of a wall faces it)\n- `view.id`: its name ('iso', or yours)\n- `view.inv`: the inverse of the ground projection (screen -> ground at z 0), null in side views\n- `view.isSide`: a side view (pitch 1 or less: x right, z up, y is depth)\n- `view.isTop`: looking straight down (pitch 89 or more)\n- `view.label`: its name for people ('Isometric')\n- `view.pitch`, `view.yaw`: the same angles in radians\n- `view.pitchDeg`: the tilt, 0 side view to 90 straight down\n- `view.scale`: screen pixels per world unit\n- `view.yawDeg`: the turn about the up axis, degrees (0: north is up the screen; iso is 45)\n- `view.zBoost`: how much heights are stretched on screen (1 = true)\n- `E.charView(view)`: the view a rig in `view` is drawn from (the sprite-like angle above; the view itself outside pitch 40-89)\n\n## 4. Screen\n\n- `E.RES: nes, snes, gb, gbc, gba, genesis, md, pce, n64, ps1, wide`: classic console resolutions for new Game({ res: 'snes' }) (width x height in pixels)\n- `game.screen.clear` †: true when the game's bg is null (or 'transparent'): the buffer and the canvas are cleared instead of filled, so a picture under the canvas shows through the game's (a 3D world drawn by engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js)\n- `game.screen.clientToScreen(cx, cy)`: browser client coords -> projected screen pixels (same space as view.p)\n- `game.screen.resize()`: fit the buffer to the canvas again (on window resize; after changing sizing options, setOptions does it)\n- `game.screen.setOptions(o)`: change sizing at runtime, e.g. { minH: 400, maxW: 1080, maxH: 660 } to pull the camera back\n- `game.screen.setScale(v)`: how a fixed resolution fills the screen: 'sharp' | 'fill' | 'auto' (see scale)\n- `game.screen.H = 200`, `game.screen.W = 320`: the buffer's size in game pixels\n- `game.screen.OX = 0`, `game.screen.OY = 0`: the letterbox margins on the canvas, in canvas pixels\n- `game.screen.S = 3`: the whole-number scale up to the canvas\n- `game.screen.buf`: the low-resolution buffer the game draws into (W + 2 by H + 2 pixels; null when headless, a stand-in when a headless game draws: o.draw)\n- `game.screen.canvas`: the page's canvas (null in a headless game); o: the sizing options (the game's o)\n- `game.screen.ctx`: its 2D context (r.ctx is the same)\n- `game.screen.dpr = 1`: the device pixel ratio used (at most 3)\n- `game.screen.filter = 'clean'` †: 'clean' | 'scanlines' | 'crt', drawn over the picture as it is presented, never on the buffer, and not under a test harness (navigator.webdriver). game.settings.filter sets it\n- `game.screen.fx = 0`, `game.screen.fy = 0`: its sub-pixel part (0..1, applied when presenting)\n- `game.screen.ix = 0`, `game.screen.iy = 0`: the camera's whole-pixel offset this frame\n- `game.screen.listeners = []` †: functions called after every resize\n- `game.screen.portrait`: true while the screen is taller than wide (a phone held upright)\n- `game.screen.scale = 'auto'`: how a fixed resolution fills the screen: 'sharp' (whole-number scale, every pixel the same size, black bars unless o.expand grows the picture into them), 'fill' (the largest fit, pixels a device pixel apart in size) or 'auto' (fill on high-density screens, phones and tablets, where that difference can't be seen; sharp elsewhere). game.settings.scale sets it\n\n## 5. Input\n\n- `new E.Input(screen, map, signal) → input`: signal: an AbortSignal that removes every listener this adds (game.dispose()). A screen without a canvas (headless) gets no DOM listeners: input comes from press(code), release(code), tap(action) and hold(action, on)\n- `E.Input.DEFAULT { attack, dash, skill, jump }`: top-down / isometric action (Zelda, Diablo): Space dashes, J or click attacks\n- `E.Input.PLATFORMER { jump, attack, dash, skill }`: side-scrollers (Mario, Mega Man): Space / Z / K jump, X / J attack or shoot, Shift / C dash\n- `E.Input.SHMUP { fire, bomb }`: shoot-'em-ups (1942, Gradius): Space / Z / J fire (hold for autofire), X / K bomb\n- `E.Input.THIRDPERSON { sprint, jump, crouch, interact, attack, block, dodge, use, map }` †: third-person action and stealth (Assassin's Creed, Metal Gear): WASD / stick move, Shift sprint, Space jump and parkour, C crouch (a toggle), E interact, J attack, K block, L dodge, Q use, M map. Pad: A jump, B crouch, X attack, Y interact, LB block, RB dodge, LT use, RT or L3 sprint, Back map\n- `E.Input.halfKeys(preset, side)` †: a preset for half a keyboard ('left' or 'right'): its moves and menu keys, the preset's other actions on its buttons in order, and the preset's gamepad codes (Input.halfKeys(Input.SHMUP, 'right') -> fire on . and Numpad 1, bomb on /). A preset's own halves come first: preset.halves = { left: { pass: ['KeyR'] }, right: { pass: ['KeyL'] } } (an action a menu reads beside confirm would take confirm's F otherwise)\n- `input.anyPressed()`: any action pressed this step (title screens: \"press any button\")\n- `input.bindButtons(root)` †: wire every element with data-act=\"<action>\" inside root to the action\n- `input.bindings` †: the player's bindings over the preset: { action: { keys: [codes], pad: [codes] } } (game.settings.bindings keeps them)\n- `input.box()` †: the pointer's gestures, read each step in screen pixels (the HUD's space), from a mouse or one finger: box() -> { x0, y0, x1, y1, from, to, done } while a box is drawn (a mouse's left button dragged; on a touch screen a long press, then a drag), done the step it is let go; else null\n- `input.buffered(a, win = .15)`: was it pressed within the last `win` seconds and not yet consumed? (input buffering)\n- `input.clear()`: Release all devices and discard buffered input on focus loss or a modal transition.\n- `input.clicked()` †: where a click or tap on the canvas landed, in screen pixels [x, y] (the HUD's space: r.W, r.H), during the step after it lifted (a press that drags away, like the touch stick's, is none); else null. Menus and dialogs answer to it; a HUD button of your own can: if (hit(input.clicked())) ...\n- `input.codeLabel(code)`: a code as a person reads it: 'W' (or the keyboard's own letter there), 'Space', '↑', 'Left click', and a gamepad button by its symbol on the controller in use ('A', '✕', 'B'...; glyphs picks the family)\n- `input.consume(a)`: mark a buffered press of action a as used, so buffered(a) is false until the next press\n- `input.consumeAll()`: forget every pending press (after closing a menu or dialog, so the same press does not also attack)\n- `input.doubleTap()` †: [x, y] the step a second tap or click lands within .35 s and 8 pixels of the first (a double tap), else null\n- `input.down(a)`: is the action held right now?\n- `input.drag()` †: how far the touch stick's finger moved since the last step, in screen pixels [dx, dy] (the HUD's space), or null while no finger holds it: to steer one to one (a shooter's ship follows the thumb) instead of by the stick's direction\n- `input.feed(type, x, y, o = {})` †: a pointer event in screen pixels, as the browser's would give: feed('down' | 'move' | 'up', x, y, { touch: false, button: 0, id: 0 (which finger, for zones) }) (a test's or a bot's mouse and fingers: clicks, boxes, long presses, double taps and pans follow; a finger in one of the zones holds its act), or feed('zoom', x, y, { k: 1.1 }): a wheel's notch or a pinch about that point\n- `input.history` †: the last second of input, oldest first: [{ t (input.clock), dir (numpad 1-9 for a fighter facing right: 5 neutral), pressed: the actions pressed then, directions aside }], one entry each time the direction changes or a button is pressed: an input display draws it; sequence() and motion() read it (section 10h: a kit without it has none)\n- `input.hold(a, on = true)`: hold an action down (on) or let it go (false), as its key would\n- `input.label(a, device = this.lastDevice)`: the key or button to show in a prompt for action a, on the device the player used last (or the one named: 'keyboard' | 'mouse' | 'gamepad' | 'touch'), with their bindings: input.label('jump') -> 'Space', 'A' or '✕'\n- `input.longPress()` †: [x, y] the step a press held still has been held .45 s (a long press), else null\n- `input.motion(digits, button, o = {})` †: a fighter's move in numpad notation, mirrored by facing: input.motion('236', 'attack', { facing: hero.facing, window }) is a quarter circle forward and punch (down, down-forward, forward, attack: a fireball); '623' a dragon punch, '41236' a half circle. facing: 1 (right, the default) or -1. button: an action, 'attack+kick', or none (the motion alone)\n- `input.mouseScreen()`: mouse position in projected screen pixels, or null\n- `input.move()`: movement intent in screen directions: x right, y down, length <= 1\n- `input.padStyle()`: the controller family whose symbols label() shows: glyphs, or with glyphs 'auto' the one its name says\n- `input.pan()` †: [dx, dy] how far a drag moved this step: one finger on open ground (not a box), or a mouse's middle or right button; else null. A free camera pans by it (game.cam.free)\n- `input.pointer()` †: the mouse pointer in screen pixels [x, y] (the HUD's space), or null before it has moved over the canvas\n- `input.press(code)`: press a key or button code ('KeyJ', 'Mouse0', 'Pad0', 'Act:attack') as if the person did: scripted input, tests, bots\n- `input.pressed(a)`: true only during the first update step after the press (menus, jumps, one-shot actions)\n- `input.rebind(a, device, code)` †: bind action a to one code for a device ('keys' = keyboard and mouse, 'pad' = gamepad), in place of that device's codes: input.rebind('attack', 'keys', 'KeyF'). Returns null when done, the action that already uses the code (nothing changes: free it first), or 'reserved' for the codes the menus keep (Escape, Enter, F keys, Start, the d-pad). The arrow keys and the d-pad always move as well\n- `input.rebindable(a, map = this.preset)` †: can the player rebind action a? Every action but the menu's (start, pause, confirm, cancel: they are how a player gets back to the menu), and those the game hides with new E.Game({ menu: { controls: { a: false } } })\n- `input.release(code)`: let go of a code pressed with press()\n- `input.released(a)`: true only during the first update step after the action was let go\n- `input.repeat(a, delay = .3, rate = .08)`: pressed, then repeats while held (after `delay` s, every `rate` s): menu cursors, puzzle pieces\n- `input.resetBindings()` †: forget the player's bindings: every action back to its preset's codes\n- `input.rumble(strength = .5, seconds = .15)` †: shake the gamepad: strength 0..1 for seconds (scaled by rumbleScale; nothing where the controller can't). Returns true when it rumbled\n- `input.screenPoint(cx, cy)` †: browser client coords -> screen pixels [x, y] (the HUD's space, with no camera), or null without a canvas\n- `input.sequence(steps, o = {})` †: true on the step a sequence of inputs ends, each one after the last within window seconds (.5) of the first: input.sequence(['down', 'down+right', 'right', 'attack']). A step names actions joined by +: its directions are held exactly as it says (up, down, left, right; 'neutral' for none) as they change to that, its buttons pressed. A cheat code: input.sequence(['up', 'up', 'down', 'down', 'left', 'right', 'left', 'right', 'kick', 'attack'], { window: 3 })\n- `input.stopRumble()` †: stop a rumble now (pause, focus lost, unplugged)\n- `input.suppress(a)` †: Suppress a held action until the next tick (a HUD widget owns the pointer this step).\n- `input.suppressCode(code)` †: A widget owns this button; other devices bound to the same action still work.\n- `input.tap(a)`: press an action for one update step, as a quick tap of its key (scripted input, tests, an AI): pressed() and down() are true in the next step, released() in the one after\n- `input.tick(dt)`: advance input by one step: read the gamepad and turn presses since the last step into this step's pressed() and released() (the engine calls it every step)\n- `input.touchArea()` †: where the touch buttons cover the screen, in screen pixels { x0, y0, x1, y1 } (the same space as r.W and r.H), or null while none show: keep a prompt or a box clear of it (a bottom E.Dialog does by itself)\n- `input.touchButtons(actions, o = {})` †: On-screen buttons for phones and tablets (shown on touch screens; the player's settings can show them always or hide them, put them on the left, and size them): input.touchButtons(['jump', 'attack'], { labels: { jump: 'A', attack: 'B' }, force }). The other half of the screen is a move stick (past halfway it presses up, down, left and right too, as a d-pad does: menus and ladders). START is the corner's menu button, top right: it opens the menu, or pauses a pausable scene into it (without the corner a START button there starts a title and pauses, as a gamepad's Start)\n- `input.touchSet(a, label = null, show = true)` †: one touch button as the moment changes: its label (TALK beside someone, NEXT in a dialog; null: its own) and whether it shows (false: nothing for it to do here). Cheap to call every step: input.touchSet('confirm', near ? 'TALK' : null, near || talking)\n- `input.unbind(a, device)` †: take away action a's codes for one device ('keys' or 'pad'); the arrow keys and d-pad stay on the moves\n- `input.use(map)`: switch key layout at runtime: input.use(E.Input.PLATFORMER) or input.use('SHMUP'). The player's own bindings (bindings, set by rebind() and the Controls screen) are laid over it. Every preset (E.Input.DEFAULT, PLATFORMER, THIRDPERSON, SHMUP) has the moves (up, down, left, right) and the menu's actions (start, pause, confirm, cancel). The keys held stay held (down() in the new layout, not pressed() again), and a press no step has seen counts in it\n- `input.value(a)` †: how far action a is pressed, 0 to 1: a gamepad button by its travel (the triggers, even short of a press), a stick's direction (PadAxisUp ..., or up / down / left / right on the touch stick) by its push, any other code held 1\n- `input.zoom()` †: the camera zoom the player asked for since the last step, as a factor (1.1: 10% closer, .9: farther; 1: none): the wheel, - and = and a pinch, where the scene lets them zoom. The game applies it (game.zoomBy)\n- `input.zoomPoint()` †: [x, y] where the last zoom was asked for: the mouse pointer for the wheel, between the fingers for a pinch; null when the keys zoomed (about the screen's middle)\n- `input.aimSource = 'move'` †: what aims right now: 'move' | 'mouse' | 'pad'\n- `input.clock = 0`: seconds of input time (the steps' dt added up)\n- `input.deadzone = .18`: how far a gamepad stick moves before it counts (0..0.8)\n- `input.glyphs = 'auto'`: the gamepad's button symbols for label(): 'auto' (from the controller's name) | 'xbox' | 'playstation' | 'nintendo' | 'generic' (positions)\n- `input.held`: the key and button codes down right now ('KeyD', 'Mouse0', 'Pad0', 'Act:jump')\n- `input.invertMoveX`, `input.invertMoveY = false` †: the move stick turned round (the Controller settings)\n- `input.keysTo = null` †: the other players' inputs the keyboard's codes go to as well (players sharing it, each with half its keys: game.players)\n- `input.lastDevice = 'keyboard'`: 'keyboard' | 'mouse' | 'gamepad' | 'touch', the last one used (show the right button prompts)\n- `input.map`: each action's codes ({ jump: ['Space', 'Pad0', 'Act:jump'] }); every action also answers to 'Act:<name>' (DOM buttons with data-act, tap, hold)\n- `input.mouse`: { cx, cy } its page position, active once it has moved over the canvas, t the input clock when it last moved\n- `input.names = null` †: the actions' names on the Controls screen ({ attack: 'Sword' }; false hides one from rebinding: the game's menu.controls); onBindings(b): called with the player's new bindings (the game saves and applies them)\n- `input.pad = 'any'` †: the gamepad this input reads: 'any' (the first connected one another player hasn't: player 1's), an index (a joined player's own), or null (none)\n- `input.padAim = null` †: its right stick, or null when centered\n- `input.padAxes = []` †: the gamepad's raw axes\n- `input.padId = ''`, `input.padIndex = null`: the gamepad in use (null and '' without one)\n- `input.padLost = false`: true for the step after the gamepad in use was unplugged (a pausable scene pauses)\n- `input.padMove = [0, 0]`: the gamepad's left stick after the dead zone\n- `input.padPrev = []`: which gamepad buttons were down at the last step\n- `input.padValues = []` †: each gamepad button's travel, 0 to 1 (the triggers; input.value reads them)\n- `input.preset` †: the layout last given to use() (E.Input.DEFAULT, PLATFORMER, SHMUP or your own)\n- `input.rumbleScale = .6` †: vibration strength 0..1 (0 = off; game.settings.rumble sets it)\n- `input.screen`: the game's screen (mouse positions are read through it)\n- `input.steer = 'stick'` †: 'stick' (a stick drawn under the thumb) or 'drag' (a finger anywhere on the screen but the buttons steers by drag(), with no stick drawn: a shooter's ship that follows the thumb)\n- `input.stick = [0, 0]` †: the touch stick's direction [x, y], length <= 1\n- `input.stickDeadzone = .12` †: the part of that radius a thumb moves before the stick counts (0..0.5)\n- `input.stickId` †: the pointer (finger) holding the touch stick\n- `input.stickOn = false` †: a finger is on it\n- `input.stickRadius = 44` †: how far (CSS pixels) a finger drags for full tilt\n- `input.stickSide = 'left'` †: 'left' or 'right', the half of the screen the stick lives on\n- `input.touchEnabled = true` †: false turns the touch stick off\n- `input.zones = null` †: areas of the screen a finger holds an action down in, [{ x0, y0, x1, y1, act }] (fractions of the canvas, 0..1; the first that holds the finger wins), or null: a pinball's two halves, a rhythm game's lanes. A finger landing in one presses act until it lifts (several fingers, several zones), and starts no stick\n\n## 6. Pixel fonts\n\n- `E.font`: Pixel fonts. 'normal' (default) is a 5x7 proportional font with lower case; 'tiny' is the 3x5 upper-case font. text(g, str, x, y, color, opts) where opts is an outline color, false, or { font: 'normal' | 'tiny', outline: '#0b0814' | false, shadow: '#000' (1 px drop shadow), gradient: ['#fff', '#8ad'] (top to bottom), scale: 1, align: 'left' | 'center' | 'right', wrap: maxWidthPx, lineGap } '\\n' starts a new line. Returns { w, h } of the drawn block. title(...) draws big logo text.\n- `E.font.default` †: the font used when none is named: 'normal' (5 x 7) or 'tiny' (3 x 5)\n- `E.font.height(lines = 1, o, lineGap)`: the height in pixels of that many lines (with o's scale and font, and lineGap between them)\n- `E.font.lineHeight(o)`: pixels from one line to the next\n- `E.font.text(g, s, x, y, color = '#ffffff', o = '#0b0814')`: draw text at (x, y) (its top-left; align: 'center' or 'right' moves it) in color. o: an outline color, or { outline, shadow, gradient, scale, align, wrap, font }; returns { w, h }\n- `E.font.title(g, s, x, y, o = {})`: Big logo text for title screens: scaled glyphs with a vertical color gradient, an outline and an extruded 3D edge. title(g, 'CASTLE OF NIGHT', x, y, { scale: 3, colors: ['#fff6c8', '#ffd36a', '#e07a2a'], outline: '#1a0c10', depth: 3, depthColor: '#6a2a1a', align: 'center' })\n- `E.font.width(s, o)`: width in pixels of the widest line. opts: a scale number or { font, scale }\n- `E.font.wrap(s, maxW, o)`: split text into lines no wider than maxW pixels\n\n## 7. Lighting\n\n- `game.lights.add(x, y, z, radius, intensity = 1, o)` †: radius in world units; intensity ~0.5..1.5. opts: { color: '#ffaa55' (both lightings), shadow: true (GPU lighting: walls and casters block it) }\n- `game.lights.caster(x, y, r, top)` †: something that blocks light (GPU shadows): a cylinder of radius r from the floor up to height top\n- `game.lights.heat(x, y, z, width, strength = 1)` †: heat shimmer above a point (GPU only). width in world units, strength ~0.5..1.5\n- `game.lights.on` †: true when the canvas lighting draws this frame: enabled, or standing in for GPU lighting the game turned on that can't draw (no WebGPU, still loading, a side view); gpu.fallback = false keeps such a frame unlit\n- `game.lights.refresh()` †: rebuild the darkness bands after changing dark, levels or glow (lights.dark = [r, g, b]; lights.refresh())\n- `game.lights.render(r)` †: darken the frame by the lights (the renderer calls it after the queue, when enabled)\n- `game.lights.ambient = .15` †: how lit unlit places are, 0 (black) .. 1\n- `game.lights.dark = [10, 6, 24]` †: the color of darkness, [r, g, b]\n- `game.lights.darkness = .9` †: how dark the darkest places get, 0..1 (the smooth style, E.style.trans 'alpha')\n- `game.lights.enabled = false` †: false (the default) turns the canvas lighting off\n- `game.lights.glow = [255, 200, 140, 0]` †: the color, [r, g, b, a], where light is brightest\n- `game.lights.glowAt = 1.7` †: the light level (1 = fully lit) above which it glows\n- `game.lights.levels = [.9, .7, .48, .26, .08, 0]` †: the darkness of each lighting band, darkest first (dithered between them)\n- `game.lights.list = []` †: this frame's lights from add()\n- `game.lights.screen = false` †: true measures lights on the screen, not the ground (side views and PlatformMaps do it by themselves)\n- `game.lights.tint = .32` †: how strongly lights add their own color, 0..1\n\n## 8. Particles\n\n- `new E.Particles(game) → game.particles` †: game.particles; game: its game\n- `game.particles.add(p)`: add one particle { x, y, z, vx, vy, vz, life, max (seconds), g (gravity, > 0 pulls down), drag, bounce (0..1), kind: 'dust' | 'spark' | 'bit' | 'ember' | 'ring' | 'text', color, size }; returns it, or null when full\n- `game.particles.bits(x, y, z, n, colors)`: n pieces of debris that fly, fall and bounce (colors: a list to pick from)\n- `game.particles.draw(r)`: queue them for drawing (the renderer calls it)\n- `game.particles.dust(x, y, z, n, o = {})`: n puffs of dust from (x, y, z) that drift and fade. o: { speed: 30, color, size }\n- `game.particles.explosion(x, y, z, size = 1, o = {})`: a full explosion in one call: fireball, smoke, sparks, debris, shockwave ring, screen shake and flash, sound. explosion(x, y, z, size = 1, { debris: ['#6a5a4a'], sound: true, flash: true })\n- `game.particles.fire(x, y, z, n = 6, o = {})`: fireball puffs for explosions and fire breath\n- `game.particles.glints(x, y, z, n = 5, color = '#fff6c0', spread = 10)`: twinkling star glints for magic, treasure and pickups\n- `game.particles.impact(x, y, z, size = 6, color = '#ffe070')`: a hit star at the point of impact: impact(x, y, z, size = 6, color)\n- `game.particles.ring(x, y, r0, r1, color, dur = .35, z = 0)`: a ring on the ground growing from radius r0 to r1 over dur seconds (shockwaves, landings)\n- `game.particles.smoke(x, y, z, n = 4, o = {})`: rising smoke puffs: smoke(x, y, z, n, { size, color, dark, light })\n- `game.particles.sparks(x, y, z, n, dir = null, o = {})`: n bright sparks thrown from (x, y, z), every way or around angle dir (radians). o: { color, speed }\n- `game.particles.text(x, y, z, text, color = '#fff2c4', o = {})`: floating number or word: text(x, y, z, '12', color, { scale: 2, bounce: true }) (bounce: lands and holds, Final Fantasy style)\n- `game.particles.update(dt)`: move and age them by dt (the engine calls it every step)\n- `game.particles.ground = null` †: null (the floor is z 0), or (x, y, z) -> the floor's height under a particle (a side-scroller's platforms, a 3D level: engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js sets it), asked again when a particle enters a new 8-unit cell\n- `game.particles.list = []`: the live particles\n- `game.particles.max = 900`: at most this many at once (add() skips the rest)\n\n## 9. Renderer\n\n- `r.H`: the screen's height in game pixels\n- `r.W`: the screen's width in game pixels\n- `r.actor(x, y, z, drawFn, o = {})`: Queue a character. drawFn(g, ox, oy) must draw with (ox, oy) as the projected position of world point (x, y, z). The engine adds outline, rim light, hit flash, afterimages and x-ray silhouettes. opts: {outline=true, rim=true, flash=false, alpha=1, xray=false, ghost:{color, life, alpha, owner}, hides, bias, every, key, emissive (it stays bright under lighting, as r.queue's)}\n- `r.actor({ xray })`: true shows the actor's hidden part (a translucent tint with a 1-px outline, r.xrayColor and r.xrayOutline; a checker in E.style.trans 'dither') wherever a wall, a prop or shape at least chest high, or anything queued with hides is drawn in front of it (not one rising less than E.HEIGHTS.level, 8, above its feet: the roof it stands on, a cut-down wall); xray: { color, outline (false: none), actors } picks the colors, and with actors: true other bodies drawn in front hide it too (a hero lost in a crowd keeps his shape)\n- `r.actor({ hides })`: true: this actor counts as an x-ray occluder even when no x-ray actor asked for actors\n- `r.actor({ ghost })`: ghost.owner (any {x, y, z}, usually the actor itself) keeps its afterimages sorted behind the live body\n- `r.actor({ every, key })`: every: n, key (any object, the character's own): its finished image is drawn again for n - 1 frames before drawFn runs again, at the place it is now (a background character animating at a lower rate, as classic games' townsfolk do: a phone's frame keeps the time for the ones that matter); not while it flashes, with a ghost, x-ray or GPU lighting\n- `r.actor({ wrap })`: [w, h], as r.sprite's: a world that wraps round shows the actor across each edge it crosses\n- `r.actorNow(x, y, z, drawFn, o = {})`: draw an actor at once, over what is drawn so far, not sorted by depth (a pass of the game's own: a body falling into a void painted over the level), with r.actor's options (outline, flash, alpha, ...)\n- `r.bh`: the buffer's height (H + 2)\n- `r.box(g, x0, y0, z0, x1, y1, z1, top, side)`: extruded box prop (crate, pedestal, table). Call inside a queue fn with its g.\n- `r.bw`: the buffer's width (W + 2: a pixel of margin each side for the sub-pixel camera)\n- `r.ctx`: the low-resolution buffer's 2D context: draw on it with E.px\n- `r.decal(fn, o)`: draw on the floor before the queue. opts.emissive (0..1) makes it glow under GPU lighting\n- `r.glowDisc(g, cx, cy, rad, color, a)`: glow halo from a cache: additive light rings (alpha style) or a dithered disc (dither style). a = 0..1 strength\n- `r.groundArc(x, y, r0, r1, a0, a1, color, alpha = 1, z = 0, g = this.tgt)`: a band of a ring on the ground from radius r0 to r1 and angle a0 to a1 (radians): melee telegraphs, sight cones\n- `r.groundDisc(x, y, rad, color, alpha = 1, z = 0)`: a dithered disc lying on the ground (radius in world units, z its height): shadows, auras, area attacks\n- `r.groundPts(x, y, rad, n = 18, z = 0)`: n screen points round a circle of radius rad on the ground at (x, y), height z: the outline of a ring or a fan\n- `r.groundRing(x, y, rad, color, alpha = 1, z = 0)`: a ring lying on the ground (radius in world units)\n- `r.marker(x, y, z = 0, o = {})`: an objective marker over a world point: a bobbing diamond (and its label) while it is on screen, an arrow at the screen's edge pointing to it while it is off (offscreen: false hides it then). o: { label, color: '#ffd040', offscreen: 'arrow' }\n- `r.overlay(fn)`: draw fn(g) on top of everything, in screen pixels (HUD, dialog, menus)\n- `r.pick(sx, sy)` †: what was drawn under a screen point (the HUD's pixels: input.clicked(), input.pointer()) in the last frame, front to back: the pick values of the actors, sprites, props and queued draws given one (r.actor(x, y, z, fn, { pick: unit })), each where its pixels went (an actor's drawn bounds; others' box, or a body's size round its point). [] for none\n- `r.ppu` †: pixels per world unit on the ground here: the view's scale times the zoom (iso 1.41, the others 1.5, overhead 1, at zoom 1). Rigs, maps and props grow with it; a sprite's pixel is a screen pixel at zoom 1 unless drawn with world: true\n- `r.prop(name, x, y, z, o = {})` †: set dressing from E.prop: r.prop('torch', x, y, z, { size, color, flip, light: true, halo: true, bias, hides, footprint }) feet on the world point; flames animate and glow. light: true also adds a warm light to game.lights. A prop at least chest high (E.HEIGHTS.chest, 22: a pillar, a statue, a lamp) drawn in front of an x-ray actor shows the actor's silhouette through it; a lower one (grass, a crate, a barrel) doesn't; hides: true or false says so.\n- `r.queue(x, y, z, fn, o)`: queue a depth-sorted draw. fn(g) draws into g. opts: {bias, occluder, hides, xray, emissive, solid (hides a glow behind it), footprint, box, top} occluder: a wall (x-ray silhouettes show through it, it sorts as a block). hides: only lets x-ray silhouettes show through it (props, sprites, bodies), nothing else changes. box: [x0, y0, x1, y1], what it covers in buffer pixels: an occluder joins the x-ray only where its box meets a silhouette (without one, a 48 x 140 box round its foot: give a long wall its box, or queue it with queueSpan); top: its top's height (one under the knees hides nothing). It sorts by its one point (x, y, z): a long thing that other things stand along sorts wrong somewhere: queueSpan\n- `r.queueSpan(a, b, z, h, fn, o = {})` †: queue a long piece of geometry (a wall, a fence, a roof's edge, drawn whole by fn(g)) a cell at a time from a to b ([x, y] world units) at height z, h tall: each cell-long piece sorts at its own middle, clipped to its part, with its box (as the engine's own walls are). o: queue's options, depth (its thickness across, 0), step (16 units)\n- `r.shadow(x, y, rad, alpha = .5, color = '#0c0818', z = 0)`: soft contact shadow under a character: a cached dithered ellipse, correct for the current view. z = height of the surface it falls on (0 = floor; the top of a block or platform otherwise)\n- `r.shape(id, x, y, z = 0, o = {})` †: Queue a shape standing at (x, y, z), feet on the point, depth sorted like an actor: r.shape(id | entry, x, y, z, o). o: { facing (radians, where its front points; default PI/2, south, as written), scale, mats: { slot: material }, flip (mirror it x to -x before facing), params (a generator's: { h: 30 }), bias, hides (x-ray silhouettes show through it: by default when it stands at least E.HEIGHTS.chest, 22, high), footprint, light (add its lights to game.lights), shadow (default true), charView (default true: steep views draw it from the characters' lower angle), flash, alpha, xray, pick (r.pick finds it) }\n- `r.sightCone(obs, o = {})` †: an observer's view on the ground: a dithered fan fov wide (radians) about its facing, out to range, cut short where the map hides a body's chest (map.ray, the height rules), draped over the ground below. o: { range: 120, fov: 1.6, facing, map, eye, color: '#ffe080', alpha: .3, rays }. Call it in draw (it is a decal: under walls and bodies)\n- `r.sky(colors, o = {})`: background of dithered color bands, top to bottom: r.sky(['#0b1030', '#3b2d6b', '#e0806a']). Call first in draw.\n- `r.sprite(x, y, z, spr, o = {})`: Queue a pixel sprite (from E.sprite) standing at world point (x, y, z). Depth sorted like an actor. opts: { flip, anchor: 'bottom' (feet on the point, default; E.shapeSprite's: its own feet) | 'center' | 'top', glow: 0..1, bias, flash, outline, alpha, xray, ghost (the last five use the actor path), t (an E.anim's clock: spr may be one), rot (radians, about its center: anchor 'center' unless given), steps (16), scale, world: true (it grows with the view's scale, as rigs and maps: r.ppu pixels a unit), wrap: [w, h] (a world that wraps round: a copy across each edge it crosses; w alone wraps x) }\n- `r.starfield(o = {})`: scrolling star dots for space and night skies. opts: { count: 70, speed: 30 px/s, dir: 'down' | 'up' | 'left' | 'right', colors, layers: 3, seed }\n- `r.text(str, x, y, color = '#ffffff', o)`: HUD text in screen pixels, drawn on top and never lit: r.text('SCORE 120', 4, 4, '#fff', { align, scale, outline })\n- `r.textAt(x, y, z, str, color = '#ffffff', o = {})`: text floating at a world point (names, prompts): r.textAt(x, y, z, 'OPEN', '#ffe08a')\n- `r.tgt` †: current draw target for ground helpers (switches during GPU info and overlay passes)\n- `r.view`: the view this frame is drawn in (the game's, with zoom and turn)\n- `r.visible(x, y, z = 0, mx = 60, up = 30, down = 90)`: is a world point near the visible area? margins in pixels (characters extend upward from their feet)\n- `r.w(x, y, z = 0)`: world -> buffer pixel coords\n- `r.game`: its game; items: this frame's queue (sorted by depth when the frame ends)\n- `r.glowMask = true` †: false lets emissive glows show over everything under canvas lighting (cheaper: the default keeps walls and bodies in front of a glow dark)\n- `r.gpu = false` †: true during a frame drawn with GPU lighting (draw code can check r.gpu)\n- `r.ix`, `r.iy`: the camera's whole-pixel offset this frame (a world point is at r.w(x, y, z) on the buffer)\n- `r.outline = '#140c1c'`: the color of the 1-pixel outline round every actor\n- `r.rim = '#ffe2b8'` †: the rim light's color on actors' lit edges\n- `r.rimAlpha = .28` †: its strength 0..1\n- `r.xrayColor = '#8fe3ff'` †: the default color of x-ray silhouettes\n- `r.xrayOutline = '#e8fdff'` †: the default 1-px outline round a silhouette's hidden part (null for none)\n\n## 10. Game\n\n- `new E.Game(o = {}) → game`: new E.Game({ canvas, view, views, res, expand, input, bg, step: 1 / 120, loop: true, headless: false, menu, fullscreen: 'tap', corner: true, backGesture: true, autoPause: true, live: false, id, pace, camera }). The standard features (section 10b) every game gets: menu, fullscreen, corner, backGesture, autoPause.\n- `new E.Game({ res })`: a fixed resolution ('ps1' or [w, h]), whole pixels\n- `new E.Game({ expand })`: true makes res the least the screen shows and grows the picture to fill the window, any shape, no bars (expand: { maxW, maxH } stops it growing there)\n- `new E.Game({ bg })`: the color behind everything, or null: the canvas is clear, and the game draws its HUD and menus over a picture under it (a 3D world, engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js)\n- `new E.Game({ headless })`: no screen, renderer, sound or DOM input (tests, tools, a simulation in Node, where it is the default): scenes update and draw is skipped; drive it with advance() and input.press / release / tap / hold\n- `new E.Game({ draw })`: true (headless): draw runs each advance on a stand-in renderer, its errors in game.errors\n- `new E.Game({ loop })`: false makes a game in a page that runs only by advance()\n- `new E.Game({ menu })`: the pause, settings and controls screens ({ items, settings, controls, quit } adjusts them, false leaves them out)\n- `new E.Game({ fullscreen })`: 'tap' (a phone goes full screen at its first tap and when a game starts, unless the player turned it off), 'button' (only when asked) or false\n- `new E.Game({ corner, backGesture })`: corner: the menu and full-screen buttons (top right); backGesture: a phone's back steps back in the game first (false: it leaves the page)\n- `new E.Game({ autoPause })`: a pausable scene pauses when the page is hidden or loses focus\n- `new E.Game({ live })`: true: a world that never stops for one player (online, a shared screen): a pause opens the menus over it, the scene's update goes on (game.paused says a menu is up) and the menu holds that player's input back\n- `new E.Game({ id })`: the name the player's settings are saved under (the page's path)\n- `new E.Game({ pace })`: false draws every refresh of a fast display even when the frames can't keep up (by default it draws every other one, evenly), unless the player picks Frame rate 60\n- `new E.Game({ camera })`: the camera of every scene without its own (a scene's camera option: the player's zoom, start)\n- `game.H`: the screen's height in game pixels (W is its width)\n- `game.W`: internal buffer size in pixels (HUD layout)\n- `game.addPlayer(o = {})` †: a player in now: { pad: index } (a gamepad) or { keys: 'right' } (the keyboard's right half; player 1 takes the left). Returns its input (null when four play already)\n- `game.advance(seconds = this.o.step, o = {})`: run the game for `seconds` of real time in exact fixed steps, at once, without requestAnimationFrame: tests, tools and headless runs (advance(1) is 120 updates). o.draw: then draw one frame (not in a headless game). A headless game made with draw: true draws one frame each advance, on a stand-in renderer, so a draw error lands in game.errors (a test that catches what a player would see break). Returns the updates run\n- `game.after(seconds, fn, o = {})`: run fn once after `seconds` of game time. Cleared on scene change, unless { keep: true } (a site's clock, a watcher of the whole game: it outlives scenes, as a kept tween does). Returns { cancel() }\n- `game.allowJoin(o = {})` †: let players join, up to max (2..4): Start on a gamepad no one holds, or 2 on the keyboard (o.keys: false not); onJoin (input, number) as each does. allowJoin(false) closes it (the names split and join are String's and Array's)\n- `game.back()`: a step back, as a phone's back gesture asks for (the engine calls it for one; a back button of the game's own can): the open menu screen goes back one; else the scene's own back() takes it (it returns true when it did); else a scene in play pauses into the menu, and a paused one goes on. Returns false where there is nowhere to go (a title)\n- `game.cornerArea()` †: where the corner's buttons (menu and full screen, top right; the touch START) cover the screen, in screen pixels { x0, y0, x1, y1 } (r.W and r.H's space), or null while none show: keep a HUD clear of it\n- `game.dispose()`: end the game for good: stop the loop and remove everything the engine added to the page (its listeners on the window, the document, the canvas and the buttons, the touch controls, the GPU lighting's canvas) and close the sound. Tests and tools that make many games call it; the canvas stays\n- `game.enableGPU(o = {})` †: turn on WebGPU lighting: where it can't draw (no WebGPU, a side view) the canvas lighting stands in, with its own ambient (game.lights.ambient) and the same lights. opts: { map, bands, ambient, fallback, ... }\n- `game.every(seconds, fn, o = {})`: run fn every `seconds` of game time. Cleared on scene change, unless { keep: true }. Returns { cancel() }\n- `game.fail(where, e)`: report an error in game code without stopping the game: logged once per place and message, kept in game.errors and shown on the error panel (the engine reports its own callbacks' errors this way): game.fail('power ' + id, err)\n- `game.flash(color = '#ffffff', seconds = .12, strength = 1)`: flash the whole screen with a color for `seconds` (dithered fade out): hits, explosions, lightning. Shorter when reduceMotion, dimmer by the player's settings.flashes\n- `game.focus(x, y, z = 0)`: camera follows this world point. Side-scrollers: game.focus(x, 0, z)\n- `game.follow(target, o = {})`: keep the camera on an object automatically (instead of calling focus every update). opts: { z: extra height to look at (default 16), lead: look ahead in the facing / moving direction }. follow(null) stops.\n- `game.freeze(s)`: hit-stop: the game stands still for s seconds (.04-.08 for a hit); input still ticks\n- `game.go(name, data, o = {})`: switch scene: game.go('play', { level: 2 }). It fades through black (dithered) unless opts.fade is 0 (a cut) or another number of seconds; opts.fade can name a transition instead: 'black', 'white', 'wipe', 'iris', 'mosaic', 'swirl' or 'dissolve', each way taking opts.seconds (.4), round opts.at (iris and swirl: a world point { x, y, z }, which each scene's camera places, or [x, y] on the screen; its centre by default). With reduce motion each is a plain fade\n- `game.hitFx(x, y, z, o = {})`: everything a good hit needs in one call: hit-stop, shake, an impact star, sparks along the hit direction, a sound, a damage number and a rumble on a gamepad. hitFx(x, y, z, { power: 1, angle, damage, color, sound: 'hit', rumble: true })\n- `game.hook(kind, fn)` †: a function of the game's own on the engine's clock: kind 'input' fn(dt) every step, paused or not, after the input is read (a host's guard, an idle timer); 'step' fn(dt) every update step in play, before the scene's update (an attract mode's watcher); 'enter' fn(name, data) as a scene starts. Returns a function that removes it\n- `game.menu(name, o)`: open the menu (the corner's menu button and the touch START do it): a pausable scene (or a game started without scenes) pauses on its pause screen; any other scene, a title, waits under the settings. menu('settings') or menu('controls') opens that screen. Without a menu (menu: false, or the agent edition) it pauses a scene in play. o: { pause: false } opens it over a world that keeps stepping, as game.live does for every pause. Returns true when a menu opened\n- `game.mouseGround()`: the mouse's point on the ground: [x, y] in world units (an array, not { x, y }), or null with no mouse over the canvas\n- `game.nextView(dir = 1)`: cycle through game.views (or every view): bind it to a key for players who like to switch\n- `game.note(text, seconds = 1.2)`, `game.shownNote`: a short notice at the top of the screen (zoom level, saved, ...): game.note('ZOOM 1.5x', 1). game.shownNote is the one showing, { text, end } (end in game.real seconds), or null: a game that draws it its own way takes it and sets shownNote to null\n- `game.onView(f)` †: call f(view) whenever the view changes (re-bake caches, move HUD)\n- `game.pause(reason = 'player')`: pause the game, saying why ('player' | 'focus' | 'controller' | 'menu': pauseReason); returns true if it paused. The player resumes (Escape, Start, Resume); resume() does it from code\n- `game.players` †: the players: [game.input (player 1), ...the ones who joined (game.allowJoin, game.addPlayer: section 10j)]; each an input with player (1..4), name ('P1'), color (E.PLAYER_COLORS) and device. A game of one: [game.input]\n- `game.removePlayer(p)` †: a player leaves (its input, or its number, 2 to 4); player 1 has the whole keyboard back when no one shares it\n- `game.resetCamera()` †: undo setZoom and rotateView: the scene's own zoom again (camera.start, or camera.tall on a screen held upright; 1 without), no turn. The player's zoom stays (settings.zoom)\n- `game.resume()`: take the game out of pause (and close the pause screen)\n- `game.rotateView(deg)` †: turn the camera around the vertical axis in ground views: game.rotateView(45) (degrees). Side views never turn\n- `game.saves()` †: game.saves: the save slots (section 10k), made when first read\n- `game.script(fn, o)` †: run a coroutine on game time, in the update step: game.script(function* (game) { yield .5; yield E.until(() => door.open); yield game.tween(cam, { y: 0 }, 1); yield E.all([a, b]); ... }, { keep }). Each yield waits: a number (seconds), nothing (one step), a condition (a function, or E.until(fn)), a tween or script (until it is done), a generator (run as a script of its own, and waited for), E.all([...]) or E.any([...]) (each of these). It runs at once to its first yield. Cutscenes, boss patterns, wave timelines, tutorials, attract modes. Returns the Script\n- `game.setView(v)`: switch the view: an id from E.VIEWS ('iso', 'side', ...) or a View; keeps zoom and turn\n- `game.setZoom(z)` †: camera zoom on top of the view: game.setZoom(1.5) (.5 .. 3), the game's own; where the scene lets the player zoom (camera.zoom), theirs multiplies it (game.zoom is the two together). Characters, maps, props, particles and lights are drawn by code, so they re-rasterize crisply at the new size; HUD text and overlays stay the same.\n- `game.shake(a)`: shake the screen by a (pixels, decaying; at most 10 in all); none when reduceMotion, scaled by the player's settings.shake\n- `game.shaking` †: how hard the screen shakes now (0 still, up to 10): shake() adds to it and it dies away. A camera of the game's own (a 3D world's) shakes by it\n- `game.splitScreen(n, o = {})` †: split the screen into n viewports (2 to 4; 0 or 1: one screen again), o: { layout: 'auto' (side by side on a wide screen, stacked on a tall one; a grid for 3 or 4) | 'columns' | 'rows' | 'grid', follow: [targets], z: 16, gap: 1 }\n- `game.start(hooks)`: Start the loop. Either one scene: game.start({ update(dt), draw(r) }) or several: game.start({ scenes: { title: {...}, play: {...} }, scene: 'title' }) A scene is { enter(data), exit(), update(dt), draw(r), back(), pausable, view, views, input, res, expand, touch, camera } (all optional). start() with nothing resumes a stopped loop. A headless game, or one made with loop: false, runs only by advance().\n- `game.start({ view, views, input, res, expand, touch })`: a scene's, applied when it starts: input ('PLATFORMER'), res ('snes'), expand (true), touch (['jump', 'attack']); res and expand stay until another scene sets them\n- `game.start({ back })`: a scene's back() takes a phone's back gesture (game.back()) first: return true when it went back somewhere (a sub-menu closed)\n- `game.start({ camera })`: a scene's { zoom: [.75, 2], start: 1, tall: 1.5, wheel: true }: the player zooms the camera within zoom (the wheel, - and =, a pinch, Settings, Camera zoom: theirs, kept, on top of the game's; false or none: they can't; a function of the game: a range per screen shape, g => [g.upright ? 1 : .85, 2]), from start, the zoom it starts at (tall: on a screen held upright, game.upright; 1 without); wheel: false leaves the mouse wheel to the game\n- `game.start({ boot })`: { title, version, steps: [[label, fn]], messages, min, checks, accent } runs the boot first (section 10c): the steps one a frame behind the boot screen (all at once when headless or loop: false); boot: false hides a splash\n- `game.stop()`: stop the loop; the game keeps its state, and start() resumes it\n- `game.tween(target, to, seconds, o)` †: ease target's numbers to new values over seconds of game time: game.tween(door, { y: 40 }, .5, { ease: 'outBack', delay, repeat (more plays; -1 forever), yoyo (every other play runs back), onDone(target), keep (outlive the scene) }). ease: a name in E.ease ('outQuad' by default) or a function of u 0..1. It starts from the values the target has once its delay is over. Returns the Tween: tween.done, tween.cancel()\n- `game.upright`: true while the game's screen (r.W x r.H) is upright, taller than 1.3 times its width: a phone held upright, with expand: true (a fixed res keeps its own shape on any phone, so it is never upright there). A scene's camera.tall zoom applies then: it zooms a tall picture in to fill it\n- `game.viewports` †: a split screen's viewports ({ i, x, y, w, h, cam, focus(x, y, z), follow(target, { z }): game.splitScreen), or [] on one screen\n- `game.zoomBy(k)` †: the player zooms the camera by a factor (1.1: 10% closer, .9: farther), as the wheel, - and = and a pinch do (a zoom button of the game's own can): within the scene's camera.zoom range, kept as their setting (settings.zoom, on top of the game's zoom), and noted at the top of the screen ('ZOOM 1.65x': the number a scene's camera.start takes). Does nothing where the scene doesn't let the player zoom. Returns the zoom\n- `game.zoomRange()` †: the zooms the player may take the camera to here, [min, max] (the scene's camera.zoom, or the game's; a function of the game gives one per screen shape or level), or null where they can't (camera.zoom false or none)\n- `game.alpha = .5`: the fraction of a fixed step (0..1) of real time not yet run: interpolate drawing with it if motion must be smooth at any refresh rate. The accumulator starts half a step in, so frame times that wobble keep a steady step count\n- `game.audio`: chip-sound synthesizer: game.audio.sfx('jump'), game.audio.music(song)\n- `game.baseView` †: the chosen view without zoom and turn\n- `game.bootError = null`: the error a boot step failed with (the game did not start), or null\n- `game.booted`: a promise of the game once its boot (start's boot option, section 10c) has run and it has started: await it in a test whose boot steps return promises\n- `game.cam`: camera. smooth = seconds of lag; bounds(view) = clamp rect; room = [w, h] world units for screen-by-screen rooms; moving = sliding between rooms; offset = [dx, dy] screen pixels from the screen's centre to where the focus sits (above a phone's touch buttons: [0, -40]); x, y: the camera's position in projected pixels (its top-left corner); vx, vy: how fast it is moving there; tx, ty, tz: the world point it is following (focus sets it); snap: true makes it jump there at the next step instead of gliding (a new level, a teleport); window: [w, h] screen pixels, a dead zone round the focus it moves in before the camera follows (Super Mario World's; [48, 0] for sideways only); snapY: true follows a followed body's height only when it lands (onGround) or nears the screen's edge (side-scrollers); frame(targets, { margin: 24, zoom: [min, max] or false }) keeps every one of them (anything with x, y, z) in view, centered between them and zoomed out (no further than min) when they spread apart, until frame(null); free: a free camera (strategy, sims, board games): true or { keys: true, edge: 10, drag: true, speed: 300, bounds: [x0, y0, x1, y1] }: the keys or a pad's stick pan it (input.move(), speed screen pixels a second), the mouse at the screen's edge (within edge pixels), a drag (input.pan(): a finger on open ground, a mouse's middle or right button) one to one; a zoom (wheel, pinch) zooms toward the pointer or the pinch; its focus keeps to bounds (world x, y); focus() still moves it; in every view and turn; null (as it starts): off\n- `game.debug = null`: a game's own handle for tools and tests: game.debug = { snapshot() { return {...} }, teleport(x, y, z) {...} }. tools/check.mjs prints snapshot() and tools/replay.mjs logs it at the end\n- `game.disposed = true`: true after dispose()\n- `game.drawEvery = 1`: 1, or 2 while a fast display (a 120 Hz phone) refreshes quicker than the game can draw (a frame's cost, or refreshes missed: its GPU behind): every other refresh is drawn, evenly, instead of random ones missed (the updates still run every refresh). The player's Frame rate setting (game.settings.frameRate: 'auto', '60', 'full') can fix it at 2 or 1\n- `game.errors = []`: the errors caught in update and draw, each { where, scene, message, stack }, once per kind\n- `game.fadeTime = .22`: seconds for the dithered fade between scenes (0 = instant cuts)\n- `game.fps = 60`: frames drawn per second over the last half second of real time\n- `game.fullscreen`: full screen: game.fullscreen.toggle(), .on, .supported\n- `game.gpu = null` †: the WebGPU lighting after enableGPU(), or null\n- `game.headless`: true for a game with no display (o.headless, or no DOM: Node)\n- `game.hitstop = 0`: seconds of hit-stop left (freeze: a scene's entry ends it)\n- `game.hz = 60`: the display's refresh rate, measured\n- `game.input`: keyboard, mouse, gamepad and touch, read as actions (pressed, down, move, ...)\n- `game.lights` †: canvas lighting (lights.enabled, lights.add in draw)\n- `game.live` †: true (o.live): a pause opens the menus over a world that keeps stepping (an online or shared world can't stop for one player): paused is true and the scene's update goes on, the menu holding the player's own input back\n- `game.o`: the options the game was made with, the defaults filled in (view, res, expand, minW, minH, maxW, maxH, bg, step, input, loop, headless)\n- `game.particles`: dust, sparks, debris and text in the world\n- `game.pauseOverlay = true`: false hides the engine's PAUSED overlay (a game drawing its own pause screen)\n- `game.pauseReason = null`: why the game paused: 'player' | 'focus' (the page was hidden) | 'controller' (unplugged) | 'menu', or null\n- `game.paused = false`: true while paused (a pausable scene toggles it on 'pause'; steps still run, scenes don't update)\n- `game.r`: the renderer handed to draw(r) (null when headless)\n- `game.real = 0`: seconds the game has stepped through, pauses and hit-stop included (blinking HUD, notes)\n- `game.reduceMotion`: the player asked for less motion (prefers-reduced-motion): shake and flashes are softened\n- `game.scene = null`: the scene running\n- `game.sceneName = null`: its name ('main' for a game started with one scene)\n- `game.scenes = null`: the scenes given to start(), by name\n- `game.screen`: the low-resolution screen (its W, H, scale and buffer)\n- `game.settings`: the player's choices (game.settings.volume, game.settings.set('volume', .5), your own from menu.settings), saved and applied (section 10b)\n- `game.shell = null` †: the E.Shell over this game (an HTML interface: screens and HUD), or null\n- `game.stats`: per-frame timings (ms) and counts, for profiling overlays\n- `game.stats.updateMs`, `game.stats.renderMs`: milliseconds the last frame spent updating and drawing\n- `game.stats.frameMs`: the real time since the last frame\n- `game.stats.steps`: the updates it ran\n- `game.stats.items`: what it queued for drawing\n- `game.stats.actors`: actors drawn\n- `game.stats.culled`: actors skipped off screen\n- `game.time = 0`: game seconds since start (it stops during pause and hit-stop, and slows with timeScale)\n- `game.timeScale = 1`: game speed, 1 normal, .25 slow motion, 0 stopped (it outlives a scene change: a game's speed setting; restore a slow motion with a kept timer)\n- `game.view`: the view the game draws in now (the chosen one, with zoom and turn: read it, change it with setView)\n- `game.views`: views this game is designed for (view cycling and the checker use it). null = every view\n- `game.yaw = 0` †: its turn in degrees on top of the view (rotateView)\n- `game.zoom = 1` †: the camera's zoom now (1 = as the view is): the game's (setZoom, a scene's camera.start) times the player's (settings.zoom: the wheel, a pinch), within the scene's camera.zoom range\n- `E.PLAYER_COLORS: #4a8aff, #ff4a4a, #4ad860, #ffd040` †: the players' colors: player 1 blue, 2 red, 3 green, 4 yellow (game.players)\n- `E.showError(title, e)`: show an error in the red box at the top of the page (the engine does it for errors in update and draw); a click hides it. Before play has started it shows on the boot screen instead, as a report a player can screenshot\n\n## 10b. Standard features\n\n- `settings.default(key)`: a setting's default value\n- `settings.define(sp)`: add a setting: { key, label, type: 'toggle' | 'slider' | 'select', value, min, max, step, options: [[value, label]] or [value], help, group: 'Game', apply(game, value) }. Its saved value is loaded (when it is a valid one) and applied; it shows on the settings screen. Returns its value\n- `settings.keys(group)`: the keys of a group's settings, in order ('Display', 'Sound', 'Comfort', 'Controller', 'Touch', 'Game'), or every key\n- `settings.reset(group)`: put a group's settings (or all of them) back to their defaults; a game's saves are not touched\n- `settings.set(key, v)`: change a setting: checked (a slider kept in range, a choice among its options), saved, applied, onChange told. Returns the value kept (the old one when v is not a valid value)\n- `settings.spec(key)`: the spec a setting was defined with (its label, type, range, options), or undefined\n- `settings.saved`: false when the last save failed (storage blocked or full: the choices last for this visit)\n- `fullscreen.enter(quiet)`: go full screen. Browsers allow it only from a tap, click or key press: call it from one (a button, the menu, a key's update step). Returns a promise of whether it worked (a refusal is kept in failed and noted on screen, unless quiet)\n- `fullscreen.exit()`: leave full screen (Escape and the phone's back gesture do it too)\n- `fullscreen.on`: true while the page is full screen\n- `fullscreen.standalone`: true when the page was opened from a home screen as an app (no browser bars: nothing to go full screen from)\n- `fullscreen.supported`: true where this page can go full screen (not an iPhone, which has Add to Home Screen instead; not a frame without permission; not headless)\n- `fullscreen.toggle()`: full screen on or off, as the player asked (the corner's button, the menu): their choice is kept in settings.fullscreen, so a game starting on a phone goes full screen by itself only while they haven't turned it off\n- `fullscreen.failed = null`: game: the game it serves; failed: why the last request was refused (a message), or null\n\n## 10c. Boot\n\n- `E.bootSplash(title = '')`: the boot screen's markup, for the very start of a page's <body>: it shows from the first paint, its bar moving while the page's scripts load (a pure CSS animation), and says so if the game's script never runs. The engine takes it over\n- `E.diagnose(game = E.current)`: the report a bug needs, at any time (and on the boot screen when a game can't start): { engine, game, time, page, browser, device, screen, canvas, gpu, sound, storage, input, scene, errors, text (all of it, one line each, for a screenshot or a bug report), short (one line) }. game: the game to report on (E.current)\n\n## 10d. Tweens and scripts\n\n- `new E.Script(game, fn, o = {}) → script` †: a script, made by game.script: done (it returned, or was cancelled), value (what it returned), cancel(); another script can yield it\n- `script.cancel()` †: stop it, and the scripts it is waiting on (its finally blocks run)\n- `script.done = false` †: it returned, or was cancelled\n- `script.keep` †: it outlives the scene\n- `script.value` †: what it returned\n- `new E.Tween(game, target, to, seconds, o = {}) → tween` †: a tween, made by game.tween: done (it ended, or was cancelled), cancel(); a script can yield it\n- `tween.cancel()` †: stop it where it is (no onDone)\n- `tween.done = false` †: it ended, or was cancelled\n- `tween.keep` †: it outlives the scene\n- `tween.target` †: what it moves\n- `tween.to` †: the values it moves them to\n- `E.all(list)` †: a script's wait for every one of its waits (seconds, conditions, tweens, scripts): yield E.all([a, b])\n- `E.any(list)` †: a script's wait for the first of its waits to be over: yield E.any([boss.script, 30])\n- `E.until(fn)` †: a script's wait until fn() is true: yield E.until(() => door.open)\n\n## 10e. Paths\n\n- `new E.Path(points, o = {}) → path` †: new E.Path(points, { closed: false, smooth: null }): points [x, y, z] (z optional) or { x, y, z } (a side view's height is z); closed runs back to the first; smooth: 'catmull' curves through every point (Catmull-Rom); at(d), nearest(x, y, z), draw(r) below\n- `path.at(d)` †: the point d along it: { x, y, z, angle (its heading on the ground, radians: 0 east), d } (closed: d wraps round; open: held at the ends)\n- `path.draw(r, color = '#ffe070')` †: draw it over the scene to see it (debugging): a line, and a dot at each of its points\n- `path.nearest(x, y, z)` †: the distance along it of its point nearest (x, y) on the ground (and z, when given): a racer's progress round a track, a rail's grab point\n- `path.closed` †: it runs back to the first\n- `path.length` †: world units from end to end (round, when closed)\n- `path.points` †: the points it was made from, [x, y, z] each\n\n## 10g. Weather\n\n- `E.WEATHER: rain, snow, ash, leaves, sand` †: the kinds of weather: n drops at amount 1, fall speed (world units a second), colors, streak (seconds of motion a drop's line shows), sway, rest (seconds it lies where it lands), splash, wind (how hard the wind pushes it), size, big (the share a pixel bigger). Add your own: E.WEATHER.embers = { ... }\n\n## 10k. Save slots\n\n- `new E.Saves(game, o) → saves` †: game.saves (made when first read): o is menu.saves ({ slots, version, migrate, write, read })\n- `saves.any` †: is there any save to load (a title's Continue)?\n- `saves.autosave(o)` †: save to the autosave (what write() gives)\n- `saves.copy(from, to)` †: copy a slot's save onto another (over what is there); false when the first is empty\n- `saves.erase(slot)` †: erase a slot\n- `saves.get(slot)` †: a slot's save as it is stored, checked: null when empty; { slot, damaged: true } when it can't be read; { newer: true } from a newer version of the game; else { slot, data, place, level, time, at (the date saved), version }\n- `saves.latest()` †: the most recent save that loads, or null (a title's Continue loads it)\n- `saves.list()` †: every slot, the autosave first: [{ slot, empty, place, level, time, at, damaged, newer }] (a menu's rows)\n- `saves.load(slot)` †: load a slot: its data brought up to this version (migrate), played set from it, read(save) called. Returns the save ({ data, place, level, time, slot }), or null when the slot is empty, damaged or from a newer version\n- `saves.save(slot, o)` †: save to a slot (0.., or 'auto'): o ({ data, place, level }) or what write() gives. Returns true when it is kept (in storage, or for the visit when storage is blocked: blocked tells), false when there was nothing to save\n- `saves.show(mode = 'load', done)` †: open the standard save screen over the game (E.Shell's): 'save' (to a slot) or 'load'; done(save) after a load or a save. False without a menu (a headless game, menu: false)\n- `saves.blocked = false` †: storage refused a save (they last for the visit)\n- `saves.canSave = true` †: the pause menu offers Save (a JRPG turns it off away from save points)\n- `saves.game` †: its game\n- `saves.migrate` †: an older version's data brought up to this one (or null: kept as it is)\n- `saves.played = 0` †: seconds played in the game's scenes (paused time and titles left out), saved with each save and set by a load\n- `saves.read` †: what a load does with { data, place, level, time, slot }\n- `saves.slots` †: how many slots besides the autosave (3)\n- `saves.version` †: the game's version of its data (1), saved with each save\n- `saves.write` †: what a save holds ({ data, place, level })\n\n## 11. Humanoid rig\n\n- `new E.Attack(spec = {}, over) → slash`: new E.Attack(spec) or new E.Attack('slash', { reach: 9 }): a name from E.MOVES plus overrides\n- `slash.active`: true while the strike can connect\n- `slash.busy`: true from the wind-up to the end of the recovery\n- `slash.cancel()`: stop the swing at once (hurt, dodged into)\n- `slash.hits(targets, test, fn)`: during 'active', call fn(target) once per swing for each target where test(target) is true. Returns how many were hit\n- `slash.start(canCancel = false)`: begin a swing if idle (or when canCancel and already recovering). Returns true if it started\n- `slash.state`: what Humanoid.update wants as `attack` (null when idle)\n- `slash.u`: 0..1 progress through the current phase\n- `slash.update(dt)`: advance the swing by dt seconds; returns the phase that began during it ('active' = play the swing sound), or null\n- `slash.hit`: the targets this swing has hit (each is hit once)\n- `slash.phase = null`: 'wind' | 'active' | 'recover', or null when idle\n- `slash.spec`: the move: seconds of wind, active and recover, and its shape for the rig (a0, a1, z0, z1, reach, hitAt)\n- `slash.t = 0`: seconds into the phase\n- `new E.Combo(specs, o = {}) → combo`: new E.Combo(items, { window: .3 }): items are move names, specs or Attacks\n- `combo.active`: true while the current hit can connect\n- `combo.busy`: true while a hit of the chain is swinging\n- `combo.cancel()`: stop the chain; the next press starts it over\n- `combo.current`: the Attack of the current hit\n- `combo.hits(targets, test, fn)`: the current hit's hits(targets, test, fn): fn(target) once per swing where test(target) is true\n- `combo.press()`: start or continue the chain. Returns true if a hit started\n- `combo.state`: what Humanoid.update wants as `attack` (null when idle)\n- `combo.update(dt)`: advance the current hit; returns the phase that began, as Attack.update\n- `combo.idle = 99`: seconds since the last hit ended\n- `combo.moves`: the Attacks\n- `combo.step = -1`: the index of the current hit (-1 before the first)\n- `combo.window`: seconds after a hit ends that a press still chains\n- `new E.Humanoid(o = {}) → rig`: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.3, build, weapon, outfit, hair, hat, sleeves, armor, hood, cape, colors, ... }): a character rig posed from math every step (API.md, \"Characters\", lists the options); update(dt, state) each step, draw(g, ox, oy, view) inside r.actor. hat: 'cap' | 'band' | 'crown' | 'helmet' | 'pointed' (or { style, color }; E.Humanoid.hat adds one); weapon: 'sword' | 'gun' | 'staff' (E.Humanoid.weapon adds one); hood: true (drawn in colors.hair); draw: { under(g, Q, rig), over(g, Q, rig) } the game's own drawing before and after the body (Q: the joints on screen, [x, y, depth]: outfits, accessories); seed: its own random stream (blinks, its clock)\n- `E.Humanoid.hat(id, draw)` †: a hat of the game's own: draw(h) with h.at(forward, right, up) a point round the head on screen, h.r its radius there, h.c its color (o.hat.color), h.dk a darker one, h.g, h.rig\n- `E.Humanoid.hats` †: the hats a Humanoid may wear, by id: the built-ins (cap, band, crown, helmet, pointed) and a game's own\n- `E.Humanoid.pose(id, fn)` †: a held pose of the game's own: fn(J, w, rig) moves the joints (the rig's frame: forward, right, up) toward the pose by w, 0..1, which eases in and out as state.pose names it or not (blended over the rig's solve)\n- `E.Humanoid.poses` †: the held poses of a game's own, by id (the built-in poses are the rig's own solve: state.pose names either)\n- `E.Humanoid.weapon(id, spec)` †: a weapon of the game's own (or a built-in replaced): { length: the tip's distance from the hand (a number, or rig => one; tip() and the swing's trail), rest: [forward, right, up] the way it points held at rest, blade: true (a swing smears along it), draw(w): its drawing in both styles (w.style, w.at(t), w.push(depth, fn), w.u: rig._weaponDraw) }\n- `E.Humanoid.weapons` †: the weapons a Humanoid may hold, by id: the built-ins (sword, gun, staff) and a game's own\n- `rig.debug(r)` †: skeleton overlay for debugging, draws straight into the buffer\n- `rig.draw(g, ox, oy, view)`: draw into g with (ox, oy) = projected position of the rig's root\n- `rig.drawPortrait(g, x, y, size = 40, o = {})` †: a close-up bust for dialog boxes and menus, drawn live from the rig (blinks, hair and hats included): rig.drawPortrait(g, x, y, 40, { zoom: 3, turn: .6, bg: '#1a2050', frame: true }) // inside r.overlay(g => ...) zoom: pixels per world unit (3 = face fills the frame), turn: 0 = facing you, .6 = three-quarter. The bust stays upright while the rig kneels or lies down (upright: false shows the real pose); cape: false hides the cape; tint: '#808080' greys it out (a KO'd party member).\n- `rig.drawSmear(r, colors = ['#ffffff', '#dff8ff', '#8fe0f2', '#4bb1d4'])` †: motion smear for the current swing, queued in front of the rig\n- `rig.hand(which = 'R')`: world position of a hand ('R' holds the weapon): spawn bullets, sparks or thrown things here\n- `rig.head()`: world position of the head (for speech bubbles, portraits, halos)\n- `rig.kick(v)`: a squash-and-stretch kick: + stretches (a jump), - squashes (a landing, a hit)\n- `rig.land(strength = 1)` †: a heavy landing: knees bend deep and spring back over a moment; strength 1 or more drops a hand to the ground (a three-point landing), .3-.6 is a soft one. Call it on the step the body lands\n- `rig.solvePose()`: set the joints J from the rig's state this step (update and setPose call it); a clip player may wrap it on one rig: const base = rig.solvePose; rig.solvePose = function () { base.call(this); ...move J... }\n- `rig.tip()`: world position of the weapon tip (sword tip, gun muzzle, staff orb)\n- `rig.update(dt, s = {})`: s: { x, y, z, vx, vy, facing, dash, hurt, attack, air, point } air: true while jumping or falling (legs tuck), point: true to hold the weapon hand forward (aiming a gun) attack: { spec, phase: 'wind' | 'active' | 'recover', u: 0..1 }. spec.kick = true swings the right foot instead of the hand also aim (radians up), crouch, sprint, climb, hang (hangZ: the top it hangs from, above the feet), flip: 0..1 (a roll), pose: 'cheer' | 'cast' | 'guard' | 'kneel' | 'crouch' | 'wave' | 'hips' | 'down' | 'die' | 'block' or one of E.Humanoid.pose's, tilt (radians: the whole body forward about the feet, a dive) and roll (sideways), stance: 'guard' | 'ready', down: 0..1, vz (with climb: the climbing speed), land: k (a heavy landing), expr: 'smile' | 'shout' | 'angry' | 'wince', run: 0..1 (run in place)\n- `rig.worldOffset(p)`: where a point in the rig's own frame ([forward, right, up]; a joint such as rig.J.head, or one offset from it) sits, in world units from the rig's feet: add rig.x, y, z for the world point, or project it to draw at a joint\n- `rig.C`: its colors (skin, hair, cloth, pants, boot, belt, cape, capeIn, metal, hilt, eye, ...), the defaults with o.colors over them; change one and it shows at the next draw\n- `rig.J`: its joints this step in its own frame ({ hipL, kneeL, footL, shC, head, handR, ... }; rig.worldOffset(J.handR) gives one in the world)\n- `rig.atkZ = 10`: the height of the weapon hand during an attack\n- `rig.blink = 2`: seconds to the next blink\n- `rig.capeL = null`: the cape's left edge points (null until it has a cape and has moved)\n- `rig.capeR = []`: the cape's right edge points\n- `rig.dieT`: pose 'die': a stagger, the knees give, then the body topples\n- `rig.downW`: how far down it is, 0..1 (state down, pose 'down' or 'die'): it falls faster and faster and gets up steadily\n- `rig.expr` †: the face shown ('smile', 'shout', 'angry', 'wince'), or null\n- `rig.facing = 0`: the angle it faces, radians\n- `rig.hop`: how high an attack's hop lifts it now\n- `rig.hurtW = 0`: 0..1, how far into the hurt flinch\n- `rig.lastView` †: drawSmear projects the trail with the same view\n- `rig.lunge`: how far the body leans into an attack now, world units\n- `rig.o`: its options, with the build's proportions and the defaults filled in (size, build, weapon, outfit, ...; legUpper, torso, headR and the other lengths are at size 1)\n- `rig.phase = 0`: the walk cycle's phase, radians\n- `rig.poseW`: held poses: 'cheer' (item get, victory), 'cast' (both hands forward), 'guard' (fists up), 'kneel', 'crouch', 'wave', 'hips' (hands on hips), and down: 0..1 (knocked flat). stance: 'guard' (fists up while moving) | 'ready' (weapon held forward)\n- `rig.reach = 7`: how far it reaches forward\n- `rig.spin = 0`: the body's extra turn during a spinning move, radians\n- `rig.sq = 0`: squash and stretch, -0.45..0.45 (kick adds to it)\n- `rig.sqV = 0`: its speed\n- `rig.t`: seconds of its own clock (idle breathing, blinks)\n- `rig.trail = []` †: recent points of the blade (or fist) tip, for the swing's smear\n- `rig.x = 0`, `rig.y = 0`, `rig.z = 0`: where its feet are (from the last update)\n- `E.MOVES: slash, backslash, overhead, rising, thrust, spin, plunge, twohand, jab, cross, hook, uppercut, haymaker, elbow, ... (24)`: a library of named attacks with timing and body motion (anticipation, lunge, step, twist, hop). new E.Attack('overhead'), new E.Attack('thrust', { reach: 10 }), new E.Combo(['jab', 'cross', 'hook', 'uppercut']) rig.update(dt, { ..., attack: E.move('roundhouse', .5) }) // any move at any moment, for previews and cutscenes The same move works on every build and in every view: heights are relative to the shoulders (rel: true), reaches scale with arm length, and horizontal swings turn into screen-plane swings in side views. A move's spec fields:\n- `E.MOVES { a0, a1, z0, z1 }`: a0 / a1 angles (+ = the right side, or up in the screen plane), z0 / z1 heights\n- `E.MOVES { reach, r0, r1, hand, plane, blade }`: reach, or r0 / r1 (an extending thrust); hand: 'R' | 'L' | 'both', plane: 'side' (always vertical), blade: 0 (no trail)\n- `E.MOVES { wind, active, recover }`: the phases' seconds\n- `E.MOVES { kick, spin, lunge, hop, crouch, lean, twist, hold }`: the body's motion (kick: true swings the right foot)\n- `E.knockback(from, target, speed = 160, up = 0)`: push a target away from a point (attacker): uses target.push (E.Body) or its vx / vy. up = upward speed (knock into the air)\n- `E.move(name, u = .5, phase = 'active')`: any move from E.MOVES at any moment, for previews and cutscenes: rig.update(dt, { ..., attack: E.move('roundhouse', .5) }) (u 0..1 through phase)\n\n## 12. Blob rig\n\n- `new E.Blob(o = {}) → blob`: new E.Blob({ R: 6.5, colors, ears, horns, wings, feet, tail, mouth, face }): a squash-and-stretch rig for slimes and jellies; blob.update(dt, state) each step, blob.draw(g, ox, oy, view) in an r.actor\n- `blob.draw(g, ox, oy, view)`: draw(g, ox, oy, view). Creature features from the constructor options: ears: 'rabbit' | 'cat', horns: true, wings: true (flapping bat wings), feet: true, tail: true, mouth: true | 'fangs', face: 'front' (eyes always on the camera side, for Mana-style monsters) update(dt, { flap: 0..1, hang: true }) slows or folds the wings and hangs a bat upside down from its anchor.\n- `blob.kick(v)`: a squash kick: + stretches (a hop), - squashes (a landing, a hit)\n- `blob.update(dt, s = {})`: s: {squash target -0.4..0.4, look:[dx,dy] ground dir, squint}\n- `blob.C`: its colors: dk, base, lt, spec, eye, pupil\n- `blob.flap`: the wings' speed now, 0 (folded) .. 1\n- `blob.flapP`: the wing beat's phase\n- `blob.hang`: roosting upside down\n- `blob.look = [1, 0]`: where its eyes look, a ground direction\n- `blob.o`: its options (R: the radius, 6.5; ears, horns, wings, feet, tail, mouth, face, colors)\n- `blob.scale = 1`: drawing scale (1)\n- `blob.sq = 0`: squash -0.45..0.45 (- flat, + tall)\n- `blob.sqV = 0`: its speed\n- `blob.squint = false`: eyes narrowed\n- `blob.t = 0`: its clock, seconds\n- `blob.target = 0`: the squash it springs toward\n- `blob.walk`: 0..1, how fast it is walking (its feet patter)\n\n## 13. Tile map\n\n- `new E.TileMap(o = {}) → map`: Walls standing on a floor (top-down, isometric, brawler). Either { rows: [...ASCII...], legend: { '#': 1, '@': 'hero' }, types, floorTex } (recommended) or { w, h, cells: [ids...], floor: [tags...], blocked: [0 | 1...], types, floorTex } (built in code: fill, setFloor; with no cells it starts as open floor).\n- `new E.TileMap({ types })`: types[id] = { h, top, side, line, course, cut, cutH, face, roof }\n- `new E.TileMap({ types[id].face })`: the wall's material (E.MATERIALS): 'brick' (default) | 'stone' | 'rock' | 'plank' | 'timber' | 'plaster' | 'hedge' | 'none' | any material id or '#hex pattern'\n- `new E.TileMap({ types[id].roof })`: the top's material: 'speckle' (default) | 'plain' | 'tiles' (roof shingles) | 'slab' (paving) | 'leaves' | 'grass' | any material id or '#hex pattern'\n- `new E.TileMap({ floorTex })`: floorTex(x, y, floorTag, view) -> [r, g, b] or [r, g, b, glow]\n- `new E.TileMap({ floorMat, floorMats })`: a material for the floor, floorMats: { tag: material } for tagged cells (in place of floorTex)\n- `new E.TileMap({ block })`: ['water', 'lava'] floor tags nobody can walk on (or put block: true in a legend entry)\n- `new E.TileMap({ chunks })`: bake the floor in squares near the camera (true, false; by default a map of more than 128 x 128 cells)\n- `map.arcClear(a, b, apex = 20, r = 0)`: does a jump arc from a to b (feet, { x, y, z }) clear every top on the way? The arc is a parabola peaking apex above the higher end; r > 0 also checks a track on each side of it. Jumps, throws, aerial takedowns\n- `map.block(cx, cy, on = true)`: mark a cell blocked or free at runtime (a bridge appears, lava cools)\n- `map.bounds(view)`: projected pixel bounds of the whole map for this view, { x0, y0, x1, y1 } with an 8-pixel margin (camera clamping: game.cam.bounds = v => map.bounds(v))\n- `map.cell(cx, cy)`: the wall type id at a cell (0 = floor, -1 outside the map)\n- `map.center(cx, cy)`: the world point at a cell's center\n- `map.collide(b)`: push a circle { x, y, r } out of walls. If the body has a number z, walls lower than z + step (default 2) do not block it (it is standing or jumping above them). Returns true when it touched a wall.\n- `map.drawFloor(r)`: draw the baked floor (first thing in draw, after r.sky); walls come from queueWalls(r)\n- `map.drop(from, dir, o = {})`: what lies past the edge ahead: the first drop of more than step within reach (default 12) of `from` in direction dir, and the ground a body of its radius lands on just past it: { x, y, z, fall } or null (no edge, or a wall first). AI that won't walk off roofs, hop-downs, fall warnings\n- `map.fill(cx, cy, w, h, id)`: put wall type id in every cell of a w x h block of cells (0 clears them): build maps in code\n- `map.find(tag)`: first spawn with this tag ({ x, y, z, cx, cy }), or null\n- `map.findAll(tag)`: every spawn with this tag\n- `map.floorAt(x, y)`: floor tag from the legend ({ floor: 'water' }) at a world point, or null\n- `map.floorBytes()` †: the bytes the baked floors hold now (each view's, chunks and the lighting's canvases counted): what a big map's floor costs in memory\n- `map.groundAt(x, y, r = 0, z = 1e9, step = 2)`: highest surface under a circle footprint that a body at height z can stand on (floor = 0)\n- `map.height(cx, cy)`: a cell's top: its wall's h, 0 on the floor, 1e9 outside the map (the height rules: E.HEIGHTS)\n- `map.heightAt(x, y)`: wall height at a world point (0 on the floor)\n- `map.isFront(cx, cy, view)`: is wall cell (cx, cy) in front of what the camera looks at, so cutaway cuts it down to its type's cutH (6 by default) in this view? A game may replace it on its map (map.isFront = (cx, cy, view) => ...)\n- `map.landing(from, dir, o = {})`: where a jump from `from` in direction dir lands across a gap: past the first drop ahead (a top more than step below its feet), the nearest spot min..max away that rises at most rise and falls at most drop from its feet, stands above the gap's bottom, has room to stand, and that a jump arc peaking apex above the higher end clears (arcClear). { x, y, z, d } or null (a wall before any gap, or nowhere to land). o: { min: 35, max: 83, rise: 11, drop: 18, apex: 20 }\n- `map.ledge(from, dir, o = {})`: a ledge to climb: in direction dir from `from`, the nearest top that rises min..rise above its feet within reach, with room to stand past the edge, looking straight ahead and spread radians to each side (a gap first means a jump: landing). { x, y, z (the spot to stand on top), rise, facing (the angle climbed), edge: [x, y] (where the hands grip) } or null. o: { reach: 18, min: 4, rise: 82, spread: .28 }\n- `map.los(x0, y0, x1, y1)`: line of sight across the ground (true if nothing solid in between). It ignores heights: sight() is the one that sees over a low wall and from a roof\n- `map.pick(sx, sy, at = E.current)`: the cell under a screen point on a map with heights (tactics, strategy, board games): the first top or wall face the line of sight meets, front to back, in any view, turn and zoom. sx, sy: the HUD's pixels (input.clicked(), input.pointer()); at: the game whose view and camera drew it (E.current by default) or { view, ix, iy }. Returns { cx, cy, x, y, z, face: 'top' | 'side' } (the world point it meets) or null off the map. Cells only, as height() has them (not props, actors, or walls cut away)\n- `map.queueWalls(r)`: queue every visible wall box. Call once per frame after drawFloor.\n- `map.ray(a, b, o = {})`: the first cell top that rises above the straight segment from a to b ({ x, y, z } world points; o.eye and o.target lift each end): { clear, t (0..1 along it), x, y, z } where it stops (b's point when clear). It walks every cell the segment crosses, not samples; a top level with the segment doesn't block it, and outside the map is solid\n- `map.set(cx, cy, v)`: put wall type v at a cell (0 opens it: a door, a broken wall); bumps version, so caches and flow fields rebuild\n- `map.setFloor(cx, cy, tag, block)`: give a cell a floor tag ('water', 'grass', what floorTex and floorAt read), and block it (deep water, lava) or not\n- `map.sight(a, b, o = {})`: can a's eyes (z + eye) see b's chest (z + chest)? E.HEIGHTS gives both unless o ({ eye, target }) or the bodies' own eye and chest say otherwise. A guard sees over a planter lower than its eyes, and down from a roof\n- `map.solid(id, x, y, z = 0, o = {})` †: make a shape solid in this map: map.solid(id | entry, x, y, z, o) with r.shape's placement (facing, scale, flip, params) -> the solid ({ x, y (the footprint's middle), z, top, hx, hy, c, s, shape, px, py (where it was placed) }; map.solids holds them; what stands on one sorts after it). A body overlapping its span in height is pushed out of its footprint; one coming down onto it lands on its top; one wholly below passes under. Register solids before the bodies' updates; drawing never sets them\n- `map.solidAt(x, y)`: solidCell at a world point\n- `map.solidCell(cx, cy)`: true where a cell has a wall (or is outside the map); walkable() also counts blocked floor\n- `map.standable(x, y, z, r = 5, step = HEIGHTS.step)`: can a body of radius r stand at (x, y) with its feet at z? Nothing under its footprint rises above z + step (it would collide), no blocked floor (water, lava) is under it at floor level, and a top within step below z holds it up\n- `map.toCell(x, y)`: world point -> [cx, cy] cell (center(cx, cy) goes back)\n- `map.walkable(cx, cy)`: true where a body can walk: no wall and not a blocked floor (water, lava). Pathfinding uses this.\n- `map.wall(from, dir, o = {})` †: the wall face a body touches or faces: from `from`, in direction dir (an angle or [dx, dy]), the first point within reach whose top rises more than a step above its feet. { x, y (on the face), nx, ny (the face's normal, out toward the body), top (how high it rises there), bottom (the floor at its foot), facing (the angle into it) } or null. Cells give axis faces; a map.solid box, the face back along dir. o: { reach: from.r + 4 }\n- `map.T`: the tile size in world units (16)\n- `map.ao`: contact shadow width (world units) along the foot of walls; 0 turns it off\n- `map.blocked`: cells nobody can walk on (deep water, lava, pits): legend { floor: 'water', block: true } or TileMap { block: ['water'] }\n- `map.cells`: the wall type id of every cell, row by row (index cy * w + cx; change one with set)\n- `map.chunks` †: the floor baked in squares of 128 screen pixels as the camera comes to them (its colors and the GPU lighting's canvas both), the far ones let go, so a 256 x 256-cell map costs what the screen shows; a change to cells re-bakes only the squares over them. True, false, or by default when the map has more than 128 x 128 cells\n- `map.cutaway`: walls between the camera and the floor drop low (types with cut: true; false turns it off)\n- `map.floorBudget` †: the bytes the floor's bakes may hold (64 MB): past it the least recently used go (floorBytes())\n- `map.floorMat` †: a material (E.MATERIALS id or '#hex pattern') for every floor cell\n- `map.floorMats` †: { tag: material } for the cells with that floor tag, over floorMat (untagged cells have the tag null). Either stands in for floorTex\n- `map.floorTags`: each cell's floor tag from the legend ('water', 'lava', ...), row by row, or null for a map built from cells\n- `map.floorTex`: floorTex(x, y, tag, view) -> [r, g, b], the floor's color per pixel\n- `map.floors`: the baked floor images by view (floors = {} re-bakes them after floorTex changes)\n- `map.h`, `map.w`: the map's size in cells\n- `map.light`: null shades wall faces with the shapes' light (from the viewer's upper left: a face toward it lit, the other face of a corner a shade darker, in every view), or a direction [x, y, 0] the light comes from\n- `map.outside`: the ground beyond the map (ground views): undefined continues the map's floor past its edge, darkening into one color that fills the rest of the screen, a color ('#2a2630') fades into that, false leaves it empty\n- `map.shimmer` †: animated sparkle on floor cells tagged 'water' or 'lava'\n- `map.spawns`: spawn points from the ASCII rows: { tag, ch, cx, cy, x, y, z } (x, y = cell center)\n- `map.types`: the wall types by id ({ h, top, side, face, roof, cut, ... })\n- `map.version = 0`: counts every change to cells and blocked cells (set, block), so caches and flow fields know to rebuild\n- `E.parseLevel(rows, legend = {})`: Parse an ASCII level (top row first; every character is one tile). legend maps a character to a number -> tile / wall type id (0 = empty) a string -> a spawn tag; the cell stays empty and spawns[] gets { tag, ch, cx, cy } an object -> any of { tile: id, floor: 'water', spawn: 'hero', block: true } (block: nobody can walk on this cell) '.' and ' ' are empty unless the legend says otherwise. Unknown characters warn once and become empty.\n\n## 13b. Materials\n\n- `E.MATERIALS: brick, stone, rock, plank, timber, plaster, hedge, speckle, plain, tiles, slab, leaves, grass, flagstone, ... (61)`: the materials by id: { color, pattern, ...its numbers, ramp, glow } or a painter of a surface (face, roof, floor, tile); add your own: E.MATERIALS.oak = { color: '#8a5a36', pattern: 'planks', bw: 3 }. Any material id takes '#hex pattern key=value' too\n- `E.PATTERNS`: the patterns, by name: (s, m) => a tone step (-1 .. +2) at a point s of a surface, for a material m's numbers. Add your own: E.PATTERNS.waves = (s, m) => Math.sin(s.u / (m.size || 3)) > .8 ? 1 : 0\n- `E.PATTERNS.bark(s)` †: fissures running up a trunk; a cut end shows its rings\n- `E.PATTERNS.bricks(s, m)` †: running bond, bw x bh (6 x 3), mortar a step darker (mortar: its step)\n- `E.PATTERNS.cloth(s, m)` †: a fine weave and soft folds (fold .9)\n- `E.PATTERNS.crate(s, m)` †: a framed and braced crate side (frame 2 wide, a diagonal brace, boards bw 3 inside); lids are planks\n- `E.PATTERNS.foliage(s, m)` †: leaf clumps (size 3.5), each lit at its top and dark at its foot, a few bright leaves\n- `E.PATTERNS.glaze(s)` †: a glossy shine where the light is strongest, a few specks\n- `E.PATTERNS.metal(s, m)` †: hard light (a shine where lit, darker in shade); rivet: a rivet every rivet units\n- `E.PATTERNS.noise(s, m)` †: soft patches (scale 4)\n- `E.PATTERNS.none()` †: the light's bands alone\n- `E.PATTERNS.planks(s, m)` †: boards bw wide (3) with joints and butt ends every len (14); along u on lids and flat sides, along v (staves) on turned surfaces; dir: 'v' turns them on flat faces\n- `E.PATTERNS.shingles(s, m)` †: staggered rows of roof tiles (course 2.5, bw 4)\n- `E.PATTERNS.speck(s, m)` †: dark and light specks (amount .08)\n- `E.PATTERNS.stones(s, m)` †: irregular stones (Voronoi cells about size across, 5) with dark joints and a lit rim\n- `E.PATTERNS.thatch(s, m)` †: straw in overlapping courses (course 3)\n- `E.PATTERNS.tiles(s, m)` †: square tiles size (4) with grout\n- `E.material(id, local, kind = 'material', depth = 0)`: a material ready to draw: { q (its entry), ramp (five [r, g, b], dark to light), hexes (the same as '#rrggbb'), pat (its pattern), glow }. id: an id (looked up in local first, a set's own, then E.MATERIALS), a shorthand string or an entry; kind names the surface, for the warning when the id is unknown (then null)\n\n## 14. Flow field\n\n- `new E.FlowField(map, o = {}) → flow`: new E.FlowField(map, { step: 2, climb: 0, drop: 0, jump: 0 }): distances over the tops a body can stand on (E.HEIGHTS). With no options it covers the floor (and walls no higher than step, which bodies walk over); a target on a roof gets a field across the roofs it connects to\n- `new E.FlowField({ step, climb, drop })`: next-door cells connect when their tops differ by at most step, or going up by up to climb and down by up to drop (agents that climb walls or hop down)\n- `new E.FlowField({ jump })`: (world units) links tops across a gap that a jump clears, straight along a row or column, by map.landing's rules (up to jumpRise higher, 11, and jumpDrop lower, 18): an agent with E.Mover's leap chases across roofs. With jump, distances are travel: a jump of k cells costs k, and a climb 1 plus its rise in cells (climbing is slow), so a chase leaps a gap rather than drop into the street and climb back up\n- `flow.dir(x, y, fx, fy)`: normalized ground direction to walk from (x, y) toward the target (straight at it where the field doesn't reach)\n- `flow.next(x, y)` †: the next cell to head for from (x, y): { x, y, z (its center and top), kind: 'walk' | 'climb' | 'drop' | 'jump', edge (how far (x, y) is from the side of its cell it leaves by) }, or null where the field doesn't reach (or at the target). An AI standing on the ground presses jump for a 'climb' or a 'jump' once edge is under its radius plus a little (E.Mover climbs or leaps; a press in the air would be kept for the landing)\n- `flow.update(x, y)`: aim the field at a world point, or at several (update([[x, y], ...]): each cell leads to the nearest, the heroes a horde chases); it rebuilds only when a target's cell or the map (map.version) changes. A map.solid shape standing on the floor raises the cells under it to its top (a crate is walked round, a low step climbed)\n- `flow.climb`: how much higher a next-door top may be (an agent that climbs walls)\n- `flow.d`: each cell's distance in steps to the target cell (1e9 where it can't get there), row by row\n- `flow.drop`: how much lower (an agent that hops down)\n- `flow.jump` †: the widest gap jumped (world units, 0 = none)\n- `flow.jumpDrop`, `flow.jumpRise` †: how much higher and lower a jump may land\n- `flow.map`: the map it paths over\n- `flow.step`: the height difference walked over freely (2)\n- `flow.tx = -1`, `flow.ty = -1`: the target's cell\n- `flow.version = -1`: the map's version it was built for\n\n## 15. Texture helpers for floortex functions\n\n- `E.tex`: floor textures for a TileMap's floorTex(x, y), each giving [r, g, b] at a world point; as materials too: floorMat: 'grass'\n- `E.tex.checker(x, y, o = {})`: checkerboard tiles (castle floors, bonus rooms). o: { a, b, size }\n- `E.tex.dirt(x, y, o = {})`: dirt, sand or snow: base color with soft noise and specks. o: { base, dark, light }\n- `E.tex.flagstone(x, y, pal, size = 16)`: stone flagstones with running-bond rows. pal: {stones:[hex..], mortar, hi, lo, speck}\n- `E.tex.grass(x, y, o = {})`: grass with darker tufts and a few flowers. o: { base, dark, light, flower } hex colors\n- `E.tex.plain(x, y, o = {})`: plain color with light noise, for roads, carpets and space floors. o: { base, amount }\n- `E.tex.planks(x, y, o = {})`: wooden floor boards running along x. o: { base, dark, line }\n- `E.tex.water(x, y, o = {})`: water with static ripple lines (baked, so it does not move). o: { base, dark, light }\n\n## 16. Platform map\n\n- `new E.PlatformMap(o = {}) → level`: { rows: [...ASCII, top row first...], legend: { '#': 1, '@': 'hero' }, types: { 1: {...} }, tile: 16, depth: 16 }. Tile kinds: 'solid' (default) | 'oneway' | 'ladder' | 'slope' | 'hazard' | 'deco' | 'back'\n- `level.bounds(view)`: projected pixel bounds of the level for this view (use for game.cam.bounds)\n- `level.cell(cx, cz)`: tile id at column cx, row cz (cz = 0 is the bottom). Left and right of the level are solid walls unless edges: 'open'\n- `level.cellsTouching(b, what)`: tiles overlapped by a body box { x, z, w, h }: [{ cx, cz, id, kind }] (optionally only one kind or id)\n- `level.center(cx, cz)`: a cell's center -> [x, z]\n- `level.crumble(cx, cz, holds = .5, respawn = 3)` †: start a crumbling block at a cell going, once (it falls away after holds seconds and comes back after respawn more; 0: never; update(dt) runs them, each step); returns its record\n- `level.draw(r)`: draw the level as the background layer: call right after r.sky() and before anything else\n- `level.drawWater(r, alpha = .45)` †: draw the water tiles see-through over what is in them (call it after the actors are queued: an overlay)\n- `level.find(tag)`: the first spawn with this tag ({ x, y: 0, z, cx, cz }), or null\n- `level.findAll(tag)`: every spawn with this tag\n- `level.groundBelow(x, z)`: top of the highest solid or one-way tile at or below height z in this column, or null over a pit\n- `level.height`: the level's height in world units (width is its width)\n- `level.kind(cx, cz)`: a cell's kind: 'solid', 'oneway', 'ladder', 'slope', 'hazard', 'deco' or 'back', null when empty ('solid' outside)\n- `level.kindAt(x, z)`: kind() at a world point\n- `level.move(b, dt, solids)`: Move a body { x, z, vx, vz, w, h } (x = center, z = feet, vz > 0 = up) by its velocity, colliding with the level. Sets b.onGround, b.onOneWay, b.hitWall (-1 left, 1 right, 0), b.hitCeiling, b.bumped ({ cx, cz, id } of a ceiling tile hit from below, for ? blocks and bricks) and b.ground (the moving solid it stands on). One-way tiles hold the body from above unless b.drop > 0. solids: optional array of extra boxes { x, z, w, h, vx, vz, oneway } (moving platforms, elevators, crates) that also carry what stands on them.\n- `level.set(cx, cz, id)`: put tile type id at a cell (0 clears it; cz counts from the bottom row); bumps version\n- `level.slopeZ(cx, cz, x)`: surface height of a slope tile at world x\n- `level.solidAt(x, z)`: true where a world point is inside a solid tile\n- `level.toCell(x, z)`: a world point -> [cx, cz]\n- `level.touching(b, what)`: does the body box overlap a tile of this kind ('hazard', 'ladder', ...) or id?\n- `level.update(dt)` †: run the crumbling blocks one step: a block falls away when its time is up, and comes back after its respawn\n- `level.width`: width and height of the level in world units\n- `level.T`: the tile size (16)\n- `level.cells`: stored bottom row first: cz = 0 is the lowest row and z grows upward\n- `level.crumbling` †: the blocks going ({ cx, cz, id, t: seconds before it falls, back })\n- `level.depth`: how deep tiles are drawn in tilted views, world units (the tile size)\n- `level.edges`: 'solid' (the default) makes the level's left and right edges walls, 'open' lets bodies walk off them\n- `level.h`, `level.w`: the level's size in cells\n- `level.spawns`: spawn points: { tag, ch, cx, cz, x, y: 0, z } with x = cell center and z = bottom of the cell (feet)\n- `level.types`: the tile types by id, with their defaults filled in ({ kind, style, side, top, ... })\n- `level.version = 0`: counts every change to the cells (set)\n\n## 16b. Platformer\n\n- `new E.Platformer(o = {}) → hero`: a side-scroller character body with modern, forgiving controls (coyote time, jump buffering, variable jump height, optional double jump, wall jump, dash, ladders, knockback). const hero = new E.Platformer({ x, z, w: 8, h: 22 }); update: hero.update(dt, level, game.input) (or an intent object for enemies: { x: -1..1, jump, jumpPressed, up, down, dash }) then read hero.onGround, hero.jumped, hero.landed, hero.facing (1 or -1), hero.climbing, hero.air draw a Humanoid with rig.update(dt, hero.rigState()) Units: world units per second. One tile = 16. A 285 jump with gravity 800 clears about 3 tiles.\n- `hero.knock(dir, speed = 130, up = 170, stun = .3)`: push the body (getting hit): dir = -1 / 1\n- `hero.release(up = 0)` †: let go of a swing, keeping its speed (up: an extra upward speed)\n- `hero.rigState(extra)`: state for Humanoid.update: rig.update(dt, hero.rigState({ attack }))\n- `hero.swingFrom(x, z, len)` †: hang from a point (a grapple's hook, a whip's ring): a pendulum from (x, z) to its hands, len long (the distance now by default); left and right pump it, jump lets go with its speed (release() does too)\n- `hero.update(dt, level, ctl = {}, solids)`: move it one step: ctl is game.input or an intent { x: -1..1, jump, jumpPressed, up, down, dash } (enemies, bots); solids: moving platforms [{ x, z, w, h, vx, vz }] to stand on\n- `hero.accel = 900`, `hero.airAccel = 650`, `hero.decel = 1100`: how fast it speeds up and slows down on the ground and in the air\n- `hero.air = false`: in the air, not climbing\n- `hero.airJumps = 0`: extra jumps in the air (1 = double jump)\n- `hero.buffer = .13`: seconds a jump pressed before landing is kept\n- `hero.climb = 70`: ladder speed\n- `hero.climbing = false`: on a ladder\n- `hero.coyote = .09`: seconds a jump still works after walking off a ledge\n- `hero.cut = .45`: the share of upward speed kept when jump is let go early (short hops)\n- `hero.dash = 0`: dash speed (0 = no dash)\n- `hero.dashTime = .22`: seconds a dash lasts\n- `hero.dashing = 0`: seconds of dash left\n- `hero.drop = 0`: seconds left of dropping through a one-way platform\n- `hero.facing = 1`: 1 right, -1 left\n- `hero.fallGravity = 1150`: while falling (snappier landings)\n- `hero.gravity = 800`: while rising\n- `hero.h = 22`, `hero.w = 8`: its box (x is its center, z its feet)\n- `hero.hanging` †: the ledge it hangs from ({ dir, top, cx }), or null\n- `hero.hitWall`: keep wall contact while holding toward it\n- `hero.jump = 285`: the jump's starting speed\n- `hero.jumped = false`, `hero.landed = false`: it jumped or landed this step\n- `hero.maxFall = 330`: the fastest fall\n- `hero.onBars` †: the bars it hangs from ({ z }), or null\n- `hero.onGround = false`: standing on something\n- `hero.onRope = null` †: true while on a rope (section 16c sets them)\n- `hero.run = 95`: top running speed\n- `hero.stun = 0`: seconds of knockback left (no control)\n- `hero.swinging` †: the point it swings from ({ x, z, L: the length, a: the angle, w: the angular speed }), or null\n- `hero.vx = 0`, `hero.vz = 0`: its speed\n- `hero.wallJump = false`: jumps off walls\n- `hero.wallSlide = 70`: the slowest fall against a wall\n- `hero.x = 0`, `hero.z = 0`: its feet's position (side view: x right, z up)\n- `hero.y = 0`: its depth (0)\n\n## 17. Body\n\n- `new E.Body(o = {}) → body`: new E.Body({ x, y, r: 5, ... })\n- `body.jump(v = 220)`: leave the ground with upward speed v (only when standing). Returns true if it jumped\n- `body.push(ix, iy, iz = 0)`: add an impulse (knockback, explosion): push(vx, vy, vz)\n- `body.update(dt, map)`: move it one step: speed, friction, walls (map.collide: lower walls are walked onto), gravity and the ground under it (map.groundAt)\n- `body.bounce = 0`: 0..1, how much speed it keeps off walls and the floor\n- `body.bounced = false`: it hit the ground and bounced up this step (dust, a thud)\n- `body.friction = 0`: slows vx and vy per second (0 = none)\n- `body.gravity = 700`: pulls z down (world units per second squared)\n- `body.groundZ = 0`: the height of what is under it\n- `body.h = 32` †: its height (32, a standing actor's: it spans z to z + h, so it passes under a solid shape above that and walks into one in that span)\n- `body.hitWall = false`: it touched a wall this step\n- `body.landed = false`: it landed this step\n- `body.onGround = true`: standing\n- `body.r = 5`: its radius\n- `body.step = 2`: the height it walks up without jumping (E.HEIGHTS.step)\n- `body.vx = 0`, `body.vy = 0`, `body.vz = 0`: its speed, world units per second\n- `body.x = 0`, `body.y = 0`, `body.z = 0`: its position (z = feet)\n\n## 17b. Actions\n\n- `E.ACTIONS: climbUp, climbJump, vault, hopDown, roll, getUp, takedown` †: the actions E.Action plays. Add your own: E.ACTIONS.name = { beats, setup, at }; setup(A) and at(A, beat, u) get the action: its options A.o, A.start (where the body began), A.body; setup may change A.beats. Play one: mover.play('takedown', { other })\n- `new E.Action(name, o = {}) → action`: new E.Action(name, { body, to, other, dir, dist, top, aerial, reverse, speed: 1 }): play E.ACTIONS[name] on a body (and on o.other for a paired action). It moves them itself: run update(dt) every step instead of their physics, and feed rigState() to the rig. Each body ends standing where the action put it\n- `action.canCancel` †: true during a beat marked as one the action may be cut short in (spec.cancel)\n- `action.cancel()` †: stop now: the bodies stay where they are\n- `action.invulnerable` †: true while hits should miss (spec.safe: [from, to] of the action's progress)\n- `action.otherState(extra)` †: the rig state for the other body of a paired action (the victim of a takedown)\n- `action.rigState(extra)` †: the rig state for the body (merge extra over it): x, y, z, its speed, facing and the beat's pose\n- `action.u` †: 0..1 through the whole action\n- `action.update(dt)` †: move the bodies one step; returns true while the action runs (false once it has ended)\n- `action.beat` †: the name of the beat playing now\n- `action.beats` †: [[name, seconds], ...]\n- `action.body`, `action.other` †: the bodies it moves\n- `action.done = false` †: it has ended (or was cancelled)\n- `action.duration` †: the action's length in seconds (o.speed shortens it)\n- `action.name` †: the action's name\n- `action.o` †: the options it was given\n- `action.spec` †: its E.ACTIONS entry\n- `action.start` †: where the body was when it began\n- `action.t = 0` †: seconds played\n\n## 17c. Mover\n\n- `new E.Mover(o = {}) → mover`: new E.Mover({ x, y, z, r: 5, ... }): any of its fields, below, as options. An E.Body: it has body.jump, push and the body's fields too\n- `mover.dodge(dir, dist = 34)` †: a dodge roll toward dir (an angle or [dx, dy]; default its facing) when it stands free. Returns the action, or null\n- `mover.play(name, o = {})` †: start an action now (E.ACTIONS name, its options): it moves the body until it ends\n- `mover.respawn()` †: back to the last safe spot (after water, a pit, a fall it shouldn't survive)\n- `mover.rigState(extra)` †: the rig state: rig.update(dt, hero.rigState({ attack })): its motion, crouch, sprint and landing, or the playing action's pose; extra is merged over it\n- `mover.update(dt, map, ctl = {}, view)` †: one step. ctl: game.input (needs the actions move, jump, and sprint / crouch if it has them; screen directions turn into ground ones through view, the game's by default) or an intent { move: [dx, dy], sprint, crouch, jumpPressed }\n- `mover.accel = 700`, `mover.airAccel = 260` †: how fast it gets there\n- `mover.action = null` †: the E.Action playing, or null\n- `mover.buffer = .12` †: seconds a jump press is kept\n- `mover.climb = true` †: climbs ledges (true)\n- `mover.climbAny = false` †: climbs any wall face (see the section's header)\n- `mover.climbMax = 82` †: the highest ledge it climbs\n- `mover.climbSpeed = 34` †: its climbing speed\n- `mover.coyote = .1` †: seconds a jump still works after leaving an edge\n- `mover.crouch = false` †: sneaking (a toggle)\n- `mover.exhausted = false` †: out of stamina until a third is back\n- `mover.facing = 0` †: the angle it faces\n- `mover.fell = 0`, `mover.hurt = false`, `mover.inHazard = false` †: what happened this step\n- `mover.gapAssist = true` †: a jump at a gap aims for the far side\n- `mover.glide = false` †: opens a glide in the air (see the header)\n- `mover.glideMin = 24`, `mover.glideSink = 26`, `mover.glideSpeed = 72` †: how high over the ground it opens, how fast it sinks and flies\n- `mover.gliding = false` †: gliding now\n- `mover.hopMax = 48` †: drops up to this deep are hopped down\n- `mover.hurtAt = 72`, `mover.rollAt = 26` †: fall heights that roll and that hurt\n- `mover.jumpSpeed = 220` †: a jump's upward speed\n- `mover.lastSafe = null` †: the last spot it stood safely, for respawn()\n- `mover.lift = 0` †: an updraft's rising speed (a number, or a function of the mover)\n- `mover.run = 85`, `mover.sneak = 32`, `mover.sprintSpeed = 125`, `mover.walk = 50` †: top speeds (world units a second)\n- `mover.sprinting = false` †: sprinting now\n- `mover.stamina` †: what is left (full to start)\n- `mover.staminaMax = 0` †: its stamina (0: none)\n- `mover.staminaRegen = 35` †: refilled a second standing\n- `mover.tire` †: { climb, climbJump, glide, sprint } it costs\n- `mover.turn = 14` †: how fast it turns, radians a second\n- `mover.vault = 14` †: walls up to this high are vaulted at a run\n- `mover.wall = null` †: the face it holds on to (map.wall's), or null\n\n## 17d. Perception\n\n- `new E.Suspicion(o = {}) → suspicion`: new E.Suspicion({ rise, decay, notice, search, alert, forget }): a guard's mind (its fields, below, are its options). Its level rises while it sees the target (faster the more exposed and nearer), decays while it doesn't, and names a state: 'calm', 'notice' (turn and look), 'search' (go to last), 'alert' (chase). An alert guard that loses the target searches for forget seconds. Each step: suspicion.update(dt, seen, target)\n- `suspicion.hear(strength, at)` †: a noise heard (strength 0..1, from E.hears) at a point: at least noticed, never more than searching\n- `suspicion.reset()` †: back to calm\n- `suspicion.update(dt, seen, target)` †: one step: seen is E.sees' result (or true / false), target the body seen. Returns the state\n- `suspicion.alert = 1`, `suspicion.notice = .3`, `suspicion.search = .65` †: the levels where each state starts\n- `suspicion.changed = false` †: the state changed this step\n- `suspicion.decay = .3`, `suspicion.rise = 1.5` †: per second\n- `suspicion.forget = 6` †: seconds an alert guard searches after losing the target\n- `suspicion.last = null` †: where it last saw or heard the target ({ x, y, z }), or null\n- `suspicion.level = 0` †: 0..1.2, how suspicious\n- `suspicion.since = 0` †: seconds since it last saw or heard anything\n- `suspicion.state = 'calm'` †: 'calm' | 'notice' | 'search' | 'alert'\n- `E.hears(lis, noise, map)`: how loud a noise ({ x, y, z, loud: its radius in world units, 80 }) is where listener stands: 1 at the noise, 0 at its radius and beyond, halved when a top of map stands between them (a wall muffles it)\n- `E.sees(obs, tgt, o = {})`: can observer see target? { seen, d (ground distance), angle (observer -> target), exposure: 0..1 }. The target must be within range and inside the cone fov wide (radians) about the observer's facing; exposure is the share of three points on it (its feet, its chest, its head) that the observer's eyes see past every top of o.map, times the target's own exposure (default 1) and .6 when it crouches (it also shows lower). o: { range: 120, fov: 1.6, facing, map, eye }\n\n## 18. Bullets\n\n- `new E.Bullets(game, o = {}) → shots`: new E.Bullets(game, { plane: 'ground' | 'side', max: 800, sparks: true }); game: its game\n- `shots.burst(base, vels)`: fire several at once from a pattern list of [vx, v2] (v2 = vy on the ground plane, vz in side view)\n- `shots.clear(team)`: remove every bullet, or only one team's\n- `shots.draw(r)`: queue them for drawing (glowing cores, or their sprites)\n- `shots.fire(b)`: fire one bullet. Fields: x, y, z, vx, vy, vz, r, dmg, life, team, color, core, sprite, pierce, grav, data\n- `shots.hit(targets, fn, team)`: test bullets of `team` against targets (objects with x, y, z and r, or w/h in side view). fn(bullet, target). Returns hits\n- `shots.update(dt, map)`: move them one step; with a map they stop at walls (a TileMap, or a PlatformMap in side views)\n- `shots.list = []`: the live bullets\n- `shots.max`: at most this many at once\n- `shots.plane`: 'ground' (they fly over x and y) or 'side' (x and z)\n- `shots.sparks`: a puff of sparks where one hits a wall\n- `E.pattern`: velocity helpers for bullet patterns. Angles in radians (0 = east / right). Each returns [vx, v2] or a list of them\n- `E.pattern.aim(a, b, speed)`: aim from a to b on the ground plane (x, y); aimSide uses x, z\n- `E.pattern.aimSide(a, b, speed)`: a velocity from a toward b in a side view (x, z)\n- `E.pattern.dir(angle, speed)`: one velocity at an angle\n- `E.pattern.ring(n, speed, offset = 0)`: n bullets in a full circle, rotated by offset\n- `E.pattern.spread(angle, n, arc, speed)`: n bullets fanned across `arc` radians around `angle`\n\n## 19. Spatial hash\n\n- `new E.SpatialHash(cell = 32) → grid`: new E.SpatialHash(cell = 32): buckets things by position, to find what is near cheaply; map: the buckets\n- `grid.add(o, x = o.x, y = o.y)`: put o in the bucket at (x, y)\n- `grid.build(list, xk = 'x', yk = 'y')`: clear, then add every live thing in list (skips o.dead); do it once a step\n- `grid.clear()`: empty it\n- `grid.near(x, y, r, fn)`: call fn(o) for everything in the cells touching the circle (check the exact distance yourself)\n- `grid.query(x, y, r)`: what lies within about r of (x, y), as a list (near() calls a function instead)\n- `grid.cell`: new E.SpatialHash(cell = 32): buckets things by position, to find what is near cheaply. cell: the bucket size in world units; map: the buckets\n\n## 20. Audio\n\n- `E.note(n)`: note name -> frequency: E.note('A4') = 440, E.note('C#5'), E.note('Bb2'). Numbers pass through\n- `E.songs: title, adventure, dungeon, boss, victory`: ready-made chiptune loops (original compositions). game.audio.music('adventure')\n- `game.audio.applyVolume()`: apply volume, musicVolume, sfxVolume and mute after setting them directly (setVolume and mute call it)\n- `game.audio.at(step, fn)` †: call fn(step) on the update step the music reaches step (counted from its start, on as it loops); the song's change cancels it. Returns { cancel() }\n- `game.audio.bus(name = 'sfx')`: a bus for a sound of the game's own (Web Audio nodes it makes on audio.ctx: a babble voice, a loop, an instrument) to connect to, so it follows the volume, the mute and the player's settings: 'sfx', 'music', 'voice' (its level is voiceVolume) or 'master'. Returns a GainNode, or null where there is no sound (headless, no Web Audio)\n- `game.audio.calibrate()` †: the audio offset's calibration: a click on each beat (100 bpm, the first of a bar higher) as the music; tap(time) on each one heard (an event's timeStamp, or nothing: this update step). After 8 taps, result is the offset to set (ms: settings.audioOffset) and done is true; taps go on refining it. stop() puts the music back\n- `game.audio.define(name, voice)`: add or replace a named sound: audio.define('zap', { wave: 'saw', freq: 1200, to: 200, dur: .2 })\n- `game.audio.duck(on = true)`: quiet the music (to 35%) while on: under a game's own pause, map or dialog screen. The engine also ducks it while game.paused\n- `game.audio.instrument(name, fn)` †: an instrument of the game's own: fn(ctx, bus, time, freq, dur, vol) makes a note's sound (Web Audio, into bus, from time); its name is a wave a music track (wave: 'pluck') or a voice takes\n- `game.audio.layer(track, vol = 1, fade = .5)` †: a track's volume as the song plays, 0..1, faded over fade seconds: layers of one song (the chase's drums in when the guards see you, out when they lose you). track: its index in song.tracks, or its name\n- `game.audio.music(song, o = {})`: Loop a song (or play once with { loop: false }). song = { bpm: 120, steps: 4, tracks: [ { wave: 'square', vol: .2, notes: 'C5 - E5 - G5 - . . | ...' }, one token per step { wave: 'triangle', notes: 'C3 . C3 . G2 . G2 .' }, { wave: 'drums', notes: 'k . h . s . h . k k h . s . h .' } ] } tokens: C4 F#3 Bb2 = note, '-' holds the previous note, '.' = rest, '|' is ignored (bar lines). steps = steps per beat (4 = sixteenth notes). Each track loops on its own length within the song's (len: steps, else its longest track's); a track may have a name, and on: false starts it silent (a layer that audio.layer brings in). THE MUSIC CLOCK: a song starts at the game's time and where it is is game time (audio.pos, headless too: the same steps hear the same beats); the sound follows that clock. o: { loop, sync: true (it pauses with the game, on game.time; else it plays on through a pause, on game.real), fade: seconds (a crossfade from the song playing), restart: true (again from its start, though it is the one playing) }. E.songs has ready-made loops: title, adventure, dungeon, boss, victory.\n- `game.audio.mute(on = !this.muted)`: toggle (or set) mute; returns the new state\n- `game.audio.onBeat(fn)` †: call fn(beat, pos) on the update step of each beat of whatever music plays (a dancer's step, a light's pulse). Returns { cancel() }\n- `game.audio.pos` †: where the music is, on the game's clock (headless too): { t: seconds since it started, step (from 0, counting on as it loops), beat, bar (four beats), at (the step in its loop), loop (times round), bpm, layers (each track's volume), playing }\n- `game.audio.ready`: true once the browser lets the game make sound\n- `game.audio.setVolume(v)`: set the master volume, 0..1\n- `game.audio.sfx(what, o = {})`: Play a sound effect: a preset name ('jump', 'coin', 'hit', 'explode', ...), a voice object, or a list of voices. opts: { vol: 0..1, pitch: multiplier, vary: random pitch spread (default 0.03), step: a step of the music playing (it sounds on that step of the music clock, scheduled with the music: a rhythm game's calls and claps), pan: -1 (left) .. 1 (right), loop: true (it sounds on until stopped: an engine, rain; through the effects' bus, so it follows the volume and the mute) }. Returns its handle: { stop(fade .03), set({ pan, vol }), playing, pan }: a handle that does nothing when nothing plays (headless, before the first key, an unknown name)\n- `game.audio.sting(song, o = {})` †: play a short song once over the music (a goal's jingle): o.at 'now', 'beat' or 'bar' (on the music's next beat or bar), o.duck (the music's volume under it, .4)\n- `game.audio.stopMusic(fade)`: stop the music (it fades out over fade seconds, .06 by default)\n- `game.audio.tempo(bpm)` †: change the music's tempo as it plays (hurry up!): where it is stays, the steps after come at the new bpm\n- `game.audio.anchors = 0` †: how many times the sound was moved to meet the clock\n- `game.audio.ctx = null`: the Web Audio context, made at the first sound (null before, or where there is none)\n- `game.audio.drift = 0` †: what is heard against the game's clock at the last frame (seconds: the sound follows the clock, so it stays near 0)\n- `game.audio.ducked = false`: true while duck(true) holds the music down\n- `game.audio.failed = null`: why there is no sound (a message), or null\n- `game.audio.musicVolume = .5`, `game.audio.sfxVolume = 1`, `game.audio.voiceVolume = 1`: 0..1 for effects, music and the voice bus (bus('voice'))\n- `game.audio.muted = false`: true while mute() is on\n- `game.audio.offset = 0` †: seconds the music sounds early, for speakers and headphones that lag (settings.audioOffset)\n- `game.audio.presets`: the named sound effects, sfx(name) plays one and define(name, voice) adds one\n- `game.audio.song = null`: the music playing (null for none)\n- `game.audio.volume = .7`: master volume 0..1\n\n## 21b. Backdrops\n\n- `new E.Backdrop(o = {}) → backdrop`: new E.Backdrop('castle-night') (a preset: E.Backdrop.presets) or new E.Backdrop({ sky: [colors top to bottom], sun, moon, stars, layers: [{ kind: 'mountains' | 'hills' | 'forest' | 'city' | 'castle' | 'clouds' | 'sea' | 'fog' | 'stalactites', color, y, height, parallax, seed }] }): a parallax sky and scenery drawn behind a level. sun, moon: { x, y (0..1 of the screen), r, color }\n- `E.Backdrop.presets` †: the preset names, all ten: day, dusk, night, castle-night, city-night, desert, forest, ocean, cave, space\n- `backdrop.draw(r)` †: draw the backdrop first in draw(r), before the level; it scrolls with the camera by each layer's parallax\n- `backdrop.layers` †: its scenery layers, back to front, with their defaults filled in\n- `backdrop.sky` †: the sky's gradient colors, top to bottom\n\n## 21c. Props\n\n- `E.PROPS: torch, candle, candelabra, chandelier, brazier, window, banner, pillar, statue, crate, barrel, chest, pot, sign, fence, lamp, door, tree, pine, bush, flowers, grass, rock, crystal, bones, skull, vines, cobweb, lantern, streetlamp, awning, hydrant, trashcan, firedrum, sandbags, cactus, palm, mushroom` †: the names of the built-in props for r.prop(name, x, y, z, opts) and E.prop(name, opts)\n- `E.prop(name, o = {})`: build (and cache) a prop: { frames: [sprite], glow: [sprite] | null, w, h }\n\n## 21e. Shapes\n\n- `E.SHAPES: barrel, crate, chest, table, chair, stool, bench, bed, bookcase, shelf, pot, jar, sack, cart, ... (25)`: the shapes by id: { about, tags, mats: { slot: material }, parts | make(params), params, foot, lights, flames } (the core library's, the sets' as 'SET:id' from E.addAssets, and your own: E.SHAPES.stool = {...})\n- `E.addAssets(set)`: register a store set (src/assets/sets/*.js: { set, format: 1, credit, sources, materials, shapes, sprites }): its entries are named 'SET:id' (so one set never changes another's, or the core's) and find their own set's materials and shapes first. An id that is already there warns and keeps the first. Returns { shapes: [ids], materials: [ids], sprites: { id: sprite } } (sprites are E.sprite data; a color may be 'material:tone', tone deep | sh | base | lt | hi)\n- `E.materialTile(id, o = {})`: a material's pattern as a tile of pixels, for a 3D renderer's texture: E.materialTile(id, { size: 32 (world units square: it repeats every size; a face pattern's whole face), px: 2 (pixels a unit), lid: false (true: as on a face looking up), curved (as on a turned face), band: 2 (the light: 0 dark .. 4 bright) }) -> { w, h, rgba (Uint8ClampedArray, top row first, v up the tile) }; null for an unknown material\n- `E.shapeBox(id, x, y, z = 0, o = {})`: where a placed shape stands, as both engines collide with it: E.shapeBox(id | entry, x, y, z, { facing, scale, flip, params }) -> { x, y (its footprint's middle), z, top, hx, hy (half its footprint, along its own x and y), c, s (the turn's cosine and sine) }; null for an unknown shape. map.solid makes one solid; world.shape gives it a collider\n- `E.shapeInfo(id, o = {})`: what a shape draws, for tools and tests: E.shapeInfo(id, { view: 'iso', facing, scale, mats, params }) -> { w, h, ox, oy, tris, prims (its primitives), parts (each primitive's pixels on screen: 0 is never seen there), colors (distinct), pixels, bounds [x0, y0, z0, x1, y1, z1], rgba }; null for an unknown shape\n- `E.shapeMesh(id, o = {})`: a shape's triangles, for a 3D renderer (the 3D engine's world.shape): E.shapeMesh(id | entry, { params, mats, seg }) -> { groups: [{ mat, key, lid, curved, face, pos, nrm, uv }], bounds [x0, y0, z0, x1, y1, z1] } in the entry's own units and frame (z up, its front +y, its own scale applied: place it with the placement's facing, scale and flip). The same parts as r.shape draws; null for an unknown shape\n- `E.shapeMesh({ seg })`: seg(r): the segments round a radius r (default 8 to 24)\n- `E.shapeMesh() → groups`: each one's mat (the material ready to draw: E.material's), key (its id), lid (true: the faces looking up or down, which some patterns draw their own way), curved (turned or round faces: planks run up them), face (a pattern drawn once per face, the crate's sides and bark's ends: uv runs 0 to 1 across each face), pos, nrm, uv (Float32Arrays: three numbers a corner, three corners a triangle; uv two a corner, world units across the surface)\n- `E.shapeSprite(id, o = {})`: a shape as a sprite (E.sprite's kind: r.sprite, px.sprite, inventories, HUDs, portraits): E.shapeSprite(id, { view: 'iso', facing, size (its scale, 1), mats, params, outline: true | a color | false }). The view is a name or a View, as given (r.shape draws steep views from the characters' angle: E.charView(view) gives that); a bag icon is the item's shape seen at an angle. r.sprite stands it on its feet (foot: [x, y] in the sprite) unless an anchor is given\n\n## 21. Ui\n\n- `new E.Dialog(game, o = {}) → talk`: new E.Dialog(game, { speed, lines, place, color, nameColor, voice, width, bg, border, vars }): a dialog box that types its text; say() opens it, update(dt) and draw(r) run it\n- `talk.choiceAt(i)` †: where choice i is on the screen while the choices show: [x, y] (its middle, for a test's tap), else null\n- `talk.close()`: close it now (and forget pending presses, so the same press doesn't also attack)\n- `talk.draw(r)`: draw it (anywhere in draw: it goes to the overlay)\n- `talk.pageLen`: the letters on the page shown\n- `talk.say(text, o = {})`: open with one string or a list of strings (each starts a new page). opts: { name, onDone, choices, onChoice, portrait: rig (a live close-up of that Humanoid beside the text) or a function (g, x, y, size), portraitSize: 40, portraitOpts: { zoom, turn, bg }, auto: seconds a page shows before it turns by itself, modal: false (play goes on while it shows: radio chatter), place: 'top' | 'middle' | 'bottom', y, alpha, vars: the values {$name} reads, voice: this call's (a sound effect, null, or a function per letter) } choices: ['YES', 'NO'], shown on the last page (picked by the keys, or a tap or click on one); onChoice(i) gets the one picked. update(dt) returns true while a modal box is open: return from your update then, and play waits\n- `talk.typing`: true while the page is still typing\n- `talk.update(dt)`: type and turn pages (confirm finishes the line, then turns the page); returns true while open and modal, so gameplay can wait: if (talk.update(dt)) return;\n- `talk.auto`: seconds each page stays after typing\n- `talk.bg = '#141a3a'`, `talk.border = '#e8e4ff'`: the box's colors\n- `talk.choice = 0`: the choice under the cursor\n- `talk.choices`: the answers offered on the last page, or null\n- `talk.color = '#ffffff'`: the text's color\n- `talk.game`: its game\n- `talk.i = 0`: the page shown\n- `talk.lines = 3`: text lines per page\n- `talk.modal`: false lets play go on (radio chatter)\n- `talk.name`: the speaker's name shown on the box\n- `talk.nameColor = '#ffd36a'`: the speaker's name's color\n- `talk.onChoice`: called with the index picked\n- `talk.onDone`: called when the last page closes\n- `talk.open = false`: true while it shows\n- `talk.pages = []`: the text wrapped into pages of lines\n- `talk.place = 'bottom'`: 'bottom' | 'top' | 'middle'\n- `talk.portrait`: the portrait beside the text (a Humanoid, or a function (g, x, y, size)), or null; pSize: its size in pixels; pOpts: its options ({ zoom, turn, bg })\n- `talk.shown = 0`: letters typed so far on it\n- `talk.speed = 50`: letters typed per second\n- `talk.vars = null`: the values {$name} in the text reads (say's vars first)\n- `talk.voice = 'text'`: the sound effect each letter blips ('text'; null for silence), or a function (letter, index, dialog) called once for each letter as it types (a babble voice: Animalese; dialog.name is the speaker)\n- `talk.width = 0`: the box's width in pixels (320 at most by default, the screen's less 12 at most)\n- `new E.Menu(game, items, o = {}) → menu`: Menu with a cursor (title screens, pause menus, shops, battle commands). const menu = new E.Menu(game, ['START', 'OPTIONS', { label: 'CONTINUE', disabled: !save }], { onPick(i, item) {...}, onCancel() {...} }); update: menu.update(dt) draw: menu.draw(r) opts: { x: 'center' | px, y: 'center' | px, title, box: true, color, pick: '#ffd36a', columns: 1 }\n- `menu.draw(r)`: draw it to the overlay\n- `menu.rowAt(i)` †: entry i's box on the screen, [x, y, w, h] in screen pixels: where a click or tap picks it (a test's feed('down') and feed('up') there); null while it is scrolled out of view\n- `menu.selected`: the entry under the cursor\n- `menu.update()`: move the cursor (up, down; held keys repeat; the mouse over an entry) and pick (confirm, or a click or tap on an entry) or cancel; returns true when it picked or cancelled\n- `menu.active = true`: false stops it reading input\n- `menu.bg = '#141a3a'`, `menu.border = '#e8e4ff'`: the window's colors\n- `menu.box = true`: draw a window round it\n- `menu.color = '#ffffff'`: the entries' color\n- `menu.dim = '#6a6488'`: disabled entries' color\n- `menu.game`: its game\n- `menu.i = 0`: the one under the cursor\n- `menu.input = null` †: the one input it reads (default: every player's, so any player moves and picks; the mouse and taps are player 1's)\n- `menu.items`: the entries (strings, or { label, disabled })\n- `menu.onCancel = null`: called on cancel\n- `menu.onMove = null`: called (i, item) when the cursor moves\n- `menu.onPick = null`: called (i, item) on confirm\n- `menu.pick = '#ffd36a'`: the color of the one under the cursor\n- `menu.rows = 0` †: at most that many entries shown, the list scrolling under its cursor (0: all)\n- `menu.scale = 1` †: its text and rows that many times (2, 3: across a room on a TV)\n- `menu.sound = true`: false silences it\n- `menu.title = null`: a heading, or null\n- `menu.x = 'center'`, `menu.y = 'center'`: where it sits, 'center' or pixels\n- `E.ui`: pixel-drawn interface pieces for r.overlay\n- `E.ui.bar(g, x, y, w, h, frac, color = '#e0463c', o = {})`: a bar (a meter: health, magic, stamina, a boss's): ui.bar(g, x, y, w, h, 0..1, '#e0463c')\n- `E.ui.box(g, x, y, w, h, o = {})`: window frame: ui.box(g, x, y, w, h, { bg, border, shadow, gradient }) bg: one color (drawn as a soft top-to-bottom gradient, like Final Fantasy windows) or [top, bottom] colors. gradient: false for a flat fill.\n- `E.ui.counter(g, x, y, label, value, color = '#ffffff', o = {})`: label + value, e.g. ui.counter(g, 4, 4, 'RUPEES', 12)\n- `E.ui.hearts(g, x, y, hp, max, o = {})`: row of hearts: hp and max in hearts (halves allowed, e.g. 2.5 of 3)\n\n## 21d. Shell\n\n- `new E.Shell(game, o = {}) → shell`: new E.Shell(game, { theme, screens: { name: spec }, settings: { items, onChange(settings, key) }, store: 'shell-settings', meters: .125, standard: true }): an HTML interface over the game (see the section's header)\n- `new E.Shell({ theme })`: CSS variables (ink, paper, accent, muted, danger, line, font: a family, titleFont), and scale: the whole interface that many times (a number, or a function of the window) for a TV across a room; tv: true picks one by the window's width (14 px text at 875 px, up to 30 at 1,875)\n- `new E.Shell({ screens })`: specs ui.show(name) starts from (the pause screen follows game.paused; screens: { pause: false } leaves it out)\n- `new E.Shell({ settings })`: settings.items: the game's own settings, { key, label, type: 'toggle' | 'slider' | 'select', min, max, step, options, value } (kept in game.settings, beside the standard ones)\n- `new E.Shell({ store, meters })`: store: where an older version saved them; meters: meters per world unit for distances\n- `new E.Shell({ standard })`: false leaves out the standard menu (the pause screen's Settings, Controls, Full screen and Quit, and the settings' groups)\n- `shell.back()` †: what cancel does: the screen's own back(), or close it (back to the screen under it; closing the pause screen resumes)\n- `shell.dispose()` †: take its elements away and let go of the game (game.dispose() does it)\n- `shell.hide()` †: close every screen (and resume, when the menu paused the game)\n- `shell.hud(st = {})` †: the HUD, from this step's state (call it every step; a piece left out is hidden, and only what changed is redrawn)\n- `shell.hud({ objective })` †: { step: '01 / 04', title, text, at: { x, y, z }, from: hero } (the distance in meters, and above or below)\n- `shell.hud({ status, place, tip })` †: status: 'UNSEEN' or { text, color }; place: 'The Old Harbour'; tip: 'text' or { title, text }\n- `shell.hud({ bars, counts })` †: bars: [{ label, value, max, kind: 'bar' | 'pips', color }] (health, stamina, a meter); counts: [[label, value]]\n- `shell.hud({ prompt })` †: { key: 'E' (or act: 'interact': its key on the player's device), label, hint, progress: 0..1 }\n- `shell.hud({ keys, buttons })` †: keys: [['WASD', 'Move'], ...] (hidden on touch screens); buttons: [[label, fn or action]] (top right: pause and map where there is no Escape key)\n- `shell.hud({ minimap })` †: { map, at (with facing), markers: [{ x, y, kind: 'dot' | 'diamond' | 'ring' | 'cross', color }], onClick(x, y) (the world point clicked), fog (an E.Fog: unseen cells black, seen ones dim), camera: true (the ground the screen shows) }\n- `shell.screen` †: the name of the open screen ('pause', 'settings', 'controls', ...), or null while the game plays\n- `shell.settings` †: the player's settings: game.settings (the standard ones and the game's own, by key)\n- `shell.show(name, spec = {})` †: open a screen: 'title' | 'pause' | 'settings' | 'help' | 'results' | 'map' or a name of your own, from spec over o.screens[name]. A spec: { kicker, title, text (strings or functions), actions: [[label, fn] or { label, fn, act, primary, disabled }], back (fn for cancel), rows: ['glyphs', 'invertY', '#A heading'] (settings' rows, changed as on the settings screen) }. From another screen it stacks (back() returns); shown again, it updates (the new spec over the old). 'settings' takes its items\n- `shell.show('title')` †: { name, subtitle, meta: [...], footer }\n- `shell.show('help')` †: { keys: [[key, what]], sections: [[heading, text]] }\n- `shell.show('results')` †: { emblem, stats: [[value, label]], medals: [[label, earned]] }\n- `shell.show('map')` †: { map, at, markers (a list or a function), labels: [[text, x, y]], onPick(x, y) }\n- `shell.toast(title, text = '', seconds = 4)` †: a message that slides in at the top for a few seconds\n- `shell.tutorial(title, text = '', seconds = 15)` †: a tutorial card (bottom left, dismissable) for some seconds; tutorial(null) takes it away\n- `shell.root = null` †: game: the game it is over; o: its options; root: its DOM element (null in a headless game)\n\n## 22. Gpu lighting (webgpu)\n\n- `game.gpu.active(view)` †: true when it lights frames in this view (ready, enabled and a ground view)\n- `game.gpu.snapshot()` †: offscreen mode only: read the last frame back as a PNG data URL\n- `game.gpu.status()` †: 'loading' | 'on' | 'off' | 'unavailable'\n- `game.gpu.adapterInfo` †: the GPU's description from the browser (vendor, architecture), or null\n- `game.gpu.ambient` †: the unlit color, [r, g, b] 0..1\n- `game.gpu.bands = 6` †: how many light bands (fewer is more retro)\n- `game.gpu.enabled = true` †: false switches it off for now (and the canvas lighting with it, unless game.lights.enabled)\n- `game.gpu.failed = null` †: why it couldn't start, or null\n- `game.gpu.fallback = true` †: false keeps a frame it can't draw unlit (by default the canvas lighting stands in, game.lights.on)\n- `game.gpu.glow = .22` †: the strength of px.glow surfaces\n- `game.gpu.map = null` †: the TileMap it measures walls on\n- `game.gpu.offscreen = false` †: render into a texture instead of the page (automated tests; read it with snapshot())\n- `game.gpu.onStatus = null` †: called (gpu) when its status changes\n- `game.gpu.ready = false` †: true once the GPU is set up\n- `game.gpu.shadows = true` †: casters block light\n- `game.gpu.skipped` †: never queue more than two frames: a slow GPU drops frames instead of building up lag\n- `game.gpu.spill = 1.1` †: how far light spills past its radius\n- `game.gpu.wrap = .45` †: how far light wraps round wall corners, 0..1\n\n## 23. Sessions\n\n- `E.session` †: records a play session on any device and replays it exactly (this section's header says how)\n- `E.session.frame` †: the frames run since the page loaded\n- `E.session.record(on)` †: turn recording on or off for the next page loads (each load is a session of its own)\n- `E.session.recording` †: true while this page load is recorded\n- `E.session.replaying` †: true while it is a replay\n- `E.session.wanted()` †: true when recording is on for page loads (record(true))\n\n## Module arcade: the coin-op's frame: scores, high-score tables and initials, lives, credits, turns, cards, the attract mode (engine/modules/arcade.js)\n\n- `new E.Arcade(game, o = {}) → arcade`: new E.Arcade(game, { id, lives: 3, extra: [] (the scores that give a life) or every (a life each that many points), credits: null (free play) or the coins a game takes, idle: 20, demo: 30, scores: 6 (the attract mode's seconds), hud: true, colors: [label, number, highlight] })\n- `arcade.add(points, at)`: add points to the player up (at: a world point { x, y, z } where they float up); an extra life at each threshold\n- `arcade.attract(dt)`: the attract mode, each step on the title: 'title', 'demo' (o.idle seconds without input; it lasts o.demo seconds or until endDemo()), 'scores' (o.scores seconds), then 'title' again; any input goes back to 'title'\n- `arcade.card(text, seconds = 2)`: a card in the middle of the screen for seconds ('STAGE 2', 'PLAYER 2, READY!')\n- `arcade.carding`: is a card up? (hold play while it is)\n- `arcade.clear()`: take every card and word down\n- `arcade.coin()`: add a coin (credits on); returns the credits\n- `arcade.continue(seconds = 10, done)`: a continue: CONTINUE? counts down seconds; start, confirm or a tap continues (with the credits it takes): every player's lives back, the scores kept, done(true); at zero, done(false)\n- `arcade.continuing`: is the continue counting down?\n- `arcade.die()`: a life lost: 'again' (lives left), 'next' (the other player's turn) or 'over' (none left for anyone)\n- `arcade.draw(r, box)`: the frame over the game, after its drawing: the HUD (o.hud: 1UP, HI, 2UP in a row) along the top of box [x, y, w, h] (the playfield; the screen when none), the lives along its foot, the cards, DEMO, the continue and the entry\n- `arcade.drawTable(r, mode = '', y = 40, title = 'HIGH SCORES')`: a table as a screen (the attract mode's, or after a game), its title at y: rank, name, score\n- `arcade.endDemo()`: the demo is over (a demo in a scene of its own: call it on the way back): the attract mode shows the scores\n- `arcade.enter(score, mode = '', done)`: open the initials entry for score; done(name) once it is taken and saved\n- `arcade.qualifies(score, mode = '')`: would score make the top ten (never in a demo)?\n- `arcade.say(text, seconds = 1.2)`: a short word under the cards for seconds ('READY!', 'EXTRA LIFE!')\n- `arcade.start(players = 1, o = {})`: begin a game for 1 or 2 players (taking turns), o: { demo: true } for the attract mode's; spends the credits a game takes unless it is free play or a demo. Returns false without them\n- `arcade.table(mode = '')`: the top ten of a mode: [{ name, score }]\n- `arcade.update(dt)`: a step: the cards count down, the entry and the continue read the keys\n- `arcade.wake()`: the attract mode back to the title, its idle clock at zero (as any input does)\n- `arcade.credits`: coins in (null: free play)\n- `arcade.demo = false`: a demo is playing (no table)\n- `arcade.entry = null`: the initials being entered ({ score, mode, name, i }), or null\n- `arcade.game`: the game it serves\n- `arcade.hi = 0`: the high score shown\n- `arcade.id`: the game's name (its tables')\n- `arcade.mode = 'title'`: 'title' | 'play' | 'over'\n- `arcade.o`: the options\n- `arcade.p = null`: the player up\n- `arcade.phase = 'title'`: the attract mode's, 'title' | 'demo' | 'scores'\n- `arcade.players = []`: [{ n, score, lives, save (the game's own: a stage kept while the other plays), next (the score of the next extra life) }]\n- `arcade.wheel = null`: where the letter wheel was last drawn (taps answer to it): { x, y, cw, up, down, ok } in screen pixels: three columns cw wide from x, the up and down bands [y0, y1], the OK button [x, y, w, h]\n\n## Module click: point-and-click adventures: walk areas with holes, depth, hotspots, verbs, an inventory (engine/modules/click.js)\n\n- `new E.Click(game, o = {}) → click` †: new E.Click(game, { rooms, items, rules, hero, ui, story, room, at, on }): see the manual\n- `click.act(verb, target, item = null)` †: do verb on target (a hotspot's id, or an item's: in the inventory), with item (held) or not: the hero walks there first (a hotspot), then the first rule that matches, else the hotspot's own, else a line. Tests and bots call it\n- `click.at(x, y)` †: what is under a room point: an inventory item never; a hotspot (the nearest-drawn), or null\n- `click.busy` †: true while play waits on it: a fade, a line, a talk, a walk\n- `click.draw(r)` †: the room over the whole screen: its backdrop, bodies and things by depth, the lines, the panel (call after the scene)\n- `click.end()` †: the step's pointer stops changing the canvas's cursor (call on leaving the scene)\n- `click.give(id)` †: add an item\n- `click.go(id, at, now)` †: to room id, the hero at at (or the room's start), with a fade (now: none)\n- `click.has(id)` †: is the item held?\n- `click.restore(s)` †: put back what state() gave\n- `click.say(lines, who)` †: the hero says lines (over his head; a line's time by its length); who: anything with x, y and k to speak instead\n- `click.scaleAt(y)` †: a body's scale where its feet are (the room's depth: [yFar, kFar, yNear, kNear])\n- `click.screen(x, y)` †: a room point on the screen: [x, y] in screen pixels (the room scrolled or centred above the panel)\n- `click.state()` †: what a save keeps: the room, the hero's place, the inventory\n- `click.take(id)` †: take an item away\n- `click.update(dt)` †: read the pointer and keys, walk the hero, run the talk and the hero's line; true while play should wait\n- `click.walkTo(x, y, then, run)` †: walk to (x, y) (the nearest point you can stand on), then(pc) when there; run: twice the speed\n- `click.where(id)` †: where a hotspot is on the screen: [x, y] (its middle; an actor's chest), or null (not in this room, or hidden)\n- `click.boxes = []` †: the panel's and the coin's buttons in the last frame ({ id: 'verb:look' | 'icon:hand' | 'item:rope' | 'coin:use' | 'inv:<', x, y, w, h }: screen pixels, for tests and bots)\n- `click.game` †: the game\n- `click.hero` †: the hero: { x, y, rig, speed, face (radians: 0 right), k (its scale), path, room }\n- `click.inv = []` †: the items held, by id\n- `click.item = null` †: the item on the cursor (or null)\n- `click.items` †: the items by id\n- `click.line = null` †: the line being said ({ lines, i, who }, or null)\n- `click.on` †: its events\n- `click.panel = true` †: false hides the verbs and the inventory, keeping their place (a cutscene)\n- `click.room = null` †: the room's id\n- `click.rooms` †: the rooms by id\n- `click.rules` †: the rules (data)\n- `click.story` †: the E.Story that talks (or null)\n- `click.ui` †: 'verbs' | 'coin' | 'two'\n- `click.verb = 'walk'` †: the verb chosen ('walk' when none)\n- `new E.Walkbox(outline, holes = []) → walkbox` †: new E.Walkbox(outline, holes): polygons of [x, y] (room pixels); holes are places inside it no one stands\n- `walkbox.inside(x, y)` †: is (x, y) in the area and in none of its holes?\n- `walkbox.nearest(x, y)` †: the point you can stand on nearest (x, y): itself when inside, else the nearest on an edge (a pixel in)\n- `walkbox.path(a, b)` †: the shortest way from a to b (b moved to the nearest point inside): [a, ..., b], round the holes\n- `walkbox.sees(a, b)` †: can one walk straight from a to b? (it crosses no edge, and its middle is inside)\n- `walkbox.edges = []` †: every side of both, as [a, b]\n- `walkbox.holes` †: its holes\n- `walkbox.nodes = []` †: the corners a shortest path turns at: the outline's inward-pointing ones and every hole's outward one, set a pixel off\n- `walkbox.outline` †: the area's edge\n\n## Module drive: cars on the ground plane, a race round a track, the drivers who race it (engine/modules/drive.js)\n\n- `new E.Car(o = {}) → car`: new E.Car({ x, y, angle, top, accel, brake, turn, grip, drift, kart, auto, surfaces, ground }): a car (the manual)\n- `E.Car.collide(cars)`: bump every pair of cars that touch apart (equal weights, a little bounce)\n- `car.boost(sec = .8, power = .4)`: a boost: seconds of it, power (.4: top speed up by that share)\n- `car.spinOut(sec = 1)`: spin out (a shot, a hazard): seconds without control, most of its speed lost\n- `car.update(dt, map, c = {})`: one step: steering, engine, grip, drift, the surface, then E.Body's walls and hop. c: an input or an intent\n- `car.airT = 0`: a hop's seconds in the air\n- `car.angle`: its heading\n- `car.boostT = 0`: a boost's seconds left\n- `car.bumped = false`: it hit a wall or a car this step\n- `car.charge = 0`: a kart's drift boost building (1 blue, 2 orange)\n- `car.drifting = 0`: -1 left, 1 right, 0 not\n- `car.o`: its options\n- `car.offroad = false`: on a surface that slows it\n- `car.onGround = false`, `car.vz`: E.Body's (a hop leaves the ground)\n- `car.slide = 0`: sideways\n- `car.speed = 0`: along it (units a second, - backing up)\n- `car.spinT = 0`: a spin-out's\n- `car.surface = null`: the floor tag under it\n- `car.vx`, `car.vy`: its speed on the ground (E.Body's): along its heading and the slide\n- `new E.Race(o = {}) → race`: new E.Race({ path, laps, checkpoints, countdown, width, items }): a race round a closed path (the manual)\n- `race.add(car, o = {})`: a racer: its car, o.name, o.input (a player's controls) or o.ai ({ skill, rubber, lane }); returns it\n- `race.box(x, y)`: an item box at (x, y): a racer that drives through it gets an item (when it holds none)\n- `race.drive(R, dt)`: a racer's way round: the intent race.update gives an AI racer's car (look ahead along the line, bends, overtakes, unsticking, the rubber band)\n- `race.grid(n)`: the start: n places behind the line, two by two, facing along it: [{ x, y, angle }]\n- `race.hud(r, R, o = {})`: the standard HUD for a racer, in its viewport: place, lap, time, item, the countdown, WRONG WAY, FINISH (o.color; o.right: the x its right-hand text ends before, clear of a corner button)\n- `race.update(dt, map)`: one step: the countdown, every car, bumps, items, progress, laps, wrong way, places, the finish\n- `race.use(R)`: fire a racer's item: boost (now), shot (ahead along its heading), hazard (behind it)\n- `race.boxes = []`, `race.hazards = []`, `race.shots = []`: the items on the track\n- `race.go = false`: racing (the countdown over)\n- `race.o`: its options\n- `race.over = false`: every racer finished\n- `race.path`: the racing line\n- `race.racers = []`: every racer, in the order added\n- `race.results = []`: the racers as they finished\n- `race.t`: seconds since GO (- before it)\n\n## Module fight: one-on-one fighting: fighters with frame data, guards, throws, specials, a meter; rounds; a CPU; training (engine/modules/fight.js)\n\n- `E.FIGHT_KEYS: up, down, left, right, lp, hp, lk, hk, start, pause, confirm, cancel` †: the fighting preset (a scene's input): the moves and menu keys of E.Input.DEFAULT, light and heavy punch (U, I; a pad's X, Y), light and heavy kick (J, K; A, B)\n- `new E.FightAI(o = {}) → fightai` †: new E.FightAI({ level: 1..8 (4), seed }): a CPU for a fighter\n- `fightai.intent(f, foe, dt)` †: what it does this step: an intent for fighter f.update(dt, intent) ({ x, y: -1..1 on screen, press, do })\n- `fightai.level` †: how well it fights (1 to 8)\n- `fightai.react` †: seconds before it sees what its foe does\n- `fightai.rng` †: its own randomness (the seed's: the same match each time)\n- `new E.FightSelect(game, o = {}) → fightselect` †: new E.FightSelect(game, { roster: [{ id, name, rig, about }], inputs: [p1, p2 or null], cpu: [false, true], seed, onDone: ids => {} }): a character select\n- `fightselect.draw(r)` †: draw the roster, the cursors and each side's fighter large\n- `fightselect.update(dt)` †: move the cursors, take the picks; onDone(ids) once both have one\n- `fightselect.at` †: each side's cursor\n- `fightselect.cpu` †: which sides the CPU picks for\n- `fightselect.game` †: the game it runs in\n- `fightselect.inputs` †: each side's E.Input\n- `fightselect.o` †: its options\n- `fightselect.picks = [null, null]` †: each side's id once chosen\n- `fightselect.rects = null` †: where each portrait was drawn ([x, y, w, h] in screen pixels: a tap there picks it)\n- `fightselect.rigs` †: each fighter's portrait rig\n- `fightselect.roster` †: the fighters to pick from\n- `new E.FightStage(x0 = 0, x1 = 520) → fightstage` †: new E.FightStage(x0, x1): a flat floor at z 0\n- `fightstage.cellsTouching()` †: no cells either\n- `fightstage.move(b, dt)` †: move a body one step: the floor and the walls stop it\n- `fightstage.touching()` †: no ladders on a stage\n- `fightstage.x0` †: its left wall\n- `fightstage.x1` †: its right wall\n- `new E.Fighter(game, o = {}) → fighter` †: new E.Fighter(game, { name, x, side: 1 (faces right), rig: Humanoid options (or a Humanoid), life: 1000, walk: 75, back: 60, jump: 390, jumpX: 90, moves, throwBtn: 'hp', throwRange, throwDmg: 120, input })\n- `E.Fighter.exchange(a, b)` †: settle one step between two fighters (E.Match calls it after both update): hits (both may land: a trade), shots against each other and against the fighters, push boxes apart\n- `fighter.draw(r)` †: draw it (and its shots) in the scene: r.actor with its rig\n- `fighter.drawBoxes(r)` †: the boxes drawn (training): hurt green, hit red, push blue\n- `fighter.free` †: true while it can act: on the ground, not stunned, down, thrown or out\n- `fighter.reset(x, side = this.facing)` †: back to the start of a round: at x, facing side (1 right), full life (the meter stays)\n- `fighter.update(dt, ctl = this.input)` †: move one step (once a step, 120 times a second): ctl is its E.Input (this.input by default) or a CPU's intent\n- `fighter.x` †: x and z: the body's feet\n- `fighter.z` †: its feet's height\n- `fighter.act = null` †: the move playing ({ m, hit: it connected, blocked }), or null\n- `fighter.air = false` †: flying from a hit\n- `fighter.blocking = false` †: guarding (holding back as an attack comes, or in block-stun)\n- `fighter.body` †: its E.Platformer (x, z: its feet; w, h: its push box)\n- `fighter.boxes` †: this step's boxes ({ hurt: [head, body, legs], hit: the striking fist or foot's, or null, push }: each { x, z, w, h }, x its left, z its bottom)\n- `fighter.combo = 0` †: the hits it has taken in a row\n- `fighter.crouch = false` †: crouching\n- `fighter.down = 0` †: seconds lying down\n- `fighter.facing` †: 1 right, -1 left\n- `fighter.foe = null` †: the other fighter (E.Match sets it)\n- `fighter.game` †: the game it fights in\n- `fighter.input` †: its E.Input, or null (a CPU passes its intent to update)\n- `fighter.inv = 0` †: seconds invulnerable\n- `fighter.juggle = 0` †: hits taken in the air\n- `fighter.kind = null` †: 'hit' | 'block' (which stun)\n- `fighter.ko = false` †: knocked out\n- `fighter.last = null` †: the frame data of its last move to connect ({ id, startup, active, recovery, adv, on: 'hit' | 'block' })\n- `fighter.life` †: what it has left (o.life at the start)\n- `fighter.meter` †: 0 to 100 a bar (bars: o.bars, 1), kept between rounds\n- `fighter.moves` †: { id: spec }, each spec with its E.Attack (atk) and its frame data (frames: [startup, active, recovery])\n- `fighter.name` †: its name on the HUD\n- `fighter.o` †: its options, with the defaults filled in\n- `fighter.rig` †: its E.Humanoid (drawn and posed from its state every step)\n- `fighter.rise = 0` †: seconds getting up\n- `fighter.shots` †: its projectiles (E.Bullets, the side plane)\n- `fighter.stun = 0` †: seconds of hit- or block-stun left\n- `fighter.thrown = null` †: a throw holding it ({ by, t, dur, dir, dmg }), or null\n- `fighter.win = false` †: in its win pose\n- `new E.Match(game, o = {}) → match` †: new E.Match(game, { fighters: [a, b], ai: [null, null] (E.FightAI each, or null for its input), inputs: [game. input, null], rounds: 2 (wins needed), time: 99, x0: 0, x1: 520, span: 250 (how far apart they may be), camera: true (the game's camera follows them), training: false | { dummy }, onEnd: winner => {} })\n- `match.draw(r)` †: draw the fighters, their shots and the HUD; in training the boxes, the frame data and player 1's inputs\n- `match.startRound()` †: the next round: both back at the middle, full life\n- `match.update(dt)` †: one step: the fighters, their exchange, the clock, the calls and the rounds\n- `match.x` †: the stage's middle\n- `match.ai` †: each one's CPU (E.FightAI), or null\n- `match.call` †: the words on the screen (ROUND 1, FIGHT!, K.O., TIME, PERFECT), or null\n- `match.clock` †: the round's seconds left\n- `match.f` †: the two fighters\n- `match.game` †: the game it runs in\n- `match.inputs` †: each one's E.Input when no CPU plays it\n- `match.o` †: its options, with the defaults filled in\n- `match.phase` †: 'intro' | 'fight' | 'ko' | 'win' | 'over'\n- `match.round = 0` †: the round's number\n- `match.stage` †: the floor and walls they stand in (E.FightStage)\n- `match.winner = null` †: 0 or 1 once known (a round's, then the match's), or null\n- `match.wins = [0, 0]` †: the rounds each has won\n\n## Module gen: generated maps: rooms and corridors, BSP, caves, mazes and scatter, from a seed (engine/modules/gen.js)\n\n- `E.gen`: the map generators (each from a seed: the same rows every time)\n- `E.gen.bsp(w, h, o = {})`: binary space partition: halves split again down to o.min (7) cells, a room in each leaf, sisters joined\n- `E.gen.cave(w, h, o = {})`: caves by cellular automata: o.fill (.45) of the cells rock, o.steps (4) rounds of the 4-5 rule, then the biggest open region alone\n- `E.gen.mark(level, ch, n, o = {})`: put character ch on n floor cells of a level (from a generator), at least o.far (4) steps from its start and o.near steps from each other (0), from o.seed; returns the cells and adds them to level.marks\n- `E.gen.maze(w, h, o = {})`: a maze on the odd cells: o.algo 'backtrack' (long halls) or 'prim' (short branches); o.braid 0..1 opens that share of dead ends into loops\n- `E.gen.open(rows)`: every floor cell ('.') of rows, as [x, y]\n- `E.gen.rooms(w, h, o = {})`: rooms placed apart and joined in order by bent corridors: { rows, rooms, start, end, marks }. o: { seed, rooms: 9, min: 4, max: 9 (a room's side, cells) }\n- `E.gen.scatter(w, h, o = {})`: Poisson disc points at least o.r (3) apart within w x h, o.k (30) tries each, where o.ok(x, y) allows (Bridson's): [[x, y], ...] in cells, not whole numbers\n\n## Module grid: square and hex grids: paths, ranges, lines, fields of view, fog, groups, movers and turns (engine/modules/grid.js)\n\n- `new E.Fog(grid) → fog`: new E.Fog(grid): every cell unseen; update() with the viewers each turn\n- `fog.draw(r, T = 16, o = {})`: draw it over the world: unseen cells black, seen ones dimmed (o.dim: .55, o.color), walls' faces covered too; call after the world and before the HUD. T: world units a cell; o.height(cx, cy): a cell's top (a TileMap's height)\n- `fog.seen(c)`: has [cx, cy] been seen (now or before)?\n- `fog.update(viewers)`: what the viewers see now ([{ cx, cy, r }]: each one's field of view) becomes visible; what was visible stays seen\n- `fog.visible(c)`: is [cx, cy] in sight now?\n- `fog.grid`: what it covers\n- `fog.state`: per cell 0 unseen, 1 seen before, 2 visible now (row by row)\n- `new E.Grid(w, h, o = {}) → grid`: new E.Grid(w, h, { hex: false | 'pointy' | 'flat', walkable(cx, cy), cost(cx, cy), height(cx, cy), opaque(cx, cy), diagonal }): w x h cells, every one walkable at cost 1 and height 0 unless the functions say otherwise\n- `E.Grid.from(map, o = {})`: a grid from a TileMap: its walkable cells (o.tops: true stands on the walls' tops at their heights, for tactics)\n- `grid.center(c, T = 16)`: a cell's centre in world units, T a cell across (square: T wide; hex: T across its flats)\n- `grid.cost(c)`: what entering [cx, cy] costs (1 by default)\n- `grid.dist(a, b)`: steps from a to b: Manhattan, Chebyshev (diagonal) or hex steps\n- `grid.face(a, b)`: the way from a looks to b, one of four [dx, dy] (the longer axis; x when they tie): a unit turning to its target\n- `grid.flood(c, same)`: the cells joined to c through neighbours for which same(cx, cy) is true (c's too): a flood fill\n- `grid.fov(c, radius = 8)`: what c sees within radius: a Set of 'cx,cy' (symmetric shadowcasting, Albert Ford's: a sees b exactly when b sees a; walls at the edge of what it sees are in it; hex grids: a line to each cell)\n- `grid.groups(key)`: the groups of neighbouring cells with the same key(cx, cy) (null or undefined: none): matches, Puyo chains, regions. [[cells]], biggest first\n- `grid.height(c)`: a cell's height (0 by default; E.Grid.from(map, { tops: true }): the wall's top)\n- `grid.inside(c)`: is [cx, cy] on the grid?\n- `grid.line(a, b)`: the cells on a line from a to b, both ends included (Bresenham; hex: one step at a time)\n- `grid.neighbours(c)`: the cells next to c on the grid: 4, 8 (diagonal) or 6 (hex), walkable or not\n- `grid.opaque(c)`: does [cx, cy] block sight? (not walkable, by default)\n- `grid.path(a, b, o = {})`: the cheapest way from a to b, [a, ..., b], or [] (A*; ties broken the same way every time). o: { blocked(cx, cy): cells taken (other units), climb, drop (the most a step rises and falls), ends: b may be unwalkable (a target), max: give up past this cost }\n- `grid.reach(a, budget, o = {})`: every cell within budget of a: { cells: [[cx, cy, cost]], has(c), cost(c) (or Infinity), path(c) } (Dijkstra: move ranges). o: { blocked, climb, drop } as path's, and pass(cx, cy): cells it may cross but not stop on (allies)\n- `grid.sees(a, b)`: can a see b: no opaque cell on the line between them (the ends themselves may be)\n- `grid.shape(c, kind = 'diamond', n = 1, dir = [1, 0])`: the cells a shape of effect covers round c (square grids; on the grid only): 'cross' (n each way), 'diamond' (n steps: dist), 'square' (n each way, corners too), 'ring' (exactly n steps), 'line' (n cells from c along dir [dx, dy], not c), 'cone' (n rows along dir, each a cell wider each side)\n- `grid.side(at, facing, from)`: where a blow from cell from lands on a unit at cell at facing [dx, dy]: 'front', 'side' or 'back' (within 45 degrees of its facing, of behind it, or between: a diagonal is a side)\n- `grid.threat(units, o = {})`: what a side can strike on its next turn: units ([{ cx, cy, move, range: [min, max] or n (1), climb, drop }]) each moving within its reach (o.blocked: cells it can't enter) and striking within range of where it stops. { cells: [[cx, cy, n]], count(c), by(c) -> [units] }: a threat map, for an AI's safe cells and a player's warning tint\n- `grid.toCell(x, y, T = 16)`: the cell under world point (x, y), T a cell across (center's inverse)\n- `grid.walk(c)`: can [cx, cy] be walked (on the grid, and walkable)?\n- `grid.diagonal`: square moves of 8 directions\n- `grid.h`, `grid.w`: its size in cells\n- `grid.hex`: false, 'pointy' (offset rows) or 'flat' (offset columns)\n- `new E.GridMover(o = {}) → gridmover`: new E.GridMover({ cx, cy, speed: 6 (cells a second) })\n- `gridmover.place(cx, cy)`: put it on a cell at once, still\n- `gridmover.update(dt, grid, want, o = {})`: a step of dt: want ([dx, dy] or null) is kept until it can be taken. o: { blocked(cx, cy): true where it can't go, push(cx, cy, dx, dy): true when what is in the way moved (a crate), keep: true goes on in the same direction (a maze) }. Returns true when it reached a cell this step\n- `gridmover.arrived = false`: true the step it reaches a cell\n- `gridmover.cx`, `gridmover.cy`: the cell it is in (or leaving)\n- `gridmover.dir = [0, 0]`: [dx, dy] it is moving (or last moved)\n- `gridmover.moving = false`: between cells\n- `gridmover.speed`: cells a second\n- `gridmover.x`, `gridmover.y`: its smooth position in cells (draw it there)\n- `new E.Turns(actors = [], o = {}) → turns`: new E.Turns(actors, { mode: 'speed' | 'ct' | 'phase' | 'atb', sides: ['hero', 'foe'] (phase), fill: 1 (atb: gauge per second at speed 100) }). An actor is any object with speed (100: normal) and, in phase mode, side\n- `turns.add(a)`: add an actor (it starts with an empty gauge)\n- `turns.done(a, cost = 100)`: after an actor acted (ct: cost 100 for a move and an action, 80 or 60 for less; atb: its gauge empties)\n- `turns.gauge(a)`: an actor's gauge 0..1 (atb and ct: how full; speed: its energy)\n- `turns.next()`: the actor whose turn it is (speed: its energy spent; ct: call done(actor, cost) after it acts; phase: each of the side once, then the next side's)\n- `turns.order(n = 5)`: who acts next, n of them, without changing anything (speed and ct: the order ahead; phase: the rest of this side)\n- `turns.ready()`: atb: the actors whose gauges are full, in the order they were added\n- `turns.remove(a)`: take an actor out (it died, it left)\n- `turns.update(dt)`: atb: fill the gauges on game time (dt seconds)\n- `turns.actors`: in the order they were added (ties go to the earlier)\n- `turns.fill`: an atb gauge's fill a second at speed 100\n- `turns.mode`: 'speed', 'ct', 'phase' or 'atb'\n- `turns.phase = null`: the side acting now (phase mode)\n- `turns.sides`: the sides in their order (phase; null: as the actors have them)\n- `E.tint(r, cells, color, alpha = .4, T = 16, height = null)`: tints on cells' tops: E.tint(r, cells ([[cx, cy], ...]), color, alpha = .4, T = 16, height(cx, cy) = 0): a move range, a path, an attack's reach, drawn on the floor (decals) where height is 0; on a raised top (a TileMap wall's) it sorts with the map: over that top, under whoever stands on it, behind what is in front\n\n## Module mode7: a perspective floor drawn a row at a time, a sky that turns with it, everything on it scaled by its distance (engine/modules/mode7.js)\n\n- `new E.Mode7(o = {}) → mode7`: new E.Mode7({ floor, size, outside, wrap, sky, fog, far }): a Mode 7 view (the manual above)\n- `mode7.add(x, y, z, fn)`: gather a billboard: fn(g, sx, sy, k) draws it with its foot at (sx, sy), k screen pixels per unit\n- `mode7.begin(rect = {}, cam = this.cam)`: set up the projection for a camera (default this.cam) and a rectangle of the screen ({ W, H, x, y, cx, cy }: its size, its top left and its centre; focal: screen pixels per unit at a distance of one, instead of the fov)\n- `mode7.chase(b, o = {})`: the camera behind a body each step: back units behind it, h high, looking along its angle (o.angle to set one)\n- `mode7.draw(r, cam = this.cam)`: draw the view as an overlay over r's frame (in a split screen, r.viewport's part): sky, floor, fog, then the billboards far to near (gathered with add, rig, sprite; they are used up)\n- `mode7.drawFloor(g, vp)`: draw the floor below the horizon in the rectangle ({ x, y, w, h }), a row at a time, and the fog over it\n- `mode7.drawSky(g, r, vp)`: the sky above the horizon: a Backdrop turned with the heading, or a gradient of two colors\n- `mode7.fullRes(game, on)`: draw at the screen's own pixels (on, called each step: sharper, slower) or the game's again (off); returns the pixel size the game had (1 when it had no other)\n- `mode7.project(x, y, z = 0)`: a world point -> [screen x, screen y, screen pixels per unit there], or null at or behind the camera\n- `mode7.refresh()`: forget the floor's baked texture (after changing the map or the function: it bakes again)\n- `mode7.rig(rig, x, y, z = 0)`: gather a rig, drawn by the engine at the size its distance gives, from the camera's side (it faces rig.facing)\n- `mode7.rowDepth(sy)`: a screen row's floor distance ahead of the camera, or 0 at or above the horizon\n- `mode7.shape(id, x, y, z = 0, o = {})`: gather one of the engine's shapes (r.shape's: 'barrel', 'tree', ...; the full engine), seen from the camera's side at its distance's size: o.facing (radians), o.size (1), o.params and o.mats (as r.shape's: a door's angle, a crate's wood)\n- `mode7.sprite(spr, x, y, z = 0, o = {})`: gather a sprite (E.sprite, or E.anim's frames at o.t, the game's time by default), its foot at the point: o.scale (world units a sprite pixel, 1), o.flip\n- `mode7.toFloor(sx, sy)`: a screen point -> the floor point under it [x, y], or null above the horizon\n- `mode7.view(k)`: the engine view a rig is drawn with at k pixels per unit, seen from the camera's heading (sixteenths, reused) and pitch (whole degrees: a bobbing camera makes no new view each frame)\n- `mode7.cam`: the camera ({ x, y, h, angle, pitch, fov })\n- `mode7.horizon = 0`: the screen row of the horizon in the last drawing\n- `mode7.items = []`: the billboards gathered for the next draw\n- `mode7.o`: its options\n\n## Module physics: 2D rigid bodies: circles, boxes, polygons, segments; fast balls that never pass through (engine/modules/physics.js)\n\n- `new E.Physics(o = {}) → physics`: new E.Physics({ gravity, substeps, iterations, sleep, on, cell }): a world of bodies (see the manual)\n- `physics.add(d)`: a body from its definition (see the manual)\n- `physics.arc(x, y, r, a0, a1, o = {})`: a curved rail: a chain round (x, y) at radius r from angle a0 to a1 (radians; o.n segments)\n- `physics.box(x, y, w, h, o)`: a box w x h about its middle (dynamic unless o.type says)\n- `physics.bumper(x, y, r, o = {})`: a bumper: a static round post that kicks a ball away (kick: pixels a second)\n- `physics.chain(points, o = {})`: static segments through the points (o.closed: back to the first); returns them\n- `physics.circle(x, y, r, o)`: a circle (dynamic unless o.type says)\n- `physics.draw(g, to = (x, y) => [x, y], color = '#7cf2a0')`: outline every body into g (to(x, y) -> [sx, sy]: the plane on the screen; its identity by default)\n- `physics.flipper(x, y, o = {})`: a flipper: a kinematic tapered bar from its pivot (x, y); flip(on) swings it up or back (see the manual)\n- `physics.hash()`: a number for the world now: every body's place, angle and speed (a proof's hash)\n- `physics.kicker(x, y, o = {})`: a kicker: a sensor that catches a ball, holds it, then fires it (a saucer, a kickback); k.catch.armed\n- `physics.poly(x, y, points, o)`: a convex polygon, its points round x, y (dynamic unless o.type says)\n- `physics.query(x, y)`: the bodies over a point\n- `physics.ray(a, b, mask = 0xffff)`: the nearest body along the line from a to b (sensors aside; mask: the layers it sees): { body, x, y, t } or null\n- `physics.remove(b)`: take a body out\n- `physics.segment(a, b, o)`: a static segment from a to b (o.r: its thickness's half)\n- `physics.slingshot(a, b, o = {})`: a slingshot: a static segment that kicks a ball off it\n- `physics.step(dt)`: advance by dt seconds (from the game's fixed step), in substeps\n- `physics.wake()`: wake every body (after a change to gravity, say)\n- `physics.bodies = []`: every body in the order added\n- `physics.contacts = []`: the last substep's ({ a, b, nx, ny, pts, fresh })\n- `physics.gravity`: [gx, gy], pixels a second a second\n- `physics.iterations`: the solver's passes\n- `physics.on`: its events\n- `physics.sleep`: bodies at rest sleep\n- `physics.substeps`: steps per step(dt)\n\n## Module rhythm: charts on a song's steps, judged on the music clock: combos, grades, holds, a highway (engine/modules/rhythm.js)\n\n- `new E.Rhythm(game, o = {}) → rhythm` †: new E.Rhythm(game, { song, chart, lanes, windows, life, lead, voice, on }): see the manual\n- `rhythm.accuracy` †: how well so far, 0..1: a perfect counts whole, a great .7, a good .4\n- `rhythm.ahead(call = false)` †: the notes to show now: [{ note, dt, dtEnd }] (seconds to the note's time and to its end; a little past the line for the missed, the held while held); call: the calls instead\n- `rhythm.draw(r, o = {})` †: draw the highway on the overlay (see the manual)\n- `rhythm.grade` †: the grade of the accuracy: 'S' (95%), 'A' (85), 'B' (70), 'C' (50), 'D'\n- `rhythm.hit(lane)` †: a press in a lane: judged against the nearest note there within the good window (a hold starts held)\n- `rhythm.release(lane)` †: a lane let go: a hold let go before its end (less the good window) drops\n- `rhythm.reset()` †: back to the start: no judgments, a full combo of nothing, life as given\n- `rhythm.start()` †: play the song from its start (synced: it pauses with the game) and judge from here\n- `rhythm.total` †: the notes to play (not the calls)\n- `rhythm.update()` †: each step, after the input: the lanes' actions pressed and let go, calls sounded, notes missed, holds kept, the end\n- `rhythm.combo = 0` †: the notes hit in a row\n- `rhythm.counts` †: { perfect, great, good, miss }\n- `rhythm.done = false` †: the song is over, or failed\n- `rhythm.down` †: each lane held now\n- `rhythm.failed = false` †: the life ran out\n- `rhythm.game` †: its game\n- `rhythm.lanes` †: the actions, one a lane\n- `rhythm.last = null` †: the last judgment { j, lane, ms, t }\n- `rhythm.lead` †: seconds of notes shown ahead\n- `rhythm.life` †: the gauge 0..1\n- `rhythm.maxCombo = 0` †: the longest combo\n- `rhythm.notes` †: every note of the chart in time order ({ lane, step, end, call, j: its judgment or null, ms })\n- `rhythm.on` †: { judge, fail, done }\n- `rhythm.score = 0` †: the points\n- `rhythm.song` †: the song charted\n- `rhythm.started = false` †: start() was called\n- `rhythm.voice` †: the calls' sound\n- `rhythm.windows` †: { perfect, great, good } in ms either side of a note\n\n## Module road: the pseudo-3D road: segments with curves and hills, lanes, rumble strips, roadside scenery, tunnels (engine/modules/road.js)\n\n- `new E.Road(o = {}) → road`: new E.Road({ segment, width, lanes, rumble, draw, fov, height, colors, fog, sky }): a road (the manual above)\n- `road.add(n, o = {})`: n segments more, bending (curve) and climbing (hill: units up, - down), eased in and out; o.tunnel, o.colors\n- `road.ahead(h = this.o.height, r)`: how far past the camera the player's car stands when drawn at the screen's bottom, for a camera h over the road; square, that depends on the screen's shape: give r (draw's), and draw the camera that far behind the car (keep the car's own place along the road as it was: the game plays the same on any screen)\n- `road.curveAt(z)`: how hard the road bends at a distance\n- `road.draw(r, v = {})`: draw the road over r's frame (an overlay; r.viewport's part in a split screen): { z, x, h, turn, cars }\n- `road.hits(z, x, w)`: the scenery a body w half roads wide at (z, x) touches\n- `road.place(z, offset, what, scale = 8)`: scenery at distance z, offset across: what = an E.sprite or E.anim (scale: world units a pixel, 8) or { draw(g, sx, sy, scale), w }\n- `road.project(z, x, y = 0)`: a point (distance z, across x in half roads, height y over the road) -> [sx, sy, scale] for the camera last drawn, or null behind it\n- `road.seg(z)`: the segment at a distance (it wraps)\n- `road.yAt(z)`: the road's height at a distance\n- `road.depth`: the camera's depth (1 / tan(fov / 2): a unit that far away fills half the screen's width)\n- `road.length = 0`: the lap in world units\n- `road.o`: its options (colors over the defaults)\n- `road.segs = []`: its segments ({ i, z, y0, y1, curve, tunnel, colors, things })\n\n## Module rooms: a world of rooms joined at their edges, the map of what was explored, gates and saves (engine/modules/rooms.js)\n\n- `new E.World(game, o = {}) → world`: new E.World(game, { id, screen: [20, 12], view: 'side' | 'top', legend, types, rooms, start, transition: 'slide', make }): a world of rooms (the manual above)\n- `world.cells()`: every cell of the world grid that a room holds: [{ gx, gy, room, seen }]\n- `world.clear()`: forget the save\n- `world.draw(r)`: draw the room's map (the kit draws its actors after)\n- `world.drawMap(g, x, y, cell = 6, o = {})`: draw the explored map with its top-left cell at x, y, each cell a square of cell pixels: the seen cells (o.all: every room's), walls where rooms end with gaps at their doors, save rooms S and the boss's B, the hero's cell blinking; o: { all, color, wall, save, boss }\n- `world.drawOver(r)`: draw the transition over everything (last): the old screen sliding away, or a fade through black\n- `world.enter(id)`: go to a room by id (the camera's bounds become its map's); returns the room\n- `world.give(name)`: an ability or a key got ('dash', 'key:red'): its gates open in every room\n- `world.has(name)`: true once it has an ability or a key\n- `world.load()`: go back to the save (its room entered, what was got and seen restored): { room, x, z, got, seen, extra }, or null\n- `world.map`: the room's map\n- `world.roomAt(gx, gy)`: the room that holds world cell (gx, gy), or null\n- `world.save(b, extra)`: keep a save: the room, the hero's place, what was got and seen, and extra (anything JSON)\n- `world.shellMap()`: the explored map for E.Shell's minimap: game.shell.hud({ minimap: world.shellMap() }) (a cell a square, the save and boss rooms marked, the hero's cell)\n- `world.update(dt, b)`: each step, after the hero moves: the cell it is in marked seen; off an edge into the next room, the room changes ('enter' that step), off one with no room past it, it stops as at a wall; the transition plays on\n- `world.defs`: the rooms' definitions by id\n- `world.game`: its game\n- `world.got`: the abilities and keys it has\n- `world.grid`: the world grid's extent in cells { x0, y0, w, h }\n- `world.here = null`: its cell [gx, gy]\n- `world.id`: its name (its saves')\n- `world.moving = false`: a transition is playing\n- `world.o`: its options\n- `world.room = null`: the room it is in\n- `world.rooms`: every room by id: { id, def, map, at, w, h } (its size in screens)\n- `world.seen`: the world grid's cells the hero has been in ('gx,gy')\n\n## Module rpg: items, inventories, equipment, shops, a party's stats and levels, status, damage, encounters (engine/modules/rpg.js)\n\n- `new E.RPG(o = {}) → rpg`: new E.RPG({ items, monsters, status, curve }): the rules of a role-playing game (the manual above)\n- `rpg.card(g, x, y, m, o = {})`: a member's card: o.portrait (a Humanoid) beside its name, level, HP and MP bars, its stats and effects; → { w, h }\n- `rpg.damage(a, b, o = {})`: a hit: { hp, crit } (a's attack, or its magic with kind 'mag', less half b's defense or magic; a tenth either way; a critical hit, physical only, ignores armor; guard halves it)\n- `rpg.encounters(zones, o)`: random encounters by zone: { zone: { every: [min, max], troops: [[weight, ids]] } }\n- `rpg.icon(id)`: item id's icon: its own sprite, or one for its kind (a flask red for HP, blue for MP)\n- `rpg.inventory(o)`: a bag: { slots: 32 } or { grid: [8, 5] }\n- `rpg.item(id)`: item id's definition, or null\n- `rpg.list(g, x, y, w, rows, o = {})`: a window of rows ({ label, note, icon, off }) with a cursor; o: { cursor, title, top (the first row shown), show (rows shown: 8), bg, rowH }. → { x, y, w, h, row(px, py) }: the row index at a screen point, or -1\n- `rpg.member(o)`: a party member: { name, level, stats, growth, equip, data }\n- `rpg.party(members, o)`: a party: its members and { gold, inv }\n- `rpg.restore(s)`: the party a save held (party.state())\n- `rpg.shop(ids, o)`: a shop selling these item ids ({ sell: .5 }: what it pays back)\n- `rpg.curve`: curve(lv): the experience level lv needs in all (15 x (lv - 1) ^ 2.4 by default)\n- `rpg.items`: the items by id\n- `rpg.monsters`: the monsters by id\n- `rpg.status`: the status effects by name (the defaults and yours)\n\n## Module songs: the songbook: the kits' best songs and more, and jingles, tagged by mood (engine/modules/songs.js)\n\n- `E.Songbook`: the songbook: songs by id, their moods, get(id), find(mood) (see the manual)\n- `E.Songbook.find(mood)`: the ids of the songs of a mood, in the order listed\n- `E.Songbook.get(id)`: a song by id (playable: game.audio.music(E.Songbook.get('town'))), or undefined\n- `E.Songbook.moods`: every mood a song has, in the order of the manual\n- `E.Songbook.songs`: every song by id ({ title, mood: [...], bpm, steps, tracks, loop }; the core's own songs are E.songs')\n\n## Module story: dialogue as data, flags and values, a quest log and emote balloons (engine/modules/story.js)\n\n- `new E.Story(game, o = {}) → story`: new E.Story(game, { people, nodes, vars, on, dialog }): the talk of a game (the manual above)\n- `story.done(id)`: finish a quest (taken now if it wasn't)\n- `story.draw(r)`: draw the balloons over their targets and the box (anywhere in draw: both go to the overlay)\n- `story.emote(target, kind = '!', seconds = 1.5)`: a balloon over target (x, y, z; h: its height) for seconds: '!', '?', 'heart', 'dots', 'note'\n- `story.get(key)`: a flag or value (key may be a dotted path into vars)\n- `story.quest(id, title = id, text = '')`: take a quest (or change its title and text): it joins the log\n- `story.restore(s)`: put back what state() gave (a load)\n- `story.resume()`: after an event with wait: true, go on (to the choice's goto, or the next step)\n- `story.run(id, o = {})`: start node id (stopping any other); o.onDone(story) when it ends\n- `story.set(key, value = true)`: set a flag (value true) or a value\n- `story.state()`: its vars and quests, as JSON (for a save)\n- `story.stop()`: stop the node running (its box closes; onDone isn't called)\n- `story.test(c)`: does a condition hold? (the manual's conditions)\n- `story.update(dt)`: run the talk and the balloons; true while a node runs (play waits)\n- `story.auto = 0`: seconds a page shows before it turns by itself (0: it waits for the player)\n- `story.game`: its game\n- `story.log = []`: what was said and chosen, oldest first ({ who, name, text }, the last 200: a visual novel's backlog)\n- `story.node = null`: its id\n- `story.nodes`: the conversations by id (lists of steps)\n- `story.on`: the game's event handlers by name\n- `story.people`: who speaks, by id ({ name, portrait, at })\n- `story.quests = []`: the quest log, in the order taken ({ id, title, text, done })\n- `story.running = false`: a node runs\n- `story.skip = false`: pages type at once and turn at once (choices still wait)\n- `story.talk`: the E.Dialog it speaks through\n- `story.vars`: every flag and value (a line's {$name} reads them)\n\n## Module strategy: real-time strategy and simulation: selection, orders, armies, production, resources, placement, fog, AI; a day clock, needs, overlays (engine/modules/strategy.js)\n\n- `new E.Clock(o = {}) → clock` †: new E.Clock({ day: 240 (real seconds of game time a day), hour: 8 (to start at) })\n- `clock.hour` †: the hour (0..23)\n- `clock.label()` †: 'DAY 2 14:30'\n- `clock.light()` †: the light by the hour: { ambient (.12 at night .. 1 at noon), color (the sky's tint: blue night, warm dawn and dusk) }\n- `clock.minute` †: the minute (0..59)\n- `clock.night` †: true from 20:00 to 6:00\n- `clock.update(dt)` †: run it by dt seconds of game time\n- `clock.day = 1` †: the day (1, 2, ...)\n- `clock.length` †: seconds a day\n- `clock.t` †: hours since the first midnight\n- `new E.Strategy(game, o = {}) → strategy` †: new E.Strategy(game, { map, kinds, sides, player, fog, on }): see the manual above\n- `strategy.afford(side, cost)` †: can side pay cost ({ gold: 50 })?\n- `strategy.ai(side, plan)` †: run side by plan ({ build: [[kind, n]], waves: { first, every, min }, script }): see the manual\n- `strategy.canPlace(kind, cx, cy, by = null)` †: can a building of kind stand on cells cx, cy (its corner)? On the map, walkable, no unit there (but by, its builder), seen by the player (when it is theirs)\n- `strategy.cancel(b)` †: take back the last in building's queue (refunded)\n- `strategy.draw(r)` †: after the world: a ring under each selected unit, health bars over the selected and the hurt, the box being drawn\n- `strategy.ghost(r, kind, cx, cy)` †: the placement ghost: kind's cells at cx, cy, green where it fits, red where it doesn't\n- `strategy.input(o = {})` †: the player's pointer and keys, each step (see the manual): selection, groups, orders. o.order: the action that orders (Mouse2 by default where it is bound); o.at(sx, sy) -> the world point under a screen point (map.pick's, by default)\n- `strategy.minimap(colors = {})` †: E.Shell's minimap spec: the map, the fog, the camera's view, the units seen as dots by side, a click moving the camera\n- `strategy.of(side, kind)` †: the side's standing units of a kind (or all of them, without kind)\n- `strategy.order(units, kind, target, o = {})` †: give units an order: kind 'move' | 'attack' | 'amove' | 'gather' | 'build' | 'patrol' | 'stop', target ({ x, y }, a unit, or { kind, cx, cy } to build), o.queue: after the orders they have\n- `strategy.remove(u)` †: take u off the map (it died, a mine ran out)\n- `strategy.side(id)` †: a side by id: { id, res: { gold: 300 }, ... }\n- `strategy.smart(units, x, y, target, o = {})` †: the right click: attack a foe at the point, gather a resource, else move (o.queue as order's); a building that makes units takes the point as its rally\n- `strategy.spawn(kind, side, x, y, o = {})` †: make a unit, building or resource of kind for side at world x, y (o.built: false leaves a building to be built)\n- `strategy.state()` †: what a proof hashes: every unit's kind, side, cell and HP, each side's resources\n- `strategy.train(b, kind)` †: queue kind at building (paid now): it comes out after its time, toward building.rally. False when it can't\n- `strategy.update(dt)` †: run the orders, production, fights, the fog and the AIs by dt seconds\n- `strategy.visible(u)` †: can the player see u? (the fog: any of its cells in sight; theirs always)\n- `strategy.fog` †: an E.Fog of the player's sight (walls block it, buildings don't), or null with fog: false\n- `strategy.game` †: the game it runs in\n- `strategy.groups` †: control groups: groups[1..9], kept with Ctrl and a digit\n- `strategy.kinds` †: the kinds by id (units, buildings, resources: see the manual)\n- `strategy.map` †: the TileMap it stands on (a building blocks its cells)\n- `strategy.on` †: its events: { attack, death, made, built, gathered, ordered }\n- `strategy.player` †: the side the player commands (its fog, selection, input)\n- `strategy.selected = []` †: the player's selection\n- `strategy.time = 0` †: seconds run (rts.update's dt summed)\n- `strategy.units = []` †: everything spawned (units, buildings, resources) that still stands\n- `E.choose(agent, options)` †: the option that best meets an agent's needs ({ gives: { hunger: -.6 }, x, y, cost }): each need it lowers, weighted by that need squared (the urgent first), less the walk (a cell's worth per 400 units) and cost against agent.money; null when none helps\n- `E.needs(agent, rates, dt)` †: raise an agent's needs (agent.needs: { hunger: .2, fun: .5 }, each 0..1) by rates ({ hunger: .01 }) a second\n- `E.overlay(r, map, value, colors = ['#2050c0', '#e0e040', '#e02020'], alpha = .45)` †: tint each cell by value(cx, cy) (0..1, or null for none) along colors (cold to hot): land value, crowding, mood\n- `E.tooltip(r, x, y, lines)` †: a box of lines (text, or [text, color]) beside a screen point (the pointer), kept on the screen\n\n## Module terrain: destructible ground in the side plane: carved and added, walked on, drawn in chunks (engine/modules/terrain.js)\n\n- `new E.Terrain(o = {}) → terrain`: new E.Terrain({ w, h, x, y, materials, chunk }): see the manual\n- `terrain.add(x, y, r, id)`: fill a circle with a material\n- `terrain.addPoly(points, id)`: a material inside a polygon of world points\n- `terrain.carve(x, y, r, o = {})`: a crater: air within r of (x, y) but for hard materials; o.particles: bits of what it took fly out; returns { id: cells taken }\n- `terrain.carvePoly(points)`: air inside a polygon of world points (hard materials stay)\n- `terrain.collider()`: a field for the physics module: world.add(land.collider())\n- `terrain.draw(r)`: draw it as decals under the bodies, in the side view (its y is the engine's z); only changed chunks are redrawn\n- `terrain.fill(fn)`: set every cell from fn(x, y) (the world point at the cell's middle) -> a material id or 0\n- `terrain.get(x, y)`: the material at a world point (0: air, and outside)\n- `terrain.ground(x, y)`: the top of the first solid ground at or below (x, y) (a world y), or null when there is none\n- `terrain.hit(x, y, r)`: a circle in the ground: [nx, ny, depth] (out along the normal by depth clears it), or null\n- `terrain.normal(x, y, r = 4)`: the ground's normal near (x, y): [nx, ny], pointing away from the solid within r\n- `terrain.ray(x0, y0, x1, y1)`: the first solid cell on the line from (x0, y0) to (x1, y1): [x, y] (the last air before it), or null\n- `terrain.set(x, y, id)`: set the cell at a world point\n- `terrain.solid(x, y)`: is the point in the ground?\n- `terrain.walk(b, dt, o = {})`: a walker's step: up and down slopes of up to climb, stopped by walls, falling and landing (see the manual)\n- `terrain.cells`: the mask, row by row from the top (0: air)\n- `terrain.chunk`: the drawn squares' size\n- `terrain.drawn = 0`: how many chunks the last draw painted again\n- `terrain.h`, `terrain.w`: its size in cells (world units)\n- `terrain.materials`: their looks (a material's id is its index + 1)\n- `terrain.x`, `terrain.y`: its bottom left corner\nAI_GUIDE.md\nAPI-INDEX.md\nAPI.md\nREADME.md\ndocs\ngame\nindex.html\nkits\nmodules\nmy-3d2dge-agent.js\nmy-3d2dge.js\npackage.json\nsrc\ntools\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
