{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '220,269p' vendor/engine-upstream/dist/handoff/API-INDEX.md && sed -n '2930,2974p' vendor/my-3d2dge.js && sed -n '9808,9870p' vendor/my-3d2dge.js && sed -n '7740,7795p' vendor/my-3d2dge.js && sed -n '3100,3128p' vendor/my-3d2dge.js\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '220,269p' vendor/engine-upstream/dist/handoff/API-INDEX.md && sed -n '2930,2974p' vendor/my-3d2dge.js && sed -n '9808,9870p' vendor/my-3d2dge.js && sed -n '7740,7795p' vendor/my-3d2dge.js && sed -n '3100,3128p' vendor/my-3d2dge.js\"","aggregated_output":"## 3. Views (projections)\n\n- `E.VIEWS: iso, threequarter, topdown, brawler, side, overhead`: the ready-made views: iso (yaw 45, pitch 30), threequarter (pitch 55), topdown (80), brawler (25), side (0) and overhead (90, scale 1: world x/y = screen pixels); new E.Game({ view: 'iso' }) and game.setView('side') take their names\n- `E.VIEW_ORDER: iso, threequarter, topdown, brawler, side`: the order game.nextView() cycles through when a game doesn't set game.views ('overhead' is left out)\n- `new E.View(id, label, yaw, pitch, scale = 1, zBoost = 1) → view`: new E.View(id, label, yaw, pitch, scale, zBoost): an orthographic camera (degrees; scale = screen pixels per world unit; zBoost stretches heights)\n- `view.depth(x, y, z = 0)`: true camera depth: use it to sort parts inside one object\n- `view.order(x, y, z = 0)`: painter's order for whole objects standing on the ground\n- `view.p(x, y, z = 0)`: world -> projected screen pixels (not yet camera-relative)\n- `view.screenDirToGround(dx, dy)`: a direction the player pushed on screen (x right, y down) -> normalized ground direction\n- `view.set(yaw, pitch, scale = 1, zBoost = 1)`: change the angles and scale in place (game.setView and rotateView use their own copies); returns the view\n- `view.toGround(sx, sy)`: projected screen point -> ground point (z = 0). null in side view\n- `view.ax`, `view.ay`: screen x = ax * x + ay * y (the projection's first row)\n- `view.bx`, `view.by`, `view.bz`: screen y = bx * x + by * y + bz * z (the second row; bz < 0 lifts things up the screen)\n- `view.dx`, `view.dy`, `view.dz`: the direction toward the camera, for true depth (view.depth)\n- `view.fx`, `view.fy`: the ground direction that points at the camera (the front of a wall faces it)\n- `view.id`: its name ('iso', or yours)\n- `view.inv`: the inverse of the ground projection (screen -> ground at z 0), null in side views\n- `view.isSide`: a side view (pitch 1 or less: x right, z up, y is depth)\n- `view.isTop`: looking straight down (pitch 89 or more)\n- `view.label`: its name for people ('Isometric')\n- `view.pitch`, `view.yaw`: the same angles in radians\n- `view.pitchDeg`: the tilt, 0 side view to 90 straight down\n- `view.scale`: screen pixels per world unit\n- `view.yawDeg`: the turn about the up axis, degrees (0: north is up the screen; iso is 45)\n- `view.zBoost`: how much heights are stretched on screen (1 = true)\n- `E.charView(view)`: the view a rig in `view` is drawn from (the sprite-like angle above; the view itself outside pitch 40-89)\n\n## 4. Screen\n\n- `E.RES: nes, snes, gb, gbc, gba, genesis, md, pce, n64, ps1, wide`: classic console resolutions for new Game({ res: 'snes' }) (width x height in pixels)\n- `game.screen.clear` †: true when the game's bg is null (or 'transparent'): the buffer and the canvas are cleared instead of filled, so a picture under the canvas shows through the game's (a 3D world drawn by engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js)\n- `game.screen.clientToScreen(cx, cy)`: browser client coords -> projected screen pixels (same space as view.p)\n- `game.screen.resize()`: fit the buffer to the canvas again (on window resize; after changing sizing options, setOptions does it)\n- `game.screen.setOptions(o)`: change sizing at runtime, e.g. { minH: 400, maxW: 1080, maxH: 660 } to pull the camera back\n- `game.screen.setScale(v)`: how a fixed resolution fills the screen: 'sharp' | 'fill' | 'auto' (see scale)\n- `game.screen.H = 200`, `game.screen.W = 320`: the buffer's size in game pixels\n- `game.screen.OX = 0`, `game.screen.OY = 0`: the letterbox margins on the canvas, in canvas pixels\n- `game.screen.S = 3`: the whole-number scale up to the canvas\n- `game.screen.buf`: the low-resolution buffer the game draws into (W + 2 by H + 2 pixels; null when headless, a stand-in when a headless game draws: o.draw)\n- `game.screen.canvas`: the page's canvas (null in a headless game); o: the sizing options (the game's o)\n- `game.screen.ctx`: its 2D context (r.ctx is the same)\n- `game.screen.dpr = 1`: the device pixel ratio used (at most 3)\n- `game.screen.filter = 'clean'` †: 'clean' | 'scanlines' | 'crt', drawn over the picture as it is presented, never on the buffer, and not under a test harness (navigator.webdriver). game.settings.filter sets it\n- `game.screen.fx = 0`, `game.screen.fy = 0`: its sub-pixel part (0..1, applied when presenting)\n- `game.screen.ix = 0`, `game.screen.iy = 0`: the camera's whole-pixel offset this frame\n- `game.screen.listeners = []` †: functions called after every resize\n- `game.screen.portrait`: true while the screen is taller than wide (a phone held upright)\n- `game.screen.scale = 'auto'`: how a fixed resolution fills the screen: 'sharp' (whole-number scale, every pixel the same size, black bars unless o.expand grows the picture into them), 'fill' (the largest fit, pixels a device pixel apart in size) or 'auto' (fill on high-density screens, phones and tablets, where that difference can't be seen; sharp elsewhere). game.settings.scale sets it\n\n## 5. Input\n    if (this.onError) try { this.onError(e, where); } catch (e2) { /* ignore */ }\n  }\n  _step(dt) {\n    if (this._next) { const nx = this._next; this._next = null; this._enter(nx.name, nx.data); }\n    this.real += dt; this._shakeAmt *= Math.exp(-dt * 9); this.input.tick(dt); if (this._players) for (let i = 1; i < this._players.length; i++) this._players[i].tick(dt);   // (the other players: section 10j)\n    this.audio._tick(false);   // (the music clock: a song on game.real plays on through a pause)\n    for (const f of HOOKS.input) f(this, dt);\n    this._ownRun('input', dt);\n    if (this.input.padLost) { this._autoPause('controller'); if (this.shell) this.shell.toast('Controller disconnected', 'Press a button on a controller to use it, or play on.'); else this.note('CONTROLLER DISCONNECTED', 2.5); }\n    if (this.shell) this.shell._step();   // its screens move the focus with this step's input, and hold it back from the game\n    if (this._flash) this._flash.t += dt / Math.max(.05, this.timeScale);   // real time: slow motion does not stretch a flash\n    if (this._fade) { const out = this._fade.out; this._fadeStep(dt); if (out) return; }   // the old scene freezes while it fades out\n    const s = this.scene;\n    if (s && s.pausable && this.input.pressed('pause')) { this.paused = !this.paused; this.pauseReason = this.paused ? 'player' : null; this.audio.sfx('pause'); }\n    if (!this.paused) { this.pauseReason = null; this._goOn = false; }\n    this.audio._duck(this.paused);\n    // the player's zoom: the wheel, - and = and a pinch ask for it while a scene that allows it plays (input.zoom())\n    const I = this.input, zr = !this.paused && !(this.shell && this.shell.screen) && !!this.zoomRange();\n    I._zoomOn = zr; I._wheelOn = zr && (this._camera() || {}).wheel !== false;\n    if (this._zoomTo && !I._pinching && this._wall - this._zoomAt > .25) this._zoomApply();   // they stopped: the new zoom, drawn crisp\n    if (this.paused && !this.live && !this._goOn) return;   // (live: the world steps on under the menu)\n    if (!this.paused) { this._zoomFit(); const zk = I.zoom(); if (zk !== 1) this.zoomBy(zk); }\n    if (this.hitstop > 0) { this.hitstop -= dt; return; }\n    this.time += dt;\n    this.audio._tick(true);   // (a synced song: on game.time, it waits with the game)\n    const T = this._timers;\n    if (T.length) {\n      for (let i = 0; i < T.length; i++) { const t = T[i]; if (t.dead || this.time < t.at) continue; if (t.every) t.at += t.every; else t.dead = true; t.fn(); }\n      prune(T, t => t.dead);\n    }\n    for (const f of HOOKS.step) f(this, dt);\n    this._ownRun('step', dt);\n    if (s && s.update) s.update(dt);\n    const f = this._follow;\n    if (f && f.target) {\n      const t = f.target, sp = Math.hypot(t.vx || 0, t.vy || 0), dir = typeof t.facing === 'number' && Math.abs(t.facing) === 1 && !t.vy ? [t.facing, 0] : sp > 1 ? [(t.vx || 0) / sp, (t.vy || 0) / sp] : [0, 0];\n      this.focus(t.x + dir[0] * f.lead, (t.y || 0) + dir[1] * f.lead, (t.z || 0) + f.z);\n    }\n    this.particles.update(dt);\n    if (this.cam.free) this._freeCam(dt);\n    this._camStep(dt);\n  }\n  // a screen-pixel move [dx, dy] as the ground's (world x, y) at the zoom the player sees (during a zoom's preview, the goal)\n  _screenToGround(dx, dy) {\n    const v = this.view, k = this.zoom / (this._zoomTo || this.zoom);\n    if (pause && g.paused && !this._stack.length) this.show('pause');\n    if (!g.paused && this._stack.length && (this._ownPause || (pause && this._stack.some(t => t.name === 'pause')))) { this._stack.length = 0; this._ownPause = false; this._capEnd(); this._render(); }   // resumed from code\n    if (this.root) {\n      if (this._toastEnd && now > this._toastEnd) { this._els.toast.classList.remove('on'); this._toastEnd = 0; }\n      if (this._tutEnd && now > this._tutEnd) this.tutorial(null);\n      this.root.classList.toggle('esh-still', !!g.reduceMotion);\n    }\n    if (!this._stack.length) return;\n    const keep = this._held; this._held = new Set();\n    const has = a => !!I.map[a], confirm = has('confirm') && I.pressed('confirm'), cancel = has('cancel') && I.pressed('cancel'), closeAll = has('pause') && I.pressed('pause'), mv = I.move();\n    const hit = ['up', 'down', 'left', 'right'].find(a => has(a) && I.pressed(a)), dir = Math.abs(mv[1]) > .5 ? (mv[1] < 0 ? 'up' : 'down') : Math.abs(mv[0]) > .5 ? (mv[0] < 0 ? 'left' : 'right') : null;\n    for (const a in I.map) if (!keep.has(a)) I.suppress(a);   // the game doesn't see what the menu used\n    if (this._cap) { this._capPad(); return; }   // binding: the next button is the answer, not a move in the menu\n    if (!this.root) { if (cancel) this.back(); else if (closeAll) this.hide(); return; }\n    if (hit) { this._dir = hit; this._dirT = now + .35; this._nav(hit); }   // a press (however short), then the held direction repeats\n    else if (dir !== this._dir) { this._dir = dir; this._dirT = now + .35; if (dir) this._nav(dir); }\n    else if (dir && now > this._dirT) { this._dirT = now + .09; this._nav(dir); }\n    const f = document.activeElement, mine = f && this._els.scr.contains(f);\n    if (confirm && mine && f.tagName === 'BUTTON') f.click();\n    else if (cancel) this.back();\n    else if (closeAll) this.hide();   // Start (or P) closes the whole menu; Escape and B go back one screen\n  }\n  /** binding a controller button: the first one pressed since binding began (Start cancels) */\n  _capPad() {\n    const c = this._cap, I = this._ctlI(); if (!c || c.device !== 'pad') return;\n    const i = I.padPrev.findIndex((on, k) => on && !c.prev[k]); c.prev = I.padPrev.slice();\n    const ra = RSTICK.find(k => I.held.has(k) && !c.prevR.includes(k)); c.prevR = RSTICK.filter(k => I.held.has(k));   // (the right stick pushed: its direction)\n    if (i === 9) this._capEnd('Not changed.'); else if (i >= 0) this._capBind('Pad' + i); else if (ra) this._capBind(ra);\n  }\n  /** move the focus (or turn a focused slider or choice), keeping it in view */\n  _nav(dir) {\n    const all = this._focusables(); if (!all.length) return;\n    const f = document.activeElement, i = all.indexOf(f), side = dir === 'left' || dir === 'right', d = dir === 'up' || dir === 'left' ? -1 : 1;\n    if (f && side && f.type === 'range') { if (d < 0) f.stepDown(); else f.stepUp(); f.dispatchEvent(new Event('input')); return; }\n    if (f && side && f._cycle) { f._cycle(d); return; }\n    const n = all[((i < 0 ? 0 : i + d) + all.length) % all.length]; n.focus({ preventScroll: true }); if (n.scrollIntoView) n.scrollIntoView({ block: 'nearest' });\n  }\n}\nE.Shell = Shell;\n\n/* =============================================================================\n * 22. GPU LIGHTING (WebGPU)  optional. game.enableGPU({ map }) turns it on.\n *    The Canvas renderer still draws every color. Alongside it, it writes an\n *    \"info\" image (height, surface type, glow per pixel). The GPU then lights each\n *    pixel: colored point lights, soft shadows marched through a heightmap of the\n *    level, light wrapping around characters (normals from their silhouettes),\n *    glow spilling onto nearby surfaces, dithered light bands, and heat shimmer.\n *    If WebGPU is missing or fails, the game keeps the Canvas lighting.\n * ============================================================================= */\nconst WGSL_COMMON = /* wgsl */`\nstruct Params {\n  a: vec4f, b: vec4f, c: vec4f, camv: vec4f,\n  right: vec4f, up: vec4f, amb: vec4f, fx: vec4f,\n  mapd: vec4f, scr: vec4f, scr2: vec4f, bufd: vec4f,\n  nheat: vec4f, p0: vec4f, p1: vec4f, p2: vec4f,\n  lights: array<vec4f, 64>,\n  heat: array<vec4f, 8>,\n};\n@group(0) @binding(0) var<uniform> U: Params;\n@vertex fn vs(@builtin(vertex_index) i: u32) -> @builtin(position) vec4f {\n  var p = array<vec2f, 3>(vec2f(-1.0, -1.0), vec2f(3.0, -1.0), vec2f(-1.0, 3.0));\n  return vec4f(p[i], 0.0, 1.0);\n}\n  constructor(game, signal) {\n    /** game: the game it plays for; ctx: the Web Audio context, made at the first sound (null before, or where there\n     *  is none); volume: master volume 0..1; sfxVolume, musicVolume, voiceVolume: 0..1 for effects, music and the voice\n     *  bus (bus('voice')); muted: true while mute() is on */\n    this._game = game; this._signal = signal; this.ctx = null; this.volume = .7; this.sfxVolume = 1; this.musicVolume = .5; this.voiceVolume = 1; this.muted = false;\n    /** presets: the named sound effects, sfx(name) plays one and define(name, voice) adds one; song: the music\n     *  playing (null for none); failed: why there is no sound (a message), or null; ducked: true while duck(true)\n     *  holds the music down */\n    this.presets = Object.assign({}, SFX); this._last = {}; this.song = null; this.failed = null; this._ducked = false; this.ducked = false; this._waves = {};\n    /** offset: seconds the music sounds early, for speakers and headphones that lag (settings.audioOffset); drift: what\n     *  is heard against the game's clock at the last frame (seconds: the sound follows the clock, so it stays near 0);\n     *  anchors: how many times the sound was moved to meet the clock */\n    this.offset = 0; this.drift = 0; this.anchors = 0; this._ats = []; this._beats = []; this._fading = []; this._stings = []; this._fr = null; this._timer = null;\n    // sound draws its own random numbers (noise, pitch variation): whether sound is on, muted or not yet unlocked by a tap\n    // never shifts the game's Math.random, so the same seed and inputs play the same game with or without it\n    this._rnd = rng(0x51A7E);\n  }\n  _init() {\n    if (this.ctx || this.failed) return this.ctx;\n    try {\n      const AC = window.AudioContext || window.webkitAudioContext; if (!AC) throw new Error('Web Audio is not available');\n      const c = this.ctx = new AC();\n      this._master = c.createGain(); this._master.connect(c.destination);\n      this._sfxBus = c.createGain(); this._sfxBus.connect(this._master);\n      this._musBus = c.createGain(); this._musBus.connect(this._master);\n      this._voiceBus = c.createGain(); this._voiceBus.connect(this._master);   // (bus('voice'): a game's babble or speech)\n      this.applyVolume();\n      const nb = c.createBuffer(1, c.sampleRate, c.sampleRate), d = nb.getChannelData(0); for (let i = 0; i < d.length; i++) d[i] = this._rnd() * 2 - 1;\n      this._noise = nb;\n      const resume = () => { if (c.state !== 'running') c.resume().catch(() => {}); };\n      for (const ev of ['pointerdown', 'keydown', 'touchstart']) addEventListener(ev, resume, { capture: true, signal: this._signal });\n      resume();\n    } catch (e) { this.failed = e.message || String(e); warn('audio', 'sound is off: ' + this.failed); }\n    return this.ctx;\n  }\n  /** true once the browser lets the game make sound */\n  get ready() { return !!this.ctx && this.ctx.state === 'running'; }\n  /** apply volume, musicVolume, sfxVolume and mute after setting them directly (setVolume and mute call it) */\n  applyVolume() { if (!this.ctx) return; this._master.gain.value = this.muted ? 0 : this.volume; this._sfxBus.gain.value = this.sfxVolume; this._musBus.gain.value = this.musicVolume * (this._ducked || this.ducked ? .35 : 1); this._voiceBus.gain.value = this.voiceVolume; }\n  /** a bus for a sound of the game's own (Web Audio nodes it makes on audio.ctx: a babble voice, a loop, an instrument)\n   *  to connect to, so it follows the volume, the mute and the player's settings: 'sfx', 'music', 'voice' (its level is\n   *  voiceVolume) or 'master'. Returns a GainNode, or null where there is no sound (headless, no Web Audio) */\n  bus(name = 'sfx') {\n    if (!['sfx', 'music', 'voice', 'master'].includes(name)) warn('bus:' + name, 'audio.bus(\"' + name + '\"): no such bus (sfx, music, voice, master): the sfx bus');\n    if (this._game.headless || !this._init()) return null;\n    return name === 'master' ? this._master : name === 'music' ? this._musBus : name === 'voice' ? this._voiceBus : this._sfxBus;\n  }\n  /** set the master volume, 0..1 */\n  setVolume(v) { this.volume = clamp(v, 0, 1); this.applyVolume(); }\n  /** toggle (or set) mute; returns the new state */\n  mute(on = !this.muted) { this.muted = on; this.applyVolume(); return on; }\n  /** the page is hidden (sleep true) or back: the sound sleeps with it unless the player keeps it on (settings.background) */\n  _background(sleep) { const c = this.ctx; if (!c) return; try { if (sleep && c.state === 'running') { this._slept = true; c.suspend().catch(() => {}); } else if (!sleep && this._slept) { this._slept = false; c.resume().catch(() => {}); } } catch (e) { /* closed */ } }\n  /** quiet the music (to 35%) while on: under a game's own pause, map or dialog screen. The engine also ducks it while game.paused */\n  duck(on = true) { on = !!on; if (this.ducked === on) return; this.ducked = on; this.applyVolume(); }\n  _duck(on) { if (this._ducked === on) return; this._ducked = on; this.applyVolume(); }   // (the engine's own: game.paused)\n    const raw = store.get(this._key, null);\n    hide('_raw', raw && typeof raw === 'object' && raw.values && typeof raw.values === 'object' ? raw.values : {});   // (a newer version's values are checked like the rest)\n    if (!(raw && raw.v >= 2) && this._raw.fullscreen === false) delete this._raw.fullscreen;\n    game.input._settings = this; game.input._onBindings = b => this.set('bindings', b);\n    for (const k in STANDARD_SETTINGS) if (!(game.o.touch === 'never' && STANDARD_SETTINGS[k].group === 'Touch')) this.define(Object.assign({ key: k }, STANDARD_SETTINGS[k]));   // (touch: 'never': no Touch group)\n    for (const sp of own) this.define(sp && !sp.label && Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(STANDARD_SETTINGS, sp.key) ? Object.assign({ key: sp.key }, STANDARD_SETTINGS[sp.key], sp) : sp);   // (a standard key with no label: the game's fields over the standard ones; with a label it is the game's own)\n  }\n  /** add a setting: { key, label, type: 'toggle' | 'slider' | 'select', value, min, max, step, options: [[value,\n   *  label]] or [value], help, group: 'Game', apply(game, value) }. Its saved value is loaded (when it is a valid one) and\n   *  applied; it shows on the settings screen. Returns its value */\n  define(sp) {\n    if (!sp || !sp.key || typeof sp.key !== 'string') { warn('setting', 'a setting needs a key: { key: \"hints\", label: \"Hints\", value: true }'); return undefined; }\n    if (sp.key in Settings.prototype || sp.key === 'saved' || sp.key === 'onChange' || sp.key[0] === '_') { warn('setting:' + sp.key, 'a setting cannot be called \"' + sp.key + '\" (a name the settings object uses)'); return undefined; }\n    const s = Object.assign({ group: 'Game', type: sp.options ? 'select' : typeof sp.value === 'number' ? 'slider' : typeof sp.value === 'boolean' || sp.value === undefined ? 'toggle' : 'value' }, sp);\n    this._specs[s.key] = s;\n    const saved = settingValue(s, this._raw[s.key]);\n    if (saved !== undefined) this._saved[s.key] = saved;\n    this[s.key] = saved !== undefined ? saved : this.default(s.key);\n    this._apply(s.key);\n    return this[s.key];\n  }\n  /** a setting's default value */\n  default(key) { const s = this._specs[key]; if (!s) return undefined; const v = typeof s.value === 'function' ? s.value(this._g) : s.value; return v === undefined && s.type === 'toggle' ? false : v; }\n  /** the spec a setting was defined with (its label, type, range, options), or undefined */\n  spec(key) { return this._specs[key]; }\n  /** the keys of a group's settings, in order ('Display', 'Sound', 'Comfort', 'Controller', 'Touch', 'Game'), or every key */\n  keys(group) { return Object.keys(this._specs).filter(k => !group || this._specs[k].group === group); }\n  /** change a setting: checked (a slider kept in range, a choice among its options), saved, applied, onChange told.\n   *  Returns the value kept (the old one when v is not a valid value) */\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
